IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `family-domain`

§ `Intestate Succession Act 1967`

-- ===========================================================================
-- INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT 1967 (ISA), 2020 Revised Edition.
--
-- The source text's own provenance line: "This revised edition incorporates
-- all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation
-- on 31 December 2021."
--
-- Long title: "An Act to make provision for the distribution of intestate estates."
-- Commencement: "[2 June 1967]".
--
-- SCOPE. Sections 1 to 10 — the whole Act. What is NOT encoded is named,
-- section by section, in the closing section `What is deliberately NOT
-- encoded, and why`; nothing has been dropped silently.
--
-- SOURCE. Every quoted string in this file was copied mechanically out of
-- source/ISA1967.txt in this directory, which is a `pdftotext -layout`
-- extraction of the official SSO PDF. Running headers (the revised-edition
-- line and the Act's name), page numbers and the repeated date stamp are not
-- statutory text and are discarded; line-wrapping is normalised to single
-- spaces. (Unlike the extraction of the Probate and Administration Act 1934
-- in the same directory, ISA1967.txt carries no "Informal Consolidation"
-- watermark line at all: the phrase does not occur in it.) Curly quotation
-- marks and apostrophes are the source's own and are reproduced. No quotation
-- here was reconstructed from memory.
--
-- HOUSE STYLE. Inert: nothing is @export'ed. Citations ride on @ref, with the
-- provision's verbatim words beside the pinpoint, so that a reader holding the
-- Act can find each rule by its own heading. The ontology is
-- `family-domain.l4`, and this module declares NO type of its own: every noun
-- the Act needs was already there, including the one that looked as though it
-- would need a new record — s 9's claim by "a child or any descendant of a
-- child", which turned out to be computable by walking the `children` edge
-- rather than a fact to be asked for.
--
-- WHAT SHAPE THE ACT IS. Almost entirely CONSTITUTIVE: it says who is
-- entitled to what fraction, never who must do what by when. There is no
-- deadline anywhere in the Act — no period, no word of deadline, and no date
-- at all except the s 5 commencement threshold "after 2 June 1967" (the years
-- in Act titles aside) — so there is no regulative rule in this file and no
-- `PARTY ... MUST ... WITHIN`. Three provisions are
-- worded as duties, and each one's duty-holder and timing come from the
-- Probate and Administration Act 1934, not from here: s 5's "shall be
-- distributed", s 7's "the following rules shall be observed", and s 10's
-- proviso "the personal representative shall ... be a trustee". The last of
-- those is a STATUS rather than a timed obligation and IS encoded, as a
-- boolean, below.
--
-- ARITHMETIC. Every share is a fraction — `Share` in the ontology — and never
-- a dollar figure, because the Act never converts: it stops at "one-half of
-- the estate" (rules 2, 4), "equal portions" (rules 5, 6, 7, 8), "by equal
-- portions per stirpes" (rule 3) and "the whole of the estate" (rules 1, 7,
-- 8, 9). L4 NUMBER arithmetic is exact rational, so a per stirpes third loses
-- nothing. THE WHOLE THAT EVERY FRACTION IS A FRACTION OF is computed once,
-- by `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction`, and it is
-- s 5's fund "after payment thereout of the expenses of due administration as
-- prescribed by the Probate and Administration Act 1934", reduced under s 10
-- by any part beneficially disposed of by will.
--
-- AMBIGUITY REGISTER. Twenty-one interpretive choices are marked `-- AMBIGUITY
-- A1` to `A21` at the site where each bites, with both readings, the reading
-- taken and why. They are tabulated again in the closing section. Five of the
-- twenty-one (A17 to A21) were added by adversarial review, which found each
-- of them being decided in the code and recorded nowhere.
--
-- TESTS, NOT DEMONSTRATIONS. Every stated expectation in the worked cases is
-- an `#ASSERT`. An earlier version of this file carried seventy-six `#EVAL`s
-- and no assertion at all, so `l4 run` printed answers that nobody checked and
-- a regression in any rule was exactly as green as correctness.
--
-- ONE CORPUS, FOUR ACTS, AND THE FOURTEEN NAMES THAT HAD TO MOVE. This module
-- is meant to be read and imported beside `wills-act.l4`,
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` and `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4`. Until
-- 2026-08-26 it could not be. L4 raises a hard error at the first USE of a name
-- that two imported modules both define, and the four modules had built the
-- same fixtures independently. Measured over the top-level definitions of the
-- four files: this one shared TWELVE names with `wills-act.l4` (`a deceased
-- person`, `a legitimate child`, `a person`, and nine invented people who
-- happened to be given the same nine labels for nine different lives), FOUR
-- with `probate-administration-act.l4` (`a deceased person`, `a family`,
-- `a legitimate child`, `an estate`) and ONE with
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` (`a person`) — fourteen distinct names.
--
-- ALL FOURTEEN NOW CARRY THIS ACT'S TAG, placed immediately after the article:
-- `an ISA deceased person`, `an ISA family`, `an ISA estate`, `an ISA person`,
-- `an ISA legitimate child`, and `the ISA Bala` through `the ISA Zainab`. That
-- is the whole convention, and it is applied to the names that collided and to
-- every fixture name added since. `Aaron` and `Beatrice` are left alone on
-- purpose: the tag records a fact — that this label is shared vocabulary in
-- this corpus — and putting it on a name nobody else uses would say something
-- untrue while adding a line for a reviewer to check.
--
-- NO STATUTORY DECISION WAS RENAMED, and that is the point of the exercise
-- rather than an exception to it. `section 6(b) — those of the whole blood, the
-- half blood ranking immediately after them` and its kind are the deliverable:
-- a reader holding the Act finds the rule by the Act's own words. A tag on
-- those names would take that away to solve a problem the fixtures caused.
--
-- THE RENAME CHANGED NO ANSWER, AND THE PROOF IS ARITHMETIC. It was done first
-- and on its own, before anything else in this revision: 362 assertions before
-- it and 362 after it, every one satisfied, and mapping the fourteen names back
-- out of the renamed file returned it byte-for-byte to the original except for
-- the leading whitespace of 46 continuation lines, which had been aligned under
-- a name that grew. Nothing printed changed either, because a fixture's
-- IDENTIFIER and the `name` string it carries are different things:
-- `the ISA Rahim` still prints "Rahim".
--
-- WHAT CAME AFTER THE RENAME, and it is why the file now reports 381 rather
-- than 362: `Joins with the other three Acts` and its two decisions, and cases
-- 40, 40a and 41, which assert them. Nineteen assertions, none of them in a rule
-- of this Act.
--
-- JOINS. What this Act hands to the other three and what they hand back is
-- tabulated, seam by seam, in `Joins with the other three Acts` below — which
-- also holds the two decisions this module offers to the other modules and no
-- provision of this Act asks for.
-- ===========================================================================


§§ `Section 1 — Short title`

-- @ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 1 — "This Act is the Intestate Succession Act 1967."
-- NOT ENCODED, and there is nothing to encode: a short-title provision has no
-- antecedent and no consequent. It is named here so that a reader walking the
-- Act section by section does not find a gap at section 1.


§§ `Section 2 — Application`

-- The hardest gate in the Act, and the one that changes the answer entirely
-- rather than at the margin. It is drafted as a NEGATIVE — "Nothing in this
-- Act shall apply" — so it is encoded as a negative, with the exclusion as the
-- named decision and the application derived from it. Writing it the other way
-- round ("this Act applies if the deceased is not a Muslim") would put the
-- burden on the wrong side of the sentence.
--
-- THE SECOND LIMB IS NOT ENCODED. s 2 has two: the estate of a Muslim is
-- outside this Act, AND nothing in this Act "shall affect any rules of the
-- Muslim law in respect of the distribution of the estate of any such person".
-- The second limb is a saving of a body of law this module does not contain
-- and cannot compute; it is named in the closing section. NOTE ALSO WHAT THE
-- SECTION DOES NOT SAY: the text of s 2 in the source names no other statute
-- at all — no Administration of Muslim Law Act, no forum, no rule of
-- distribution. It says only that the Muslim law is unaffected. Any encoding
-- that routed the estate to a named Act would be adding words to the section.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A1 — "any Muslim". The Act supplies no test of who is a Muslim
-- and no date at which the question is asked. Readings: (i) a fact about the
-- deceased at the date of his death, arriving already construed; (ii) a
-- question this Act itself decides. Taken: (i) — the ontology's
-- `a Muslim` on `Deceased person` is an already-construed input, and a section
-- that supplies no test cannot be read as creating one.

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 2 — "Nothing in this Act shall apply to the estate of any Muslim or shall affect any rules of the Muslim law in respect of the distribution of the estate of any such person."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 2 — nothing in this Act applies to the estate` IF
    `the deceased person`'s `a Muslim`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 2
-- The converse of the section, and the gate every operative rule below reads.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `this Act applies to the estate` IF
    NOT `section 2 — nothing in this Act applies to the estate` `the deceased person`


§§ `Section 3 — Interpretation`

-- s 3 opens "In this Act —" and defines three terms. Each gets its own
-- decision, named for the term.

-- "child" is defined RELATIONALLY — legitimate to whom, adopted by whom — so
-- it is a predicate over the ontology's `Child` record (the edge), not over a
-- `Person`. The two limbs are a DISJUNCTION: an adopted child answers the
-- definition whether or not the first limb is made out, because the section
-- has "includes" as its second verb, not a bare "and".
--
-- AMBIGUITY A2 — the adoption limb's jurisdictions. "any written law for the
-- time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam" is a closed
-- list of three, so an adoption order made anywhere else does not answer the
-- definition. Readings: (i) the list is exhaustive; (ii) it is illustrative of
-- adoption orders generally. Taken: (i) — the ontology's boolean carries the
-- whole phrase including the three jurisdictions, and a drafter who meant an
-- adoption order made anywhere would not have named three countries.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 3 — "“child” means a legitimate child and includes any child adopted by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam;"
GIVEN `the relationship of child to parent` IS A Child
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` IF
        "means a legitimate child"
              ... `the relationship of child to parent`'s `a legitimate child of the person whose child he is`
    OR  "and includes any child adopted by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam"
              ... `the relationship of child to parent`'s `adopted by that person by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam`

-- ***** A JOIN TO THE OTHER TWO ACTS THAT ASK ABOUT CHILDREN, AND THE SEAM A
-- ***** CROSS-MODULE AUDIT FOUND BROKEN. It is SEAM 1 in `Joins with the other
-- ***** three Acts` below.
--
-- s 3 opens "In this Act —". So the definition above is the INTESTATE
-- SUCCESSION ACT's "child" and nobody else's. That is not a nicety: `Child` in
-- `family-domain.l4` carries s 3's two limbs and is the record every module
-- walks, so a module that reads those limbs without authority has silently
-- applied this Act to another — and a module that borrows this Act's classes
-- and then does NOT read them has silently applied another Act to this one.
-- Both errors are available, and the corpus has had one of them.
--
-- `wills-act.l4` DECLINES THE DEFINITION, AND IS RIGHT TO. WA s 26 speaks of
-- "any person being a child or other issue of the testator" and defines neither
-- word, so it stands on its own words. That module reads it as descent down the
-- `children` edge to any depth — `the descendants bearing the reference`
-- compares `the reference for this person` and consults neither limb of `Child`
-- — and says why in terms. Its words: because ISA s 3 opens "In this Act" and
-- defines "child" for the Intestate Succession Act alone. Agreed, and recorded
-- from this side too so that the pointer runs in both directions.
--
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` TAKES THE DEFINITION, AND IS ALSO RIGHT TO,
-- BECAUSE IT HAS BORROWED THE CLASSES. PAA s 18(4)(a) opens letters of
-- administration "to the husband or widow or next of kin or any of them" and
-- never defines "next of kin"; that module's AMBIGUITY A3 fills the gap by
-- pointing at "the classes to whom the Intestate Succession Act 1967
-- distributes on an intestacy — issue, parents, brothers and sisters and their
-- issue, grandparents, uncles and aunts — taken as a flat class". Having
-- pointed, it takes what is there: its AMBIGUITY A12 rules that the definition
-- travels with the classes and applies it at every generation. The two modules
-- are therefore NOT in conflict, and the difference between them is the
-- difference between a phrase that stands on its own words (WA s 26) and a
-- phrase construed by reference to another Act (PAA "next of kin").
--
-- THIS SEAM WAS BROKEN AND HAS BEEN REPAIRED ON THE OTHER SIDE. A cross-module
-- audit measured the Probate and Administration Act module opening a grant to,
-- and counting under s 18(4)(a), a man this Act pays nothing — its A3 said it
-- was borrowing these classes while its walk read the raw `children` edge. That
-- has since been fixed there, and the fix is recorded here because the next
-- reader of this file should not have to re-derive it.
--
-- RE-MEASURED 2026-08-26, with both modules imported into one file (possible
-- only since the rename recorded in the module header), on ONE shared
-- `Deceased person` — `case 18 — a legitimate child, an adopted child and
-- neither` below, whose Kiran is neither legitimate nor adopted:
--   the PAA's `the classes from which the next of kin are drawn`  =  LIST "Ivan", "Jasmine"
--   the PAA's `the next of kin of the deceased`                   =  LIST "Ivan", "Jasmine"
--   the PAA's `s 18(4)(a) — one of the husband or widow or next of kin` of "Kiran"    =  FALSE
--   the PAA's same decision of "Jasmine" (adopted, so within s 3)  =  TRUE
--   this Act's `the distribution, read out`                       =  LIST "Ivan takes 0.5", "Jasmine takes 0.5"
-- The two modules now answer alike, on both limbs of the definition.
--
-- THE REMAINING RISK IS DRIFT, AND IT IS REAL. The other module states this
-- definition a second time, as `a “child” within the meaning of section 3 of
-- the Intestate Succession Act 1967`, rather than importing the decision above
-- — deliberately, since an Act module in this cleanroom imports the shared
-- ontology and nothing else, and grant machinery that needed the distribution
-- rules to typecheck would be the wrong dependency. So one definition is
-- written twice, in two files, with nothing making them agree. Measured on all
-- three children of case 18 the two decisions return the same three answers
-- (TRUE, TRUE, FALSE). If s 3 is ever amended, both sites change or the corpus
-- is wrong; that is reported as a domain gap, since the fix belongs in
-- `family-domain.l4` and this module is not the place to make it.

-- "intestate" is an INCLUSIVE definition — it widens the ordinary meaning to
-- cover the partly-testate. So the decision has two limbs: the person who left
-- no will at all (the ordinary meaning, which s 3 does not displace), and
-- s 3's own extension.
--
-- "dies intestate as to some beneficial interest in his property" is decided
-- from the will's dispositions, per `family-domain.l4`'s ruling on
-- `Disposition`: a universal or residuary beneficial gift disposes of the
-- whole, so anything short of one leaves some beneficial interest undisposed.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 3 — "“intestate” includes any person who leaves a will but dies intestate as to some beneficial interest in his property;"
GIVEN `the will` IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the will beneficially disposes of the whole of his property` IF
    any `a universal or residuary beneficial gift` (`the will`'s `the dispositions made by the will`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 3 ("intestate"), s 10; Probate and Administration Act 1934 Second Schedule paras 1-2
GIVEN `the disposition` IS A Disposition
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a universal or residuary beneficial gift` IF
        `the disposition`'s `beneficial`
    AND CONSIDER `the disposition`'s `the kind of disposition`
        WHEN `a universal gift`                             THEN TRUE
        WHEN `a residuary gift`                             THEN TRUE
        WHEN `a specific devise or bequest`                 THEN FALSE
        WHEN `a pecuniary legacy`                           THEN FALSE
        WHEN `an appointment by will under a power`         THEN FALSE
        WHEN `a charge or direction for the payment of a debt` THEN FALSE

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 3 — "“intestate” includes any person who leaves a will but dies intestate as to some beneficial interest in his property;"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` IF
    CONSIDER `the deceased person`'s `the will`
    WHEN NOTHING       THEN TRUE
    WHEN JUST `a will` THEN NOT `the will beneficially disposes of the whole of his property` `a will`

-- "issue" is likewise INCLUSIVE — "includes children and the descendants of
-- deceased children" — so it does not shut out a descendant the ordinary
-- meaning would catch. It is not given a decision of its own here, and the
-- reason is worth stating rather than leaving as silence: the Act never asks
-- for the SET of issue, only whether an intestate "leaves issue" (rules 1, 2,
-- 3) and, under rule 3, which of them take and in what stocks. Both questions
-- are answered by rule 3's own machinery below, which walks exactly the class
-- s 3 describes — children, and the descendants of children who are dead. A
-- separate `the issue` list would be a second answer to the same question,
-- free to disagree with the first.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A3 — "issue" (rules 1, 2, 3) against "descendants" (rules 5, 6,
-- 7, 8). The Act defines the first and not the second and never says they are
-- the same class. Readings: (i) coextensive, so rule 5's "no descendants" is
-- rule 2's "no issue"; (ii) "descendants" is the wider word and catches a
-- grandchild whose own parent is alive, who takes nothing under rule 3.
-- Taken: (i), but with the gate stated as SURVIVING descendants who would take
-- or be represented under rule 3 — see `the intestate leaves issue` below. On
-- reading (ii) a living grandchild of a living child would keep parents out
-- under rule 5 while taking nothing himself, which leaves that half of the
-- estate undistributed by any rule; reading (i) has no such gap.


§§ `Section 4 — Law regulating distribution`

-- s 4 is a CHOICE-OF-LAW section, and its two subsections split on the nature
-- of the property, not on where it is.
--
-- s 4(1) refers MOVABLES OUT to the law of the deceased's domicile. Where that
-- domicile is Singapore, the law referred to is Singapore law and so this Act;
-- where it is not, the operative rule is another country's law, which this
-- cleanroom does not model. That branch is named in the closing section as out
-- of scope. It is a boundary, not a gap.
--
-- s 4(2) claims IMMOVABLES for this Act unconditionally — "wherever he may
-- have been domiciled".
--
-- AMBIGUITY A4 — the situs of the property s 4 claims. ONE FORK, APPEARING
-- TWICE, and an earlier draft framed it for the immovables alone. s 4(2) is
-- silent as to where the immovable property is; s 4(1) refers the movables of
-- a Singapore-domiciled deceased to Singapore law WHEREVER THEY ARE SITUATED,
-- which is the same silence read from the other side. s 5, the section that
-- says what is actually distributed, speaks only of property "situated in
-- Singapore" in both of its limbs.
-- Readings: (i) s 4 states a choice-of-law rule of unlimited situs — for the
-- movables of a Singapore domiciliary and for immovables generally — which
-- s 5 then narrows to the fund the Act distributes; (ii) s 4 is impliedly
-- confined to Singapore property, since a Singapore statute does not regulate
-- the descent of foreign land or of assets it can never reach. Taken: BOTH ARE
-- ENCODED AND KEPT APART. `section 4 — the value of the property whose
-- distribution is regulated by this Act` follows the words of s 4 on reading
-- (i), and its movables leg carries `the value of the movable property
-- situated outside Singapore`; the fund that is actually divided among the
-- takers is s 5's, which is confined to Singapore. The two differ whenever
-- there is foreign property of either kind, and case 20 now carries a non-zero
-- figure in each cell so that both legs of the gap are exercised rather than
-- only the immovable one.

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 4(1) — "The distribution of the movable property of a person deceased shall be regulated by the law of the country in which he was domiciled at the time of his death."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 4(1) — the law of the country of domicile is the law of Singapore` IF
    `the deceased person`'s `domiciled in Singapore at the time of his death`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 4(2) — "The distribution of the immovable property of a person deceased shall be regulated by this Act wherever he may have been domiciled at the time of his death."
-- A definition carrier whose node is literally TRUE: s 4(2) has no condition
-- at all, and "wherever he may have been domiciled" is the point of it. The
-- constant is the isomorphic encoding of a rule with no antecedent.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 4(2) — the distribution of the immovable property is regulated by this Act` IF
    TRUE

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 4(1)-(2)
-- The reach of s 4 read on its own words. NOT the fund distributed — that is
-- s 5's, below, and the two are deliberately different. See AMBIGUITY A4.
--
-- IN PARTICULAR, THIS FIGURE IS GROSS OF THE TRUSTEE PROPERTY AND s 5'S IS
-- NOT, and that is the Act's own doing rather than an oversight. s 4 speaks of
-- "the movable property of a person deceased" and "the immovable property of a
-- person deceased" and does not qualify either; the word "beneficially" enters
-- only at s 5, where it appears in both limbs. So this decision follows s 4's
-- unqualified words and `the property situated in Singapore, whether movable
-- or immovable, or both, possessed beneficially` follows s 5's qualified ones.
-- Case 24a asserts the two figures side by side on an estate that contains
-- trust property, so the divergence is exhibited and not left to be discovered.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`section 4 — the value of the property whose distribution is regulated by this Act` MEANS
        (IF `section 4(1) — the law of the country of domicile is the law of Singapore` `the deceased person`
         THEN `the estate`'s `the value of the movable property situated in Singapore`
              PLUS `the estate`'s `the value of the movable property situated outside Singapore`
         ELSE 0)
    PLUS (IF `section 4(2) — the distribution of the immovable property is regulated by this Act`
          THEN `the estate`'s `the value of the immovable property situated in Singapore`
               PLUS `the estate`'s `the value of the immovable property situated outside Singapore`
          ELSE 0)
    WHERE
        `the estate` MEANS `the deceased person`'s `the estate`


§§ `Section 5 — Property of intestate to be distributed`

-- s 5 is a three-part sentence and is encoded as three things: a COMMENCEMENT
-- GATE ("dies intestate after 2 June 1967"), a two-limbed SITUS-AND-DOMICILE
-- GATE ((a) or (b)), and the DEDUCTION that turns the property into the fund
-- ("after payment thereout of the expenses of due administration as prescribed
-- by the Probate and Administration Act 1934").
--
-- THE DEDUCTION IS WHAT MAKES THE WHOLE DEFINITE. Every fraction in s 7 is a
-- fraction of this, not of the gross estate.
--
-- ***** A JOIN THIS ACT STATES BY NAME AND THE OTHER SIDE CANNOT ANSWER.
-- ***** SEAM 2 in `Joins with the other three Acts` below.
--
-- "as prescribed by the Probate and Administration Act 1934" is a pointer to
-- another Act for the CONTENT of the figure this section subtracts. The
-- provisions pointed at are that Act's ss 57-58 with its First and Second
-- Schedules: s 57(1) — "Where the estate of a deceased person is insolvent his
-- estate shall be administered in accordance with the rules set out in the
-- First Schedule" — and s 57(4), which sends a solvent estate to the Second
-- Schedule and makes it "applicable towards the discharge of the funeral,
-- testamentary and administration expenses, debts and liabilities payable
-- thereout in the order mentioned in the Second Schedule", subject to s 58's
-- charged property. `family-domain.l4` declares their inputs and says it does
-- so "so that probate-administration-act.l4 can state s 57 completely".
--
-- IT DOES NOT STATE IT, AND SAYS SO. `probate-administration-act.l4` puts
-- ss 57-58 and both Schedules out of scope, and its closing section names this
-- pointer expressly — "it is not that this Act declines to prescribe, it is
-- that this MODULE stops short of the sections that do". The consequence for
-- THIS module is that the figure the line below subtracts — `Estate`'s `the
-- funeral, testamentary and administration expenses` — has NO PRODUCER anywhere
-- in this cleanroom: it arrives on the case file as a sworn number, and the
-- pointer in s 5's own words is answered by nothing. That is a limit of the
-- corpus, not of this Act, and it is said here so that a reader does not take
-- the pointer for a computation that happens somewhere else. Both sides of this
-- seam now say so; neither can close it alone.
--
-- FOUR ONTOLOGY FIELDS EXIST FOR THIS JOIN AND HAVE NO READER AT ALL. Measured
-- across the four Act modules on 2026-08-26: `the debts and liabilities of the
-- deceased`, `the value of the interest in property which at the time of his
-- death is charged with the payment of money`, `the amount of the charge on
-- that interest` and `the deceased has by will, deed or other document
-- signified a contrary or other intention` occur only as fixture WRITES — every
-- occurrence is an `IS` in a record literal — and no rule in any of the four
-- files reads one. This module writes all four, in `an ISA estate`, and reads
-- none — which is correct for this Act, since s 5 names the expenses and
-- nothing else (see the closing section on why the debts are not deducted). It
-- is reported as a domain gap for the corpus, not repaired here.

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5 — "If a person dies intestate after 2 June 1967"
`2 June 1967` MEANS YMD 1967 6 2

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5 — "he being at the time of his death —"
-- The moment every survivorship question in the Act is asked from. NOTHING
-- where the death is not dated, which the ontology's `Vital status` keeps
-- distinct from a person who is still living: `dead, the date of death not
-- known` is a real case in a probate file and must not be allowed to read as
-- a date.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the date of the death of the intestate` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the deceased person`'s `the deceased`'s `vital status`
    WHEN `living`                             THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `died on` `the date of death`        THEN JUST `the date of death`
    WHEN `dead, the date of death not known`  THEN NOTHING

-- AMBIGUITY A5 — an undated death. s 5's gate is "dies intestate after 2 June
-- 1967", and a death with no date in evidence cannot be shown to fall after
-- it. Readings: (i) the gate fails, and this Act distributes nothing until the
-- date is proved; (ii) the gate is presumed satisfied, the Act having been in
-- force for the whole of any plausible window. Taken: (i). The burden of
-- bringing the death within the section is on whoever claims under it, and
-- reading (ii) would also have to supply a moment for every "surviving" test
-- in s 7 out of nothing. An undated death therefore yields an empty
-- distribution, which reads as not proved rather than as nobody taking; an
-- assertion below exhibits it (case 21).
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5 — "If a person dies intestate after 2 June 1967"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 5 — the person dies intestate after 2 June 1967` IF
        `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `the deceased person`
    AND CONSIDER `the date of the death of the intestate` `the deceased person`
        WHEN NOTHING                    THEN FALSE
        WHEN JUST `the date of death`   THEN Day `the date of death` GREATER THAN Day `2 June 1967`

-- "possessed beneficially" IS TWO WORDS AND TWO QUESTIONS, AND AN EARLIER
-- DRAFT OF THIS MODULE ANSWERED ONLY ONE OF THEM. It read a gross value cell
-- and never subtracted the property the deceased held for somebody else, so
-- an estate whose whole Singapore content was trust property passed this gate
-- and was distributed to the intestate's family. That is the exact opposite of
-- what the section says: "beneficially" appears in BOTH limbs of s 5 and again
-- in its operative words, "the persons entitled to succeed beneficially".
--
-- THE SUBTRAHEND IS IN THE ONTOLOGY, UNDER THE STATUTE'S OWN CONTRASTING
-- WORDS: `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was
-- entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore`. The
-- four value cells are GROSS by construction — `family-domain.l4` says so
-- where it splits the trustee exclusion by situs — so the subtraction has to
-- be made here, and it is made in both limbs and in the fund alike.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A6 — "possessed", the word that is left over once "beneficially"
-- has been given effect. The Act gives no test of possession, and the ontology
-- records values, not chattels. Readings: (i) a positive NET value in the
-- relevant cell — gross less the trustee figure — is the evidence of
-- beneficial possession; (ii) possession is a further already-construed fact,
-- so an estate could be possessed of property of nil net value. Taken: (i) —
-- an estate worth nothing beneficially in Singapore yields nothing to
-- distribute either way, so the two readings differ only where a nil-valued
-- asset exists, and the ontology offers no field for one.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A17 — the trustee figure is recorded by SITUS and not by kind, so
-- there is one Singapore number covering movables and immovables together.
-- s 5(a) reaches both kinds and nets exactly; s 5(b) reaches only "immovable
-- property situated in Singapore" and cannot. Readings: (i) subtract the whole
-- Singapore trustee figure from the Singapore immovable value, floored at nil;
-- (ii) subtract nothing under limb (b), the ontology being unable to say how
-- much of the trustee property is land. Taken: (i). Reading (ii) distributes
-- property the deceased held for somebody else, which is the error the word
-- "beneficially" exists to prevent; reading (i) can only fail to bring an
-- estate within the section, which is the same allocation of the burden as
-- AMBIGUITY A5 and AMBIGUITY A8. If a later ontology splits the trustee cell
-- by kind, this is the line that changes.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5(a) — "domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property, whether movable or immovable, or both, situated in Singapore; or"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` IF
        "domiciled in Singapore"
    ... `the deceased person`'s `domiciled in Singapore at the time of his death`
    AND "and possessed beneficially of property, whether movable or immovable, or both, situated in Singapore"
    ... `the property situated in Singapore, whether movable or immovable, or both, possessed beneficially` `the deceased person` GREATER THAN 0

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5(b) — "domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore,"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 5(b) — domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore` IF
        "domiciled outside Singapore"
    ... NOT `the deceased person`'s `domiciled in Singapore at the time of his death`
    AND "and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore"
    ... `the immovable property situated in Singapore, possessed beneficially` `the deceased person` GREATER THAN 0

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5(a) — "possessed beneficially of property, whether movable or immovable, or both, situated in Singapore"; Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 62(2)(a) — "any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially"
-- THE TWO FLOORS BELOW ARE NOT THE SAME KIND OF THING, and the difference is
-- worth stating because a reader auditing coverage will otherwise ask the same
-- question of both.
--
-- This one, on limb (a), is DEFENSIVE AND UNREACHABLE ON A WELL-FORMED FILE.
-- `family-domain.l4` treats the trustee cell as an EXCLUSION drawn out of the
-- same asset base — "the trustee exclusion is split by situs like everything
-- else" — so on any consistent record the Singapore trustee figure is at most
-- the sum of the Singapore movable and immovable cells, and the subtraction
-- cannot go below nil. The floor guards a malformed record, not a case the Act
-- contemplates. Case 24d is such a record, labelled as one, and asserts what
-- the floor does with it; it is the only fixture in this file that is not a
-- possible state of affairs.
--
-- The floor on limb (b), by contrast, is REACHED BY A CONSISTENT FILE, because
-- limb (b) subtracts the whole Singapore trustee figure from the Singapore
-- IMMOVABLE cell alone — that is AMBIGUITY A17 — and the trustee property may
-- be partly movable. Case 24b is an ordinary estate that drives it.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`the property situated in Singapore, whether movable or immovable, or both, possessed beneficially` MEANS
    max 0 (      `the deceased person`'s `the estate`'s `the value of the movable property situated in Singapore`
           PLUS  `the deceased person`'s `the estate`'s `the value of the immovable property situated in Singapore`
           MINUS `the property held as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5(b) — "possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore"; Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 62(2)(a)
-- See AMBIGUITY A17: the whole Singapore trustee figure comes off the
-- immovable cell, because the ontology cannot say what part of it is land.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`the immovable property situated in Singapore, possessed beneficially` MEANS
    max 0 (      `the deceased person`'s `the estate`'s `the value of the immovable property situated in Singapore`
           MINUS `the property held as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5(a)-(b) — "possessed beneficially"; Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 62(2)(a) — "any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially"
-- The quantity s 5's word "beneficially" excludes, named once so that both
-- limbs and the fund subtract the same thing.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`the property held as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore` MEANS
    `the deceased person`'s `the estate`'s `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5 — "that property or the proceeds thereof"
-- The words "that property" pick up whichever limb was satisfied, and the two limbs reach
-- different property: (a) catches both kinds situated in Singapore, (b) only
-- the immovable. A deceased domiciled outside Singapore whose only Singapore
-- asset is a bank balance is caught by neither, and takes nothing under this
-- Act — which is s 4(1) doing its work, not an omission. Either way "that
-- property" is what the limb found him possessed of BENEFICIALLY, so the
-- trustee property is out of the fund as well as out of the gate.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`section 5 — that property` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `the deceased person`
           THEN `the property situated in Singapore, whether movable or immovable, or both, possessed beneficially` `the deceased person`
           IF `section 5(b) — domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore` `the deceased person`
           THEN `the immovable property situated in Singapore, possessed beneficially` `the deceased person`
           OTHERWISE 0

-- AMBIGUITY A7 — an estate whose expenses exceed the property. s 5 says the
-- property is distributed "after payment thereout of the expenses of due
-- administration" and does not contemplate a shortfall. Readings: (i) the fund
-- is nil; (ii) the fund is negative and every share is a negative quantity.
-- Taken: (i) — the fund is floored at nil, because a share of a negative estate
-- is not a thing the Act creates, and the creditors' shortfall is the Probate
-- and Administration Act's business under its First Schedule, not this Act's.
--
-- SAY EXACTLY WHERE THE NIL LANDS, because an earlier wording of this entry
-- said "the fund is nil and nobody takes" and the second half of that is not
-- what happens. The GATE is s 5's, and s 5(a) asks whether the deceased was
-- "possessed beneficially of property ... situated in Singapore" — a question
-- about the property, not about what is left of it after the expenses. So the
-- gate opens, this Act has effect, the persons entitled are identified and
-- their fractions still sum to 1. What is nil is the thing the fractions are
-- fractions OF. Case 23a asserts that combination.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5 — "after payment thereout of the expenses of due administration as prescribed by the Probate and Administration Act 1934, shall be distributed among the persons entitled to succeed beneficially to that property or the proceeds thereof."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`section 5 — the property to be distributed, after payment thereout of the expenses of due administration` MEANS
    max 0 (   `section 5 — that property` `the deceased person`
        MINUS `the deceased person`'s `the estate`'s `the funeral, testamentary and administration expenses`)


§§ `Section 6 — Persons held to be similarly related to deceased`

-- s 6 opens "For the purposes of distribution —" and states two rules of
-- construction, one abolishing a distinction and one creating a ranking.

-- s 6(a), FIRST LIMB — the paternal / maternal distinction. This is encoded IN
-- THE SHAPE OF THE ONTOLOGY rather than in a test: `Family`'s
-- `the grandparents of the deceased` and `the uncles and aunts of the
-- deceased` are FLAT lists with no paternal/maternal split, so the
-- distinction s 6(a) abolishes cannot be expressed, let alone applied.
-- Recording a side and then instructing this module to ignore it would invite
-- back exactly what the limb removes. The node is TRUE because the limb has no
-- antecedent: it is unconditional.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 6(a) — "there shall be no distinction between those who are related to a person deceased through his father and those who are related to him through his mother"
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 6(a) — there shall be no distinction between those related through his father and those related through his mother` IF
    TRUE

-- s 6(a), SECOND LIMB — the child en ventre sa mère. Unlike the first limb,
-- this one settles MEMBERSHIP and so has to be tested: a child conceived at
-- the death and later born alive is in, and a child conceived after the death
-- is out. See `family-domain.l4`'s note on the field for why the two limbs of
-- one paragraph are treated differently.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A18 — WHETHER THE SECOND LIMB SETTLES MEMBERSHIP AT ALL. The
-- limb is drafted as a non-discrimination rule — "there shall be no
-- distinction between ... nor between ..." — and on its face it levels two
-- classes rather than shutting anybody out. Readings: (i) it says nothing
-- about a person who is in neither class, whose exclusion (if any) comes from
-- the general law and not from this paragraph; (ii) the two classes it names
-- are exhaustive of the persons from among whom takers are drawn, so a person
-- conceived after the death is outside them. Taken: (ii). On reading (i) this
-- Act has no answer at all for a child conceived after the death — nothing
-- else in it excludes him, so he would take as though he had been alive
-- throughout — and reading (ii) at least gives the paragraph's own enumeration
-- its full effect. THE PART THAT WAS A DEFECT RATHER THAN A CHOICE: an earlier
-- draft applied the test to only two of the six classes of taker, so a
-- posthumously-conceived brother took the whole estate under rule 6 while a
-- posthumously-conceived child on identical facts took nothing under rule 3.
-- It is now applied to every class, through
-- `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a)
-- names` below. Case 17a exhibits the sibling case that used to escape it.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A9 — a claimant whose date of birth is not in evidence. Readings:
-- (i) taken as actually born in the intestate's lifetime, the s 6(a)
-- conceived-in-the-womb case being the exceptional one that carries its own
-- recorded fact; (ii) not proved to be within either class, and so excluded.
-- Taken: (i). Reading (ii) would drop an undated child silently out of a
-- distribution, which is the worse failure: a probate file routinely records a
-- surviving adult child without a birth certificate, and no one reading the
-- output would see why the share had gone.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A19 — THE SAME-DAY BOUNDARY, in both of the places it arises. The
-- Act's boundaries are MOMENTS — s 5's "he being at the time of his death",
-- s 6(a)'s "actually born in his lifetime", s 7's "surviving" — and the
-- ontology records only DATES, so a birth or a death sharing the intestate's
-- date of death cannot be placed on either side of the moment and the
-- comparison has to be made inclusive or strict by choice.
-- Readings: (i) resolve the unknown moment against the person asserting it;
-- (ii) resolve it in favour of inclusion. Taken: (i) for survivorship and (ii)
-- for birth, which is the same allocation of the burden as AMBIGUITY A8 and
-- AMBIGUITY A9 respectively. A claimant recorded as having died on the
-- intestate's own date of death is NOT shown to have survived him, so
-- `the person survives the intestate` is strict (`GREATER THAN`); a claimant
-- born on that date IS taken to have been born in the lifetime, so
-- `actually born in the lifetime of the intestate` is inclusive (`AT MOST`).
-- The two are therefore decided in opposite directions on purpose, and the
-- boundary is asserted from both sides beside case 17.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 6(a) — "nor between those who were actually born in his lifetime and those who at the date of his death were only conceived in the womb but who have subsequently been born alive"
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the date of the death of the intestate` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `actually born in the lifetime of the intestate` IF
    CONSIDER `the person`'s `date of birth`
    WHEN NOTHING                  THEN TRUE
    WHEN JUST `the date of birth` THEN
        CONSIDER `the date of the death of the intestate`
        WHEN NOTHING                        THEN TRUE
        WHEN JUST `the day the intestate died` THEN Day `the date of birth` AT MOST Day `the day the intestate died`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 6(a)
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the date of the death of the intestate` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` IF
        "those who were actually born in his lifetime"
              ... `actually born in the lifetime of the intestate` `the person` `the date of the death of the intestate`
    OR  "those who at the date of his death were only conceived in the womb but who have subsequently been born alive"
              ... `the person`'s `at the date of the death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive`

-- s 6(b). Whole or half blood is a RELATION to the deceased, so the two
-- predicates below read the `Blood tie` the ontology hangs on the edge; there
-- is no such field on a `Person`, and there should not be, because a lineal
-- descendant has no whole-or-half blood tie at all.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 6(b) — "those of the whole blood related to him in the same degree"
GIVEN `the relative` IS A `Collateral relative`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `related to the deceased by the whole blood` IF
    CONSIDER `the relative`'s `of the whole blood or of the half blood`
    WHEN `of the whole blood` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `of the half blood`  THEN FALSE

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 6(b) — "those related to a person deceased by the half blood"
GIVEN `the relative` IS A `Collateral relative`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `related to the deceased by the half blood` IF
    NOT `related to the deceased by the whole blood` `the relative`

-- AMBIGUITY A11 — what "rank immediately after" does to a share. Readings:
-- (i) the half blood take NOTHING while anyone of the whole blood in the same
-- degree is entitled, and take in full in default of them; (ii) both classes
-- share concurrently, the half blood taking a reduced portion (the rule of
-- some other systems, under which a half-blood relative takes half a share).
-- Taken: (i). The word "rank" is the language of an order of succession, not of
-- abatement, and the Act nowhere states a fraction for the half blood, which
-- it would have had to do on reading (ii) — every other portion in s 7 is
-- spelled out.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A12 — the degree at which the ranking is applied under rule 6.
-- s 6(b) ranks only persons "related to him in the same degree", and rule 6
-- puts brothers and sisters (second degree) beside the children of deceased
-- brothers and sisters (third degree) in one distribution. Readings: (i) the
-- ranking is applied to the CLASS OF STOCKS, i.e. at the sibling level, so a
-- half-blood brother is excluded while a whole-blood brother's children still
-- represent him; (ii) the ranking is applied to the individual takers, so a
-- half-blood brother (second degree) would outrank a whole-blood nephew
-- (third degree). Taken: (i). Rule 6 divides "in equal portions between the
-- brothers and sisters" and only then lets children take "the share which the
-- deceased brother or sister would have taken" — the division is a division
-- among siblings, so the class s 6(b) orders is the class of siblings.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 6(b) — "those related to a person deceased by the half blood shall rank immediately after those of the whole blood related to him in the same degree."
GIVEN `the relatives of one degree` IS A LIST OF `Collateral relative`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Collateral relative`
`section 6(b) — those of the whole blood, the half blood ranking immediately after them` MEANS
    IF   null `those of the whole blood`
    THEN `those of the half blood`
    ELSE `those of the whole blood`
    WHERE
        `those of the whole blood` MEANS
            filter `related to the deceased by the whole blood` `the relatives of one degree`
        `those of the half blood` MEANS
            filter `related to the deceased by the half blood` `the relatives of one degree`


§§ `Section 7 — Rules for distribution — the facts the nine rules read`

-- s 7's chapeau: "In effecting such distribution, the following rules shall be
-- observed:". The words "such distribution" point back to s 5, so every rule below is a rule
-- about the fund s 5 defines.
--
-- These first definitions are not themselves rules. They answer the questions
-- the nine rules keep asking — who survives, who is a spouse, who is a parent
-- — and they answer each once so that nine rules cannot disagree.

-- AMBIGUITY A8 — a claimant recorded as dead with no date of death. Readings:
-- (i) he is not shown to have survived, and so does not take; (ii) the
-- distribution cannot be computed at all until the date is proved. Taken: (i).
-- Survivorship is what the claimant must establish — rule 3's own words put
-- the alternative as "in case any of those children be then dead" — and under
-- reading (i) a stock whose head died on an unknown date is still represented
-- by his descendants, which is the answer rule 3's Proviso No. (2) points to.
--
-- The comparison below is STRICT, so a claimant recorded as having died on the
-- intestate's own date of death does not survive him. That is AMBIGUITY A19,
-- argued at s 6(a) above, where the mirror-image choice is made the other way.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rules 1, 2, 4 ("a surviving spouse"), rules 6, 7, 8 ("no surviving spouse"), rule 3 ("in case any of those children be then dead"), s 8 ("leaves surviving him")
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the date of the death of the intestate` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person survives the intestate` IF
    CONSIDER `the person`'s `vital status`
    WHEN `living` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `died on` `the date of death` THEN
        CONSIDER `the date of the death of the intestate`
        WHEN NOTHING                           THEN FALSE
        WHEN JUST `the day the intestate died` THEN Day `the date of death` GREATER THAN Day `the day the intestate died`
    WHEN `dead, the date of death not known` THEN FALSE

-- THE TWO TESTS EVERY CLASS OF TAKER HAS TO PASS, IN ONE PLACE. s 7's rules
-- speak of persons "surviving"; s 6(a) opens "For the purposes of
-- distribution —" and so reaches every one of those rules, not the two it
-- happens to be easiest to apply to. Naming the conjunction once is what stops
-- the two questions from being asked of children and of the children of a
-- deceased sibling and of nobody else — which is what an earlier draft did,
-- with the result that a brother born eight months after the death took the
-- whole estate while a child born on the same facts took nothing. See
-- AMBIGUITY A18.
--
-- WHICH SITES CAN BITE, AND WHICH ARE INERT — MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. This
-- decision is called at seven places, and reverting each of them in turn to
-- the bare `the person survives the intestate` gives the following:
--
--   CAN BITE, and each now has a case that fails if the site is reverted:
--     `the uncles and aunts of the intestate who survive him` (case 17d — an
--       uncle conceived after the death is possible, the intestate's
--       grandparent bearing another child, and rule 8 is the rule that pays
--       him);
--     `rule 3 — this child is a stock which takes ...` (case 17);
--     `rule 6 — this brother or sister shares, or has children who take
--       according to their stocks` (cases 17a and 17e).
--   The same test is also conjoined, spelled out rather than through this
--   decision, in `rule 6 — the children of a deceased brother or sister who
--   take according to their stocks` (case 17f).
--
--   CANNOT BITE ON ANY WELL-FORMED FILE, and reverting each leaves every
--   assertion in this module green:
--     `the spouses surviving the intestate` — a person born after the
--       intestate's death and not conceived at it cannot have contracted a
--       marriage to him subsisting at that death;
--     `the parents of the intestate who survive him` and `the grandparents of
--       the intestate who survive him` — a parent or grandparent of the
--       intestate is necessarily born before him;
--     `rule 3 — divided by equal portions per stirpes`'s `the takers of one
--       stock` — it is reached only for a child that has already passed
--       `rule 3 — this child is a stock which takes ...`, which conjoins the
--       same test on the same person, so the second call is redundant;
--     `rule 6 — the takers of the share which the brother or sister would have
--       taken` — redundant for the same reason since AMBIGUITY A21: a stock
--       qualifies either because the sibling is within s 6(a) and survives, in
--       which case the test is true, or because he is DEAD, in which case both
--       this test and the bare survivorship test are false. It was NOT
--       redundant before A21 was fixed, and case 17e is the fixture that shows
--       why: the earlier stock test admitted a sibling who was alive and
--       excluded, and this site was the only thing then deciding whether he or
--       his children were paid.
--
-- The test is applied at the inert sites anyway, and the reason is not
-- decoration: a rule applied wherever it could bite and nowhere else is a rule
-- whose next reader has to re-derive the exhaustive list of where it could
-- bite, and the list above is exactly the derivation that was got wrong once
-- already (see AMBIGUITY A18). At the three inert class sites it also acts as
-- a guard on a malformed record, in the same way as the floor at s 5(a).
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 6 — "For the purposes of distribution —"; s 6(a); s 7 rules 1-8 ("surviving")
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the date of the death of the intestate` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a) names` IF
        `the person survives the intestate` `the person` `the date of the death of the intestate`
    AND `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive`
            `the person` `the date of the death of the intestate`

-- The plural is s 8's, not s 7's: rules 1, 2 and 4 each say "a surviving
-- spouse" in the singular, and s 8 is the section that contemplates more than
-- one. The list is therefore built here and counted there.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rules 1, 2, 4; s 8 — "leaves surviving him more than one wife"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Marriage
`the spouses surviving the intestate` MEANS
    filter `a subsisting marriage to a spouse who survives the intestate`
           (`the deceased person`'s `the family`'s `the marriages of the deceased`)
    WHERE
        `a subsisting marriage to a spouse who survives the intestate` `the marriage` MEANS
                `the marriage`'s `subsisting at the death of the deceased, in the sense of not dissolved or annulled before the death`
            AND `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a) names`
                    (`the marriage`'s `the spouse`)
                    (`the date of the death of the intestate` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rules 1, 2, 4
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the intestate leaves a surviving spouse` IF
    NOT null (`the spouses surviving the intestate` `the deceased person`)

-- AMBIGUITY A10 — "a parent or parents". Rules 1, 4 and 5 say "parent" and
-- never add the word "surviving" to it, although the very same rules say
-- "surviving spouse".
-- Readings: (i) a parent who survives the intestate, a dead parent being
-- incapable of taking an estate; (ii) the bare existence of a parent, so that
-- rule 1's "no parent" would almost never be satisfied — everyone has two.
-- Taken: (i). Reading (ii) makes rule 1 nearly a dead letter and contradicts
-- rules 6 to 8, which run the same class through the phrase "If there are no
-- surviving spouse, descendants or parents".
--
-- AND A SECOND, NARROWER CHOICE, ON THE ONTOLOGY RATHER THAN THE ACT: a
-- `NOTHING` in `the father of the deceased` means the parent is NOT IN
-- EVIDENCE, not that he is dead. A parent not in evidence cannot be shown to
-- survive, so he does not take; that is the same allocation of the burden as
-- AMBIGUITY A8, and it is stated here because a reader could otherwise take an
-- empty field for a positive finding of death.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 1 ("no parent"), rule 4 ("a parent or parents"), rule 5 ("the parent or parents of the intestate"), rule 6 ("no surviving spouse, descendants or parents")
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`the parents of the intestate who survive him` MEANS
    filter `a parent who survives the intestate`
           (append (maybeToList (`the deceased person`'s `the family`'s `the father of the deceased`))
                   (maybeToList (`the deceased person`'s `the family`'s `the mother of the deceased`)))
    WHERE
        `a parent who survives the intestate` `the parent` MEANS
            `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a) names`
                `the parent` (`the date of the death of the intestate` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 7 — "but grandparents of the intestate"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`the grandparents of the intestate who survive him` MEANS
    filter `a grandparent who survives the intestate`
           (`the deceased person`'s `the family`'s `the grandparents of the deceased`)
    WHERE
        `a grandparent who survives the intestate` `the grandparent` MEANS
            `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a) names`
                `the grandparent` (`the date of the death of the intestate` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 8 — "but uncles and aunts of the intestate"
-- Rule 8 gives the uncles and aunts no representatives: it says they "shall
-- take the whole of the estate in equal portions" and stops. So this class
-- holds only those who survive, and a deceased uncle's children take nothing.
-- Contrast rule 6, which expressly provides for "children of deceased brothers
-- or sisters", and rule 3, which reaches "the remotest degree". The silence is
-- the Act's, not this module's.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Collateral relative`
`the uncles and aunts of the intestate who survive him` MEANS
    filter `an uncle or aunt who survives the intestate`
           (`the deceased person`'s `the family`'s `the uncles and aunts of the deceased`)
    WHERE
        `an uncle or aunt who survives the intestate` `the relative` MEANS
            `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a) names`
                (`the relative`'s `the relative`)
                (`the date of the death of the intestate` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rules 1-9; s 8
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the fraction of the property distributable under this Act` IS A Share
GIVETH AN Entitlement
`the entitlement of` MEANS
    Entitlement WITH
        `the person entitled` IS `an individual` `the person`
        `the fraction of the property distributable under this Act` IS `the fraction of the property distributable under this Act`


§§ `Section 7 rule 3 — per stirpes, and the two provisos`

-- THE RECURSION IS THE RULE. Rule 3 divides "by equal portions per stirpes to
-- and amongst the children of the person dying intestate and such persons as
-- legally represent those children, in case any of those children be then
-- dead", and Proviso No. (2) extends that to "Descendants of the intestate to
-- the remotest degree". To divide per stirpes you must ask, of a predeceased
-- child, who stands in his place — and of those, again, without limit. So the
-- distribution below calls ITSELF on a predeceased child, with that child's
-- own portion as the share to divide.
--
-- PROVISO No. (1) FIXES THE DIRECTION OF THE RECURSION: "The persons who
-- legally represent the children of an intestate are their descendants and not
-- their next-of-kin." So it runs DOWN the `children` edge only, and never
-- sideways to a brother, a spouse or a next-of-kin of the predeceased child.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A13 — a child who predeceased leaving no surviving descendant.
-- Readings: (i) he is not a stock at all, so the fund is divided among the
-- stocks that do take (three children, one of whom died childless, gives one
-- half each to the other two); (ii) he is a stock whose portion simply fails
-- (each of three children takes one third, and the dead child's third goes
-- nowhere, leaving the estate part-undistributed). Taken: (i). Reading (ii)
-- would leave a fraction of the estate belonging to no one, which no rule in
-- s 7 provides for — rule 9 catches only a total default — and rule 3's own
-- phrase "in case any of those children be then dead" pairs the dead child
-- with the persons who represent him, so a child with no representative is
-- outside the division rather than inside it with an empty share.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A14 — whether s 3's definition of "child" is imported at every
-- generation of the descent. Readings: (i) yes: each link must be a "child"
-- within s 3, so an illegitimate, unadopted link breaks the chain; (ii) no:
-- Proviso No. (1) and (2) speak of "descendants", which s 3 does not define,
-- so descent is a question of fact. Taken: (i). s 3's definition governs "this
-- Act", and s 3's own definition of "issue" — "children and the descendants of
-- deceased children" — builds the wider word out of the defined one.

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 3 — "and such persons as legally represent those children, in case any of those children be then dead"; Proviso No. (1); Proviso No. (2); s 3 ("child"); s 6(a)
GIVEN `the relationship of child to parent` IS A Child
      `the date of the death of the intestate` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rule 3 — this child is a stock which takes, in his own right or through those who legally represent him` IF
        `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` `the relationship of child to parent`
    AND `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive`
            (`the relationship of child to parent`'s `the child`) `the date of the death of the intestate`
    AND (   `the person survives the intestate` (`the relationship of child to parent`'s `the child`) `the date of the death of the intestate`
         OR `rule 3 — the ancestor leaves a child who takes, or who is himself represented`
                (`the relationship of child to parent`'s `the child`) `the date of the death of the intestate`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 3; Proviso No. (2) — "Descendants of the intestate to the remotest degree stand in the place of their parent or other ancestor"
GIVEN `the ancestor` IS A Person
      `the date of the death of the intestate` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rule 3 — the ancestor leaves a child who takes, or who is himself represented` IF
    any `a stock of this ancestor which takes` (`the ancestor`'s `children`)
    WHERE
        `a stock of this ancestor which takes` `the relationship of child to parent` MEANS
            `rule 3 — this child is a stock which takes, in his own right or through those who legally represent him`
                `the relationship of child to parent` `the date of the death of the intestate`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 3 — "shall be distributed by equal portions per stirpes to and amongst the children of the person dying intestate and such persons as legally represent those children, in case any of those children be then dead"; Proviso No. (1); Proviso No. (2) — "and take according to their stocks the share which he or she would have taken"
GIVEN `the ancestor` IS A Person
      `the date of the death of the intestate` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the share to be divided` IS A Share
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`rule 3 — divided by equal portions per stirpes` MEANS
    IF   count `the stocks` EQUALS 0
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE concat (map `the takers of one stock` `the stocks`)
    WHERE
        `the stocks` MEANS
            filter `a stock of this ancestor which takes` (`the ancestor`'s `children`)
        `a stock of this ancestor which takes` `the relationship of child to parent` MEANS
            `rule 3 — this child is a stock which takes, in his own right or through those who legally represent him`
                `the relationship of child to parent` `the date of the death of the intestate`
        -- "by equal portions": one portion per stock, however many people end
        -- up standing in that stock's place.
        `the share of one stock` MEANS `the share to be divided` DIVIDED BY count `the stocks`
        -- "in case any of those children be then dead" — the head of the stock
        -- takes if he survives; otherwise the same question is asked of him,
        -- which is Proviso No. (2)'s "to the remotest degree".
        `the takers of one stock` `the relationship of child to parent` MEANS
            IF   `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a) names`
                     (`the relationship of child to parent`'s `the child`) `the date of the death of the intestate`
            THEN LIST (`the entitlement of` (`the relationship of child to parent`'s `the child`) `the share of one stock`)
            ELSE `rule 3 — divided by equal portions per stirpes`
                     (`the relationship of child to parent`'s `the child`)
                     `the date of the death of the intestate`
                     `the share of one stock`

-- "leaves issue" (rules 1, 2, 3) and "there are no descendants" (rules 5, 6,
-- 7, 8) are one question asked twice — see AMBIGUITY A3 — so they are one
-- decision under two names.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rules 1, 2, 3 ("issue"), rules 5, 6, 7, 8 ("descendants"); s 3 ("issue")
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the intestate leaves issue` AKA `there are descendants` MEANS
    `rule 3 — the ancestor leaves a child who takes, or who is himself represented`
        (`the deceased person`'s `the deceased`)
        (`the date of the death of the intestate` `the deceased person`)


§§ `Section 7 rules 1, 2 and 4 — the surviving spouse`

-- The three rules that give a spouse a share are read together, because they
-- partition the same question: rule 1 where there is neither issue nor parent,
-- rule 2 where there is issue, rule 4 where there is no issue but a parent.
-- They are mutually exclusive on their own terms and they are exhaustive of
-- the case where a spouse survives, so the BRANCH below has an OTHERWISE only
-- for the case where no spouse survives at all.
--
-- NOTE WHAT RULES 2 AND 4 DO **NOT** SAY. Neither is a fall-through: rule 2
-- gives the spouse one half and leaves the other half to rule 3; rule 4 gives
-- the spouse one half "and the parent or parents to the other half". An
-- occupied rung does not close the next one.

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 1 — "If an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent, the spouse shall be entitled to the whole of the estate."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rule 1 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` IF
        "leaving a surviving spouse"
    ... `the intestate leaves a surviving spouse` `the deceased person`
    AND "no issue"
    ... NOT `the intestate leaves issue` `the deceased person`
    AND "and no parent"
    ... null (`the parents of the intestate who survive him` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 2 — "If an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and issue, the spouse shall be entitled to one-half of the estate."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rule 2 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and issue` IF
        "leaving a surviving spouse"
    ... `the intestate leaves a surviving spouse` `the deceased person`
    AND "and issue"
    ... `the intestate leaves issue` `the deceased person`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 4 — "If an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and no issue but a parent or parents, the spouse shall be entitled to one-half of the estate and the parent or parents to the other half of the estate."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rule 4 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and no issue but a parent or parents` IF
        "leaving a surviving spouse"
    ... `the intestate leaves a surviving spouse` `the deceased person`
    AND "and no issue"
    ... NOT `the intestate leaves issue` `the deceased person`
    AND "but a parent or parents"
    ... NOT null (`the parents of the intestate who survive him` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 1 ("the whole of the estate"), rule 2 ("one-half of the estate"), rule 4 ("one-half of the estate")
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Share
`the share of the estate to which the surviving spouse is entitled` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `rule 1 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` `the deceased person`
           THEN 1
           IF `rule 2 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and issue` `the deceased person`
           THEN 1 DIVIDED BY 2
           IF `rule 4 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and no issue but a parent or parents` `the deceased person`
           THEN 1 DIVIDED BY 2
           OTHERWISE 0


§§ `Section 7 rule 3 — the share the issue take`

-- "Subject to the rights of the surviving spouse, if any" is the whole of the
-- interaction between rule 3 and rules 1, 2 and 4: what the issue take is the
-- estate less whatever the spouse takes. Where there is no spouse the
-- subtraction is of nothing and the issue take the whole.
--
-- RULE 3'S PARENTHETICAL — "(both as to the undistributed portion and the
-- reversionary interest)" — has no separate encoding, and the reason is in
-- `family-domain.l4`: it does not create an asset class beside the four value
-- fields, it directs that a reversionary interest is not held back from the
-- per stirpes division. There is no rule here that would hold it back.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 3 — "Subject to the rights of the surviving spouse, if any, the estate (both as to the undistributed portion and the reversionary interest) of an intestate who leaves issue shall be distributed by equal portions per stirpes"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Share
`rule 3 — the share of the estate distributed among the issue, subject to the rights of the surviving spouse` MEANS
    IF   `the intestate leaves issue` `the deceased person`
    THEN 1 MINUS `the share of the estate to which the surviving spouse is entitled` `the deceased person`
    ELSE 0

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 3; Proviso No. (1); Proviso No. (2)
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`rule 3 — the entitlements of the issue` MEANS
    `rule 3 — divided by equal portions per stirpes`
        (`the deceased person`'s `the deceased`)
        (`the date of the death of the intestate` `the deceased person`)
        (`rule 3 — the share of the estate distributed among the issue, subject to the rights of the surviving spouse` `the deceased person`)


§§ `Section 7 rules 4 and 5 — the parents`

-- Rule 4 and rule 5 give the parents the same quantity by two routes, and
-- rule 5 says so in terms: it takes the estate "subject to the rights of the
-- surviving spouse (if any) as provided in rule 4". So the parental share is
-- computed ONCE, on rule 5's wider words, and rule 4's half is stated beside
-- it and asserted to agree rather than distributed a second time. Case 4
-- asserts the agreement on facts where both rules apply.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 4 — "and the parent or parents to the other half of the estate"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Share
`rule 4 — the share of the estate to which the parent or parents are entitled` MEANS
    IF   `rule 4 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and no issue but a parent or parents` `the deceased person`
    THEN 1 DIVIDED BY 2
    ELSE 0

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 5 — "If there are no descendants, the parent or parents of the intestate shall take the estate, in equal portions if there be 2 parents, subject to the rights of the surviving spouse (if any) as provided in rule 4."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Share
`rule 5 — the share of the estate the parent or parents take` MEANS
    IF   `rule 5 — there are no descendants, and there are a parent or parents of the intestate` `the deceased person`
    -- "subject to the rights of the surviving spouse (if any) as provided in
    -- rule 4": the parents take what is left after the spouse's share, which
    -- is one half where rule 4 applies and the whole where no spouse survives.
    THEN 1 MINUS `the share of the estate to which the surviving spouse is entitled` `the deceased person`
    ELSE 0

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 5 — "If there are no descendants, the parent or parents of the intestate shall take the estate"
-- RULE 5'S ANTECEDENT, NAMED, BECAUSE IT HAS TO GATE TWO THINGS AND AN EARLIER
-- DRAFT GATED ONLY ONE. The share below was already conditioned on it; the
-- LIST of entitlements was not, so wherever surviving parents existed AND
-- there were descendants — the case rule 5 expressly excludes — each parent was
-- emitted with a share of nought and then appeared in `the persons entitled to
-- succeed beneficially, and their shares`, which is s 5's own phrase. The
-- module's own sum-check could not see it, a share of nought adding nothing to
-- the total. Case 4a is the case that exhibits it; no fixture had both a child
-- and a surviving parent before.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rule 5 — there are no descendants, and there are a parent or parents of the intestate` IF
        "If there are no descendants"
    ... NOT `there are descendants` `the deceased person`
    AND "the parent or parents of the intestate"
    ... NOT null (`the parents of the intestate who survive him` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 5 — "in equal portions if there be 2 parents"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`rule 5 — the entitlements of the parent or parents` MEANS
    IF   NOT `rule 5 — there are no descendants, and there are a parent or parents of the intestate` `the deceased person`
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE map `the entitlement of one parent` `the parents`
    WHERE
        `the parents` MEANS `the parents of the intestate who survive him` `the deceased person`
        `the entitlement of one parent` `the parent` MEANS
            `the entitlement of` `the parent`
                (`rule 5 — the share of the estate the parent or parents take` `the deceased person`
                     DIVIDED BY count `the parents`)


§§ `Section 7 rule 6 — brothers and sisters, and the children of deceased brothers or sisters`

-- RULE 6 SAYS "children", NOT "descendants", AND THE DIFFERENCE IS KEPT.
-- Rule 3's Proviso No. (2) reaches "the remotest degree"; rule 6 lets only
-- "the children of any deceased brother or sister" take that sibling's stock,
-- and stops there. So the representation below is ONE GENERATION DEEP and does
-- not recur.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A15 — the grandchild of a predeceased brother. Readings: (i) he
-- takes nothing, rule 6 naming only "children"; (ii) rule 6 imports rule 3's
-- per stirpes machinery wholesale, so representation runs to the remotest
-- degree among collaterals as it does among issue. Taken: (i). The Act uses
-- "descendants" and "the remotest degree" where it means them, in rule 3 and
-- its provisos, and uses "children" here; reading (ii) would make Proviso
-- No. (2)'s express extension redundant. The consequence is real and is not
-- hidden: where every brother and sister is dead and only their grandchildren
-- survive, rule 6's class is empty and the estate falls through to rule 7.

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 6 — "If there are no surviving spouse, descendants or parents"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rule 6 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants or parents` IF
        "no surviving spouse"
    ... NOT `the intestate leaves a surviving spouse` `the deceased person`
    AND "descendants"
    ... NOT `there are descendants` `the deceased person`
    AND "or parents"
    ... null (`the parents of the intestate who survive him` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 6 — "the children of any deceased brother or sister shall take according to their stocks the share which the deceased brother or sister would have taken"; s 3 ("child"); s 6(a)
GIVEN `the brother or sister` IS A `Collateral relative`
      `the date of the death of the intestate` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`rule 6 — the children of a deceased brother or sister who take according to their stocks` MEANS
    map `the person who is that child` (filter `a child of the brother or sister who takes` (`the brother or sister`'s `the relative`'s `children`))
    WHERE
        `a child of the brother or sister who takes` `the relationship of child to parent` MEANS
                `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` `the relationship of child to parent`
            AND `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive`
                    (`the relationship of child to parent`'s `the child`) `the date of the death of the intestate`
            AND `the person survives the intestate`
                    (`the relationship of child to parent`'s `the child`) `the date of the death of the intestate`
        `the person who is that child` `the relationship of child to parent` MEANS
            `the relationship of child to parent`'s `the child`

-- AMBIGUITY A21 — WHAT TRIGGERS REPRESENTATION UNDER RULE 6: DEATH, OR MERELY
-- NOT TAKING. Rule 6 says the word "deceased" three times in one sentence —
-- "children of deceased brothers or sisters", "the children of any deceased
-- brother or sister", "the share which the deceased brother or sister would
-- have taken" — and rule 3 says "in case any of those children be then dead".
-- Both condition representation on DEATH. But since AMBIGUITY A18, a sibling
-- can fail to take for a second reason: he is outside the classes s 6(a)
-- names, being conceived after the intestate's death. Is he then "deceased"
-- for the purposes of this rule?
-- Readings: (i) no — representation runs only from a dead sibling, so a
-- sibling who is alive but outside s 6(a) is neither a taker nor a stock, and
-- his children take nothing; (ii) yes — s 6(a) deems him not to have been in
-- being at the relevant moment, which is the same thing as a predecease, so
-- his children stand in his place exactly as they would if he had died first.
-- Taken: (i), ON THE WORDS. The Act uses "deceased" and "then dead" and uses
-- them repeatedly; reading (ii) requires "deceased" to mean "not entitled",
-- which is a meaning the drafter had no occasion to give it in 1967 and did
-- not.
--
-- IT ALSO RESTORES THE PARITY THAT A18 IS ABOUT. Rule 3's stock test already
-- takes reading (i): a child outside s 6(a) is not a stock at all, and his own
-- descendants take nothing, even though they would take if he had predeceased.
-- An earlier version of THIS decision took reading (ii), so a
-- posthumously-conceived BROTHER's children took the whole estate while a
-- posthumously-conceived CHILD's children, on identical facts, took nothing —
-- the same asymmetry between rules 3 and 6 that A18 records one generation up,
-- and it survived the repair that fixed A18 because no fixture had an excluded
-- sibling WITH children. Case 17e is that fixture.
--
-- The consequence is real and is not hidden: where the only sibling is outside
-- s 6(a), rule 6's class is empty however many children he has, and the estate
-- goes down the ladder to rule 7 and beyond — which is the answer case 17a
-- already gives for an excluded sibling with no children.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 6 — "the brothers and sisters and children of deceased brothers or sisters of the intestate shall share the estate in equal portions between the brothers and sisters"; s 6(a)
GIVEN `the brother or sister` IS A `Collateral relative`
      `the date of the death of the intestate` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rule 6 — this brother or sister shares, or has children who take according to their stocks` IF
        "the brothers and sisters ... shall share the estate in equal portions between the brothers and sisters"
              ... `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a) names`
                      (`the brother or sister`'s `the relative`) `the date of the death of the intestate`
    OR  "and the children of any deceased brother or sister shall take according to their stocks"
              ... (    NOT `the person survives the intestate`
                               (`the brother or sister`'s `the relative`) `the date of the death of the intestate`
                   AND NOT null (`rule 6 — the children of a deceased brother or sister who take according to their stocks`
                                     `the brother or sister` `the date of the death of the intestate`))

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 6; s 6(b)
-- The class of stocks between which the estate is shared "in equal portions",
-- after s 6(b) has put the half blood behind the whole blood. See AMBIGUITY
-- A12 for why the ranking is applied here, to the siblings, and not to the
-- individual takers.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Collateral relative`
`rule 6 — the stocks of brothers and sisters between which the estate is shared in equal portions` MEANS
    `section 6(b) — those of the whole blood, the half blood ranking immediately after them`
        (filter `a brother or sister whose stock takes`
                (`the deceased person`'s `the family`'s `the brothers and sisters of the deceased`))
    WHERE
        `a brother or sister whose stock takes` `the brother or sister` MEANS
            `rule 6 — this brother or sister shares, or has children who take according to their stocks`
                `the brother or sister` (`the date of the death of the intestate` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 6 — "shall take according to their stocks the share which the deceased brother or sister would have taken"
-- THE TEST IN THE `IF` IS REDUNDANT SINCE AMBIGUITY A21, and it is kept for
-- the reason given at `the person survives the intestate and is within the
-- classes section 6(a) names`: this is reached only for a stock that has
-- already qualified, and a stock qualifies either because the sibling is
-- within s 6(a) and survives — in which case the test is true and he takes —
-- or because he is DEAD, in which case it is false and his children take.
-- Reverting it to the bare `the person survives the intestate` now changes no
-- answer. It did before A21 was fixed, which is what case 17e found.
GIVEN `the brother or sister` IS A `Collateral relative`
      `the date of the death of the intestate` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the share of one stock` IS A Share
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`rule 6 — the takers of the share which the brother or sister would have taken` MEANS
    IF   `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a) names`
             (`the brother or sister`'s `the relative`) `the date of the death of the intestate`
    THEN LIST (`the entitlement of` (`the brother or sister`'s `the relative`) `the share of one stock`)
    ELSE map `the entitlement of one child of the deceased brother or sister` `the children who take`
    WHERE
        `the children who take` MEANS
            `rule 6 — the children of a deceased brother or sister who take according to their stocks`
                `the brother or sister` `the date of the death of the intestate`
        `the entitlement of one child of the deceased brother or sister` `the child` MEANS
            `the entitlement of` `the child` (`the share of one stock` DIVIDED BY count `the children who take`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 6
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`rule 6 — the entitlements of the brothers and sisters and the children of deceased brothers or sisters` MEANS
    IF       NOT `rule 6 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants or parents` `the deceased person`
         OR  count `the stocks` EQUALS 0
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE concat (map `the takers of one stock` `the stocks`)
    WHERE
        `the stocks` MEANS
            `rule 6 — the stocks of brothers and sisters between which the estate is shared in equal portions` `the deceased person`
        `the takers of one stock` `the brother or sister` MEANS
            `rule 6 — the takers of the share which the brother or sister would have taken`
                `the brother or sister`
                (`the date of the death of the intestate` `the deceased person`)
                (1 DIVIDED BY count `the stocks`)


§§ `Section 7 rule 7 — the grandparents`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 7 — "If there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rule 7 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate` IF
        "no surviving spouse, descendants, parents"
    ... `rule 6 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants or parents` `the deceased person`
    AND "brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters"
    ... null (`rule 6 — the stocks of brothers and sisters between which the estate is shared in equal portions` `the deceased person`)
    AND "but grandparents of the intestate"
    ... NOT null (`the grandparents of the intestate who survive him` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 7 — "the grandparents shall take the whole of the estate in equal portions."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`rule 7 — the entitlements of the grandparents` MEANS
    IF   NOT `rule 7 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate` `the deceased person`
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE map `the entitlement of one grandparent` `the grandparents`
    WHERE
        `the grandparents` MEANS `the grandparents of the intestate who survive him` `the deceased person`
        `the entitlement of one grandparent` `the grandparent` MEANS
            `the entitlement of` `the grandparent` (1 DIVIDED BY count `the grandparents`)


§§ `Section 7 rule 8 — the uncles and aunts`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 8 — "If there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rule 8 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate` IF
        "no surviving spouse, descendants, parents"
    ... `rule 6 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants or parents` `the deceased person`
    AND "brothers and sisters or their children"
    ... null (`rule 6 — the stocks of brothers and sisters between which the estate is shared in equal portions` `the deceased person`)
    AND "or grandparents"
    ... null (`the grandparents of the intestate who survive him` `the deceased person`)
    AND "but uncles and aunts of the intestate"
    ... NOT null (`the uncles and aunts of the intestate who survive him` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 8 — "the uncles and aunts shall take the whole of the estate in equal portions."; s 6(b)
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`rule 8 — the entitlements of the uncles and aunts` MEANS
    IF   NOT `rule 8 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate` `the deceased person`
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE map `the entitlement of one uncle or aunt` `the uncles and aunts who take`
    WHERE
        -- s 6(b) again: within the third degree, the half blood rank
        -- immediately after the whole blood.
        `the uncles and aunts who take` MEANS
            `section 6(b) — those of the whole blood, the half blood ranking immediately after them`
                (`the uncles and aunts of the intestate who survive him` `the deceased person`)
        `the entitlement of one uncle or aunt` `the relative` MEANS
            `the entitlement of` (`the relative`'s `the relative`)
                                 (1 DIVIDED BY count `the uncles and aunts who take`)


§§ `Section 8 — Special provision if intestate leaves lawful widows`

-- s 8 does not create a share; it DIVIDES the share rules 1, 2 and 4 already
-- gave "the wife of the intestate". So it is encoded as the way the spousal
-- share is paid out, and the arithmetic degenerates correctly where there is
-- only one spouse — a division by one — which is why the Act only needed to
-- speak of "more than one wife". Both arms are written out so that s 8's own
-- antecedent is visible rather than inferred from the arithmetic.
--
-- NO SEX FIELD, AND NONE IS NEEDED: s 8's test is a COUNT ("more than one
-- wife") and its remedy is an equal division among however many there are, so
-- counting the subsisting marriages of a surviving spouse discharges it. That
-- is `family-domain.l4`'s ruling and this module keeps to it.
--
-- SAY THE WIDENING OUT LOUD, THOUGH, because the decision below is named after
-- a word it does not implement. What is counted is SURVIVING SPOUSES OF EITHER
-- SEX, which is wider than s 8's "wife": a deceased woman leaving two
-- surviving husbands satisfies the decision and s 8 does not reach her. The
-- section heading is "Special provision if intestate leaves lawful widows", so
-- the noun is not incidental to the drafting. The widening is inert in
-- Singapore, where no such estate can lawfully arise, and the arithmetic in
-- the widened case is the arithmetic s 8 prescribes for the case it does
-- reach; but a reader should not have to infer any of that from a count.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 8 — "If any person so dying intestate leaves surviving him more than one wife"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 8 — the intestate leaves surviving him more than one wife` IF
    count (`the spouses surviving the intestate` `the deceased person`) GREATER THAN 1

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 8 — "such wives shall share among them equally the share that the wife of the intestate would have been entitled to, had the intestate left only one wife surviving him."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`section 8 — the entitlements of the surviving spouse or spouses` MEANS
    IF   null `the wives`
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE map `the entitlement of one wife` `the wives`
    WHERE
        `the wives` MEANS `the spouses surviving the intestate` `the deceased person`
        -- "the share that the wife of the intestate would have been entitled
        -- to, had the intestate left only one wife surviving him" is exactly
        -- the share rules 1, 2 and 4 compute.
        `the share of a sole wife` MEANS
            `the share of the estate to which the surviving spouse is entitled` `the deceased person`
        `the entitlement of one wife` `the marriage` MEANS
            `the entitlement of` (`the marriage`'s `the spouse`)
                (IF   `section 8 — the intestate leaves surviving him more than one wife` `the deceased person`
                 THEN `the share of a sole wife` DIVIDED BY count `the wives`
                 ELSE `the share of a sole wife`)


§§ `Section 7 rule 9 — the Government`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 9 — "In default of distribution under rules 1 to 8, the Government shall be entitled to the whole of the estate."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rule 9 — in default of distribution under rules 1 to 8` IF
        `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the deceased person`
    AND null (`the distribution under rules 1 to 8` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rule 9 — "the Government shall be entitled to the whole of the estate."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`rule 9 — the entitlement of the Government` MEANS
    IF   `rule 9 — in default of distribution under rules 1 to 8` `the deceased person`
    THEN LIST (Entitlement WITH
                   `the person entitled` IS `the Government`
                   `the fraction of the property distributable under this Act` IS 1)
    ELSE EMPTY


§§ `Section 9 — Children’s advancement not to be taken into account`

-- s 9 STATES A SUBTRACTION THAT IS NOT MADE. It is a rule about how a share is
-- ESTIMATED, and it can only be encoded if the thing it excludes exists in the
-- model to be excluded — which is why `family-domain.l4` carries the
-- advancement as a value on `Person` rather than dropping it. Encoding it as a
-- constant nil is not a way of ignoring the section; the nil IS the section,
-- and case 19 asserts the advancement standing at a positive figure while the
-- amount taken into account is nil.
--
-- THE ANTECEDENT IS COMPUTED, NOT ASKED. "claimed by a child or any descendant
-- of a child of that person" is answered by walking the `children` edge from
-- the intestate, so no new input has to be invented and no case file can say
-- that a stranger is a descendant.
--
-- WHY THE RESULT IS A `MAYBE`. s 9 answers the question only for a child or a
-- descendant of a child. For anyone else the Act says nothing at all: there is
-- no rule of hotchpot anywhere in the ISA, so an encoding that returned the
-- advancement in the other branch would be asserting a rule the Act does not
-- contain, and one that returned nil in both branches would make s 9
-- indistinguishable from silence. `NOTHING` is the honest third answer — this
-- Act does not decide it.

-- s 9's "child" IS s 3's "child", AND AN EARLIER DRAFT CITED s 3 WITHOUT
-- CONSULTING IT. The @ref below claims s 3's definition, and AMBIGUITY A14
-- rules that the definition governs at every generation of a descent; the walk
-- below compared bare person references down the raw `children` edge and never
-- called `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act`, so a person
-- who was not a "child" for rule 3 — which pays him — was a "child" for s 9,
-- which is cited to the definition. Rule 6's helper already applied it to the
-- same kind of walk. It is applied here now, at every generation, as A14 says.
-- Case 18 asserts both sides of it on Ivan and Kiran.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 9 — "Where a distributive share of the property of a person dying intestate is claimed by a child or any descendant of a child of that person"; s 7 rule 3 Proviso No. (1); s 3 — "“child” means a legitimate child and includes any child adopted by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam;"
GIVEN `the intestate` IS A Person
      `the claimant` IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 9 — the distributive share is claimed by a child or any descendant of a child of that person` IF
    any `this child is the claimant, or the claimant descends from him` (`the intestate`'s `children`)
    WHERE
        `this child is the claimant, or the claimant descends from him` `the relationship of child to parent` MEANS
                `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` `the relationship of child to parent`
            AND (   `the relationship of child to parent`'s `the child`'s `the reference for this person`
                        EQUALS `the claimant`'s `the reference for this person`
                 OR `section 9 — the distributive share is claimed by a child or any descendant of a child of that person`
                        (`the relationship of child to parent`'s `the child`) `the claimant`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 9 — "no money or other property which the intestate may during his life have given, paid or settled to or for the advancement of the child by whom or by whose descendant the claim is made shall be taken into account in estimating such distributive share."
GIVEN `the intestate` IS A Person
      `the claimant` IS A Person
GIVETH A MAYBE Money
`section 9 — the money or other property to be taken into account in estimating such distributive share` MEANS
    IF   `section 9 — the distributive share is claimed by a child or any descendant of a child of that person` `the intestate` `the claimant`
    THEN JUST 0
    ELSE NOTHING

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 9; s 5 ("after payment thereout of the expenses of due administration"); s 7 rules 1-8
-- The conversion from a fraction to dollars, which is where s 9 bites: the
-- estimate of the distributive share is the fraction of the whole, with
-- nothing subtracted for what the intestate gave the claimant in his lifetime.
GIVEN `the intestate` IS A Person
      `the claimant` IS A Person
      `the fraction of the property distributable under this Act` IS A Share
      `the property of which the share is a fraction` IS A Money
GIVETH A MAYBE Money
`section 9 — the distributive share of a child or a descendant of a child, estimated` MEANS
    CONSIDER `section 9 — the money or other property to be taken into account in estimating such distributive share` `the intestate` `the claimant`
    WHEN NOTHING                   THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `the advancement`    THEN
        JUST ((`the fraction of the property distributable under this Act` TIMES `the property of which the share is a fraction`)
                  MINUS `the advancement`)


§§ `Section 10 — Application to cases of partial intestacy`

-- s 10 does two things: it CONFINES the Act to the undisposed part, and its
-- proviso makes the personal representative a trustee of that part.
--
-- "subject to the provisions contained in the will" is a deferral to a
-- document this module does not construe, and is named in the closing section.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A16 — where the expenses of due administration fall in a partial
-- intestacy. s 5 takes the fund "after payment thereout of the expenses of due
-- administration"; s 10 gives this Act "the part of his property not so
-- disposed of". Neither says whether the expenses are borne by the undisposed
-- part alone or rateably by the whole estate. Readings: (i) wholly by the
-- undisposed part; (ii) rateably between the disposed and undisposed parts.
-- Taken: (i) — the ISA defines its phrase by pointing at the Probate and
-- Administration Act 1934, whose Second Schedule applies "Property of the
-- deceased undisposed of by will" first. The two readings give different
-- figures whenever a will disposes of part, so this is a live fork and not a
-- formality.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A20 — the SITUS of "the part of his property not so disposed of",
-- which is a second and independent fork in the same subtraction. s 10 gives
-- this Act "the part of his property not so disposed of" and says nothing
-- about where that property is; s 5 reaches only property "situated in
-- Singapore", in both of its limbs. So where a will beneficially disposes of
-- FOREIGN property, the quantity subtracted below is unconfined by situs while
-- the fund it comes off is confined to Singapore, and the whole disposed value
-- reduces the Singapore fund pound for pound although foreign assets stand
-- available to satisfy it.
-- Readings: (i) subtract the whole value beneficially disposed of from the
-- Singapore fund; (ii) subtract only so much of it as falls on Singapore
-- property. Taken: (i) — and taken for want of an alternative rather than on
-- the words, which is why it is recorded here rather than argued. `Disposition`
-- in `family-domain.l4` carries a donee, a kind and a value and NO situs, so
-- the model cannot say out of which property a legacy is satisfied and reading
-- (ii) is not expressible in it at all. That is the same shape of limit as
-- AMBIGUITY A17 records about the trustee cell, and it is recorded for the
-- same reason: the choice is being made in the code either way, and a reader
-- who cannot see it cannot disagree with it. Reading (i) can only UNDER-state
-- the fund, never over-state it, which is the same allocation of the burden as
-- AMBIGUITY A5, A7 and A17. Case 15c exhibits the gap on facts that differ
-- from case 15 by one field. If a later ontology gives a disposition a situs,
-- `section 10 — the part of his property not so disposed of` is the line that
-- changes.
--
-- Note that A20 is NOT A16 read twice. A16 asks which part bears the EXPENSES;
-- A20 asks which part bears the DISPOSITION. They are different subtractions
-- and either could be decided the other way without touching the other.

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 10 — "Where any person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property"
GIVEN `the will` IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the will makes some beneficial disposition` IF
    any `a beneficial disposition` (`the will`'s `the dispositions made by the will`)
    WHERE
        `a beneficial disposition` `the disposition` MEANS `the disposition`'s `beneficial`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 10 — "Where any person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 10 — the person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property` IF
    CONSIDER `the deceased person`'s `the will`
    WHEN NOTHING       THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST `a will` THEN     `the will makes some beneficial disposition` `a will`
                            AND NOT `the will beneficially disposes of the whole of his property` `a will`

-- "so disposed of" POINTS BACK AT THE SAME DISPOSITIONS THAT ANSWER THE
-- ANTECEDENT, AND AN EARLIER DRAFT UNTIED THEM. It read the KIND of
-- disposition off `Will`'s `the dispositions made by the will` — which is what
-- decides whether the will disposes of the whole, of part, or of no part — and
-- the QUANTITY off `Estate`'s `the value of the property beneficially disposed
-- of by the will`, with nothing comparing the two. A case file could then hold
-- at once that the will disposed of part and that the fund was the whole,
-- distributing the same legacy twice. `family-domain.l4` says of that cell
-- that "The two must agree, and intestate-succession-act.l4 is where they are
-- made to": these next two definitions are that promise discharged.
--
-- THE QUANTITY IS COMPUTED FROM THE DISPOSITIONS, not read off the estate,
-- because s 10's word "so" ties it to them. The estate's own cell is not
-- thereby made dead — it is compared, by
-- `the value beneficially disposed of by the will recorded in the estate
-- agrees with the dispositions made by the will`, and case 22 exhibits a file
-- where it does not.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 10 — "a will beneficially disposing of part of his property ... the part of his property not so disposed of"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`section 10 — the value of the part of his property so disposed of` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the deceased person`'s `the will`
    WHEN NOTHING       THEN 0
    WHEN JUST `a will` THEN sum (map `the value of a beneficial disposition`
                                     (filter `a beneficial disposition` (`a will`'s `the dispositions made by the will`)))
    WHERE
        `a beneficial disposition` `the disposition` MEANS `the disposition`'s `beneficial`
        `the value of a beneficial disposition` `the disposition` MEANS `the disposition`'s `the value of the disposition`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 10; Probate and Administration Act 1934 Second Schedule para 1 — "Property of the deceased undisposed of by will"
-- Not a rule of the Act: a consistency check on the case file, so that a
-- divergence between the will's own dispositions and the estate's recorded
-- disposed value is visible instead of silently resolved.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the value beneficially disposed of by the will recorded in the estate agrees with the dispositions made by the will` IF
        `the deceased person`'s `the estate`'s `the value of the property beneficially disposed of by the will`
    EQUALS `section 10 — the value of the part of his property so disposed of` `the deceased person`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 10 — "the provisions of this Act shall have effect as respects the part of his property not so disposed of"; s 5
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`section 10 — the part of his property not so disposed of` MEANS
    IF   `section 10 — the person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property` `the deceased person`
    THEN max 0 (   `section 5 — the property to be distributed, after payment thereout of the expenses of due administration` `the deceased person`
             MINUS `section 10 — the value of the part of his property so disposed of` `the deceased person`)
    ELSE `section 5 — the property to be distributed, after payment thereout of the expenses of due administration` `the deceased person`

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 10 proviso — "unless it appears by the will that the personal representative is entitled to take that part beneficially"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it appears by the will that the personal representative is entitled to take that part beneficially` IF
    CONSIDER `the deceased person`'s `the will`
    WHEN NOTHING       THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST `a will` THEN `a will`'s `it appears by the will that the personal representative is entitled to take that part beneficially`

-- THE PROVISO IS A "shall ... unless", AND IT IS ENCODED THAT WAY ROUND. The
-- trusteeship is the default and the will's contrary provision is the
-- exception; drafting it as "is a trustee if the will does not say otherwise"
-- would move the burden.
--
-- "subject to his rights and powers for the purposes of administration" is NOT
-- encoded: those rights and powers are the Probate and Administration Act
-- 1934's, and they have no determinate output here. Named in the closing
-- section.
--
-- s 10's body says "not so disposed of" (i.e. not beneficially disposed of)
-- while the proviso says "not expressly disposed of". The two phrases are not
-- word-for-word the same, and property disposed of by implication would fall
-- differently under each. The proviso is encoded on the body's antecedent,
-- which is the reading that keeps the section internally consistent; the
-- difference is flagged here rather than smoothed away.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 10 proviso — "Provided that the personal representative shall, subject to his rights and powers for the purposes of administration, be a trustee for the persons entitled under this Act in respect of the part of the estate not expressly disposed of unless it appears by the will that the personal representative is entitled to take that part beneficially."
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 10 proviso — the personal representative is a trustee for the persons entitled under this Act in respect of the part of the estate not expressly disposed of` IF
        "Provided that the personal representative shall ... be a trustee for the persons entitled under this Act in respect of the part of the estate not expressly disposed of"
    ... `section 10 — the person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property` `the deceased person`
    AND "unless it appears by the will that the personal representative is entitled to take that part beneficially"
    ... NOT `it appears by the will that the personal representative is entitled to take that part beneficially` `the deceased person`


§§ `The Act as a whole — the gate, the fund, and the distribution`

-- THE GATE. Three things have to hold before a single fraction is computed:
-- s 2 (this is not the estate of a Muslim), s 5's commencement and intestacy
-- limb, and one of s 5's two situs-and-domicile limbs. Where any of them
-- fails, the distribution is EMPTY — and, in particular, rule 9 does NOT fire,
-- because rule 9 is a rule of this Act and s 2 says nothing in this Act
-- applies. A Muslim's estate does not escheat to the Government by way of an
-- Act that does not apply to it.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 2; s 3 ("intestate"); s 5
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` IF
        `this Act applies to the estate` `the deceased person`
    AND `section 5 — the person dies intestate after 2 June 1967` `the deceased person`
    AND (   `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `the deceased person`
         OR `section 5(b) — domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore` `the deceased person`)

-- THE WHOLE. Every fraction s 7 computes is a fraction of THIS: s 5's property
-- less the expenses of due administration, less (under s 10) any part the will
-- beneficially disposed of. Nil where the Act has no effect at all.
@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5 — "after payment thereout of the expenses of due administration as prescribed by the Probate and Administration Act 1934"; s 10 — "as respects the part of his property not so disposed of"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Money
`the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` MEANS
    IF   `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the deceased person`
    THEN `section 10 — the part of his property not so disposed of` `the deceased person`
    ELSE 0

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rules 1-8; s 8
-- The order of the concatenation is the order in which s 7 first names each
-- class, and it is presentational only: the shares are computed independently
-- and their sum is checked, not assumed. Rule 4's parental half is NOT a
-- member, because rule 5 states the same quantity in wider words and paying
-- both would double-count; see the note at rule 5.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`the distribution under rules 1 to 8` MEANS
    IF   NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `the deceased person`
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE concat
             (LIST `section 8 — the entitlements of the surviving spouse or spouses` `the deceased person`,
                   `rule 3 — the entitlements of the issue` `the deceased person`,
                   `rule 5 — the entitlements of the parent or parents` `the deceased person`,
                   `rule 6 — the entitlements of the brothers and sisters and the children of deceased brothers or sisters` `the deceased person`,
                   `rule 7 — the entitlements of the grandparents` `the deceased person`,
                   `rule 8 — the entitlements of the uncles and aunts` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5 — "shall be distributed among the persons entitled to succeed beneficially to that property or the proceeds thereof."; s 7 — "In effecting such distribution, the following rules shall be observed:"
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` MEANS
    append (`the distribution under rules 1 to 8` `the deceased person`)
           (`rule 9 — the entitlement of the Government` `the deceased person`)


§§ `Reading the answer`

-- Presentation only, and the only place in this module where a string literal
-- is NOT a quotation of the Act: " takes ", "the Public Trustee" and the
-- corporation's name below are display text. ("the Government" happens to be
-- rule 9's own word, but it is used here as a label too.) `Entitlement`
-- carries a `Person or corporation`, so the name of a taker has to be got out
-- of the sum type; rule 9's taker is not a human being at all.
GIVEN `the person entitled` IS A `Person or corporation`
GIVETH A STRING
`the name of the person entitled` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the person entitled`
    WHEN `an individual` `the individual` THEN `the individual`'s `name`
    WHEN `the Public Trustee`             THEN "the Public Trustee"
    WHEN `a corporation licensed as a trust company under the Trust Companies Act 2005` `the name of the corporation`
                                          THEN `the name of the corporation`
    WHEN `the Government`                 THEN "the Government"

GIVEN `the entitlement` IS AN Entitlement
GIVETH A STRING
`who takes what` MEANS
    CONCAT (`the name of the person entitled` (`the entitlement`'s `the person entitled`)),
           " takes ",
           TOSTRING (`the entitlement`'s `the fraction of the property distributable under this Act`)

GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the distribution, read out` MEANS
    map `who takes what` (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `the deceased person`)

-- The sum of every share. It should be 1 wherever this Act has effect, and 0
-- where it does not. Nothing in the Act says so in terms — it is the check
-- that the nine rules together exhaust the estate, and it is computed rather
-- than assumed.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A Share
`the total of the shares distributed` MEANS
    sum (map `the fraction taken`
             (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `the deceased person`))
    WHERE
        `the fraction taken` `the entitlement` MEANS
            `the entitlement`'s `the fraction of the property distributable under this Act`


§§ `Joins with the other three Acts`

-- WHY THIS SECTION EXISTS. Read one at a time, each of the four modules in this
-- cleanroom documents its own boundary well and its neighbours' badly, and the
-- consequence is a corpus in which one Act writes a hand-off contract that
-- nothing on the other side executes. Of the five seams below this module spoke
-- at ONE — s 5's pointer at the Probate and Administration Act — and was silent
-- about its neighbours at the other four. All five are now set out from this
-- Act's side: what the other module does, what this one does, and why.
--
-- WHAT IS SAID HERE ABOUT ANOTHER MODULE WAS MEASURED, NOT REMEMBERED. Every
-- claim below about `wills-act.l4`, `probate-administration-act.l4` or
-- `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` was re-read in that file, and every
-- cross-module number was produced by importing the modules into one probe and
-- running it, on 2026-08-26. That matters more than usual here, because the
-- four files are being repaired in parallel: two of the seams below were audited
-- as broken and had been fixed on the other side by the time this was written,
-- and the audit's own wording is preserved as history rather than restated as
-- fact.
--
-- NOTHING BELOW IS A RULE OF THIS ACT. Three definitions are added here: the
-- two joins — SEAM 3's and SEAM 4's — and one helper they share. None is named
-- after a provision, because none answers one, and both joins exist for the same
-- reason: a fact this Act can compute, which another Act needs and currently
-- takes as a sworn input. They are also the only decisions in this file that
-- carry an `@ref` to a section of another Act as their SUBJECT rather than as a
-- cross-reference.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SEAM 1. ISA s 3 "child"  <->  WA s 26 and PAA s 18(4)(a).
--                        AUDITED AS DISAGREEING; RE-MEASURED AS AGREEING.
--   Argued in full at s 3 above, with the measurement. The Wills Act declines
--   this Act's definition, because s 26 stands on its own words, and is right
--   to. The Probate and Administration Act takes it, because its "next of kin"
--   has been construed by pointing at this Act's classes, and is right to. A
--   cross-module audit found that second module borrowing the classes and not
--   applying the definition — it opened a grant to a man this Act pays nothing
--   — and that has since been repaired there; re-measured on 2026-08-26 the two
--   answer alike on the same `Deceased person`. This Act did not move and does
--   not need to. What remains is that one definition is now written in two
--   files with nothing making them agree: reported as a domain gap.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SEAM 2. ISA s 5 "the expenses of due administration as prescribed by the
--   Probate and Administration Act 1934"  <->  PAA ss 57-58.       BOTH SPEAK,
--   NEITHER CAN ANSWER. Argued at s 5 above. This Act names the other Act in
--   its own text; that module puts ss 57-58 and both Schedules out of scope and
--   says so against this pointer by name. So the figure is an input everywhere,
--   and four ontology fields declared for the join have no reader in any of the
--   four modules. Reported as a domain gap.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SEAM 3. GIA s 17 (no good discharge for an infant's legacy)  <->  what this
--   Act makes an infant ENTITLED to.        THIS ACT WAS SILENT. NOW IT IS NOT.
--
--   THE STATUTE FIRST, because it is the stable thing. GIA s 17: "A guardian of
--   the property of an infant shall not, unless in any case the court otherwise
--   orders, be empowered to give a good discharge for any legacy or other
--   capital moneys payable to or receivable by an infant." An infant's share of
--   an intestate estate is capital moneys receivable by an infant, so s 17
--   stands between a share this Act awards and that share being validly paid
--   over.
--
--   `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` states this seam from its side, calls s 17
--   its Act's direct bite on succession, points at this module's
--   `the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` as the
--   thing s 17 bites on, and records that of its four joins this is the one
--   whose pointer runs in one direction only. (Paraphrased rather than quoted:
--   that file is being edited in parallel with this one, and a verbatim
--   quotation of a moving neighbour is a stale claim waiting to happen. The
--   statutory words above are quoted, and they do not move.)
--
--   THE ONE-DIRECTION COMPLAINT WAS EXACTLY TRUE OF THIS FILE, and exactly is
--   the word: in the 3,848 lines this file had before this section was written,
--   "guardian" and "infant" occurred TWICE, both times as a fixture writing
--   EMPTY into a field of the shared ontology, and never in a rule or a comment.
--   So a reader could not tell whether the payability constraint had been
--   considered and left to another Act or simply not noticed. This section, and
--   the decision below it, are the pointer running back.
--
--   IT WAS CONSIDERED, AND IT IS NOT THIS ACT'S. The Intestate Succession Act
--   1967 fixes who succeeds beneficially and in what fraction and stops there:
--   s 5 ends at "shall be distributed among the persons entitled to succeed
--   beneficially", and nothing in ss 1-10 says to whom the money may be handed,
--   or when, or on whose receipt. GIA s 17 answers that, and the Probate and
--   Administration Act answers who does the handing. An encoding here that
--   withheld a share from an infant would be adding a rule this Act does not
--   contain.
--
--   BUT THE FACT IS READABLE FROM HERE, AND SO IT IS READ. `Deceased person`
--   carries `the infants concerned in the estate`, and each `Infant` carries a
--   `Person` whose `the reference for this person` is the same identity this
--   Act's takers carry. So this module can say which of the shares it computes
--   are shares of an infant concerned in the estate — which is exactly the set
--   GIA s 17 governs the payment of — without deciding anything s 17 decides.
--   `the entitlements under this Act taken by an infant concerned in the estate`
--   below is that, and case 40 asserts it on both sides.
--
--   THE OTHER MODULE CALLS THIS THE ONE JOIN OF ITS FOUR THAT IS NOT EVEN
--   TYPED, and it is right about the reason and half right about the
--   consequence. `family-domain.l4` gives
--   no field the type `Entitlement`, so an entitlement is computed here and
--   never stored, and nothing carries the OBJECT from this module to that one.
--   What does carry is the IDENTITY: both modules read `the reference for this
--   person`, and both read `Deceased person`'s `the infants concerned in the
--   estate`, so a single case file can be put to both. Measured on case 40,
--   with the two modules imported into one file: this Act answers "Amirah takes
--   0.5" and the Guardianship of Infants Act answers that her guardian has no
--   power to give a good discharge for it. Storing the entitlement would make
--   the join a computation instead of a pairing, and is reported as a domain
--   gap; until then, the pairing works and is asserted.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SEAM 4. PAA s 29(5) "the administrator is entitled to the whole of the estate
--   after payment of the debts"  <->  the `Entitlement` list this Act computes.
--                                            THIS ACT WAS SILENT. NOW IT IS NOT.
--
--   `probate-administration-act.l4` reports this one against itself, as a
--   DOMAIN GAP beside its s 29(5): "`Entitlement` is declared in
--   `family-domain.l4` and no field anywhere has its type, so no route runs from
--   `Deceased person` to who is entitled to what. Until one does, s 29(5)'s
--   antecedent arrives here as a proved fact rather than as a computation over
--   the Intestate Succession Act's answer." It names this module as the side
--   that does compute it, and spells out the cost: "a case file may swear this
--   boolean TRUE while that Act's rules give the administrator half, and nothing
--   in this cleanroom would object". Right on both counts, and until now nothing
--   here said so — one fact travelling under two names, with the pointer running
--   in one direction only.
--
--   WHAT THIS MODULE CANNOT DO: give `Deceased person` a field of type
--   `Entitlement`. `family-domain.l4` is shared and three other modules are
--   relying on it holding still, so that is reported as a domain gap and not
--   done here. WHAT IT CAN DO: compute its own half of s 29(5)'s antecedent and
--   name it, so that the two answers are comparable instead of merely
--   coexisting. The half this Act owns is "entitled to the whole of the estate";
--   the half it does not own is "after payment of the debts", which is PAA s 57
--   and is deducted nowhere in this module — see SEAM 2 and the note at s 5. So
--   the decision below is deliberately NOT named after s 29(5), and a case file
--   that finds the two disagreeing has learnt something rather than caught a bug
--   in either.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SEAM 5. The age of majority.                     THIS ACT NEVER ASKS FOR IT.
--
--   `Person` carries `the date on which the person attains the age of majority`
--   and this module writes NOTHING into it in every fixture and reads it
--   nowhere. That is a decision and not an omission, so it is recorded rather
--   than left to look like one. The Intestate Succession Act 1967 fixes shares
--   by relationship and survivorship, and no rule in s 7 turns on how old a
--   taker is: a minor takes exactly what an adult in the same degree takes. The
--   check is stronger than an argument from the rules, and it was run — the
--   string "age" does not occur anywhere in source/ISA1967.txt at all, not in
--   ss 1-10, not in the arrangement of sections, not even inside another word.
--   There is therefore nothing here for an age to answer, and writing a date
--   into the field would invite a reader to think some rule below consults it.
--
--   WHAT THE OTHER THREE DO WITH THE SAME QUESTION, so that a reader meeting
--   three answers does not take them for three accidents:
--     `wills-act.l4` computes its OWN and must not read the shared field. WA
--       s 4 states a number in the Act itself — "No will made by any person
--       under 21 years of age shall be valid" — and s 27(1) disapplies it for a
--       soldier or a mariner, so the wills module compares `date of birth`
--       against the twenty-first anniversary of it. `family-domain.l4` says so
--       at the field: the WA "is different and must NOT read this field".
--     `probate-administration-act.l4` reads the shared field and names no
--       number, across s 6(2), s 21, s 29(8)(a) and s 63(1). Where the date is
--       not in evidence it folds the answer to FALSE.
--     `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4` reads the shared field too, and offers a
--       second route whose `the age of majority, in years` comes from its
--       CALLER, because its own Act supplies no age of majority anywhere. That
--       is checkable from the source and was checked: source/GIA1934.txt names
--       an age exactly once, at s 11(b) — "where the infant is of an age to
--       express an independent opinion" — which is a capacity and not majority.
--       Where the majority date is not in evidence that module yields NOTHING
--       rather than FALSE, and the Probate Act module records why it cannot use
--       that shape: "a gate has to open or close".
--   So the corpus does not answer the age two ways: the Wills Act's 21 is its
--   own Act's, walled off deliberately, and the other two defer to one already-
--   construed field. The three modules differ only on the SILENT case, and this
--   Act has no view on it because it never asks.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 7 rules 1-9, s 8; family-domain.l4 `Person or corporation`
-- Not a rule: the identity of a taker, where the taker is a human being. Rule
-- 9's Government and the other two corporate members of `Person or corporation`
-- have no `the reference for this person` and cannot be an infant, so the
-- answer for them is NOTHING rather than a manufactured string.
GIVEN `the person entitled` IS A `Person or corporation`
GIVETH A MAYBE STRING
`the reference for the person entitled` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the person entitled`
    WHEN `an individual` `the individual` THEN JUST (`the individual`'s `the reference for this person`)
    WHEN `the Public Trustee`             THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `a corporation licensed as a trust company under the Trust Companies Act 2005` `the name of the corporation`
                                          THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `the Government`                 THEN NOTHING

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 17 — "A guardian of the property of an infant shall not, unless in any case the court otherwise orders, be empowered to give a good discharge for any legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by an infant."; Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5 — "shall be distributed among the persons entitled to succeed beneficially"
-- SEAM 3, read from this side. NOT A RULE OF THIS ACT, and named so that it
-- cannot be taken for one: it decides nothing about the discharge, the guardian
-- or the court, all of which are GIA s 17's. It says only which of the
-- entitlements this Act computes belong to a person the case file records as an
-- infant concerned in the estate — the set whose payment s 17 governs.
--
-- IDENTITY IS BY REFERENCE, NOT BY NAME. `family-domain.l4`'s note on
-- `the reference for this person` rules that equality of that field is identity
-- and that "`name` cannot do the work — two people in one estate may share
-- one"; the same child appears here
-- as a `Person` inside the `children` tree and there as an `Infant` in the
-- deceased's list, and the reference is what says they are the same child.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF Entitlement
`the entitlements under this Act taken by an infant concerned in the estate` MEANS
    filter `taken by an infant concerned in the estate`
           (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `the deceased person`)
    WHERE
        `the references of the infants concerned in the estate` MEANS
            map (GIVEN `the infant` YIELD `the infant`'s `the infant`'s `the reference for this person`)
                (`the deceased person`'s `the infants concerned in the estate`)
        `taken by an infant concerned in the estate` `the entitlement` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the reference for the person entitled` (`the entitlement`'s `the person entitled`)
            WHEN NOTHING              THEN FALSE
            WHEN JUST `the reference` THEN elem `the reference` `the references of the infants concerned in the estate`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 17
-- Presentation only, in the same shape as `the distribution, read out`.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the shares taken by an infant concerned in the estate, read out` MEANS
    map `who takes what` (`the entitlements under this Act taken by an infant concerned in the estate` `the deceased person`)

@ref Intestate Succession Act 1967 s 5, s 7 rules 1-9, s 8; cf Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 29(5) — "When the administrator is entitled to the whole of the estate after payment of the debts, sureties in the bond may ordinarily be dispensed with."
-- SEAM 4, read from this side. NOT A RULE OF THIS ACT and NOT s 29(5): it is
-- this Act's half of s 29(5)'s antecedent, offered so that the sworn boolean
-- `probate-administration-act.l4` declares for that subsection has something to
-- be checked against. The other half — "after payment of the debts" — is PAA
-- s 57's and is not computed anywhere in this cleanroom, so a case file in
-- which the debts exhaust the estate can satisfy this decision and not s 29(5).
-- Nor does it ask whether the sole taker is the administrator: who obtained the
-- grant is the Probate and Administration Act's question, over `the grants made
-- in respect of the estate`, and this Act never looks at a grant.
GIVEN `the deceased person` IS A `Deceased person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act` IF
        count (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `the deceased person`) EQUALS 1
    AND `the total of the shares distributed` `the deceased person` EQUALS 1


§§ `Worked facts — the fixtures`

-- FIXTURES, NOT LAW. Nothing below states a rule; each definition builds a
-- record for the assertions in the next section. Every field the ontology requires
-- but this Act never reads is filled with a neutral value.
--
-- THE PERSONAL NAMES IN THE NEXT TWO SECTIONS ARE INVENTED. "Aaron",
-- "Beatrice", "Clara" and the rest are fixture labels, not quotations: the
-- Intestate Succession Act 1967 names no person anywhere.
--
-- WHERE THE `ISA` TAG COMES FROM AND WHAT IT MEANS. `an ISA person`,
-- `the ISA Rahim` and the twelve others carrying it are the fixture names that
-- another module in this corpus had also taken, which made the four Act modules
-- impossible to import together; the tag is the repair, and the module header
-- records the measurement and the convention. It marks the identifier only. The
-- `name` string a fixture carries is untagged and unchanged — `the ISA Rahim`
-- is the binding, "Rahim" is the man — which is why the rename moved no answer.
--
-- `the date on which the person attains the age of majority` IS WRITTEN NOTHING
-- BELOW, AND THAT IS SEAM 5. No rule in this Act asks anyone's age; the word
-- does not appear in ss 1-10. The Probate and Administration Act and the
-- Guardianship of Infants Act both read that field and this one deliberately
-- does not, so a positive date here would be a fact about another Act's
-- question sitting in this Act's fixtures. See `Joins with the other three
-- Acts` above.

GIVEN `the name` IS A STRING
      `his vital status` IS A `Vital status`
      `his date of birth` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `conceived in the womb at the death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the advancement` IS A Money
      `his children` IS A LIST OF Child
GIVETH A Person
`an ISA person` MEANS Person WITH
    `name`                            IS `the name`
    `the reference for this person`   IS `the name`
    `date of birth`                   IS `his date of birth`
    `vital status`                    IS `his vital status`
    `children`                        IS `his children`
    `at the date of the death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` IS `conceived in the womb at the death`
    `money or other property given, paid or settled by the intestate during his life to or for the advancement of the person` IS `the advancement`
    `the date on which the person attains the age of majority` IS NOTHING
    `a soldier being in actual military service` IS FALSE
    `a mariner or seaman being at sea` IS FALSE
    `a member of any naval or marine forces so circumstanced that if he were a soldier he would be in actual military service` IS FALSE
    `so desires that letters of administration be granted to such person as the court thinks fit` IS FALSE
    `lacks mental capacity (within the meaning of the Mental Capacity Act 2008) to make decisions with respect to the management and administration of the person's property and affairs` IS FALSE
    `absent from Singapore`           IS FALSE
    `willing to act`                  IS TRUE

GIVEN `the name` IS A STRING
GIVETH A Person
`living, childless` MEANS `an ISA person` `the name` `living` NOTHING FALSE 0 EMPTY

GIVEN `the name` IS A STRING
      `his children` IS A LIST OF Child
GIVETH A Person
`living, with children` MEANS `an ISA person` `the name` `living` NOTHING FALSE 0 `his children`

GIVEN `the name` IS A STRING
      `the date of his death` IS A DATE
      `his children` IS A LIST OF Child
GIVETH A Person
`who died on` MEANS `an ISA person` `the name` (`died on` `the date of his death`) NOTHING FALSE 0 `his children`

GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
GIVETH A Child
`an ISA legitimate child` MEANS Child WITH
    `the child` IS `the person`
    `a legitimate child of the person whose child he is` IS TRUE
    `adopted by that person by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam` IS FALSE

GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
GIVETH A Child
`a child adopted by order of court in Singapore` MEANS Child WITH
    `the child` IS `the person`
    `a legitimate child of the person whose child he is` IS FALSE
    `adopted by that person by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam` IS TRUE

GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
GIVETH A Child
`a child neither legitimate nor adopted` MEANS Child WITH
    `the child` IS `the person`
    `a legitimate child of the person whose child he is` IS FALSE
    `adopted by that person by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam` IS FALSE

GIVEN `the husband or wife` IS A Person
GIVETH A Marriage
`a subsisting marriage to` MEANS Marriage WITH
    `the spouse` IS `the husband or wife`
    `the date of the solemnisation of the marriage` IS NOTHING
    `subsisting at the death of the deceased, in the sense of not dissolved or annulled before the death` IS TRUE

GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
GIVETH A `Collateral relative`
`of the whole blood,` MEANS `Collateral relative` WITH
    `the relative` IS `the person`
    `of the whole blood or of the half blood` IS `of the whole blood`

GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
GIVETH A `Collateral relative`
`of the half blood,` MEANS `Collateral relative` WITH
    `the relative` IS `the person`
    `of the whole blood or of the half blood` IS `of the half blood`

GIVEN `the marriages` IS A LIST OF Marriage
      `the father` IS A MAYBE Person
      `the mother` IS A MAYBE Person
      `the brothers and sisters` IS A LIST OF `Collateral relative`
      `the grandparents` IS A LIST OF Person
      `the uncles and aunts` IS A LIST OF `Collateral relative`
GIVETH A Family
`an ISA family` MEANS Family WITH
    `the marriages of the deceased`            IS `the marriages`
    `the father of the deceased`               IS `the father`
    `the mother of the deceased`               IS `the mother`
    `the brothers and sisters of the deceased` IS `the brothers and sisters`
    `the grandparents of the deceased`         IS `the grandparents`
    `the uncles and aunts of the deceased`     IS `the uncles and aunts`

`no family in evidence` MEANS `an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY

GIVEN `movable in Singapore` IS A Money
      `immovable in Singapore` IS A Money
      `movable outside Singapore` IS A Money
      `immovable outside Singapore` IS A Money
      `the expenses of due administration` IS A Money
      `the value disposed of by the will` IS A Money
      `held as trustee and not beneficially, in Singapore` IS A Money
      `the debts and liabilities` IS A Money
GIVETH AN Estate
`an ISA estate` MEANS Estate WITH
    `the value of the movable property situated in Singapore`        IS `movable in Singapore`
    `the value of the immovable property situated in Singapore`      IS `immovable in Singapore`
    `the value of the movable property situated outside Singapore`   IS `movable outside Singapore`
    `the value of the immovable property situated outside Singapore` IS `immovable outside Singapore`
    `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore` IS `held as trustee and not beneficially, in Singapore`
    `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated outside Singapore` IS 0
    `the value of any moneys payable by an appointed insurer pursuant to the Dependants’ Protection Insurance Scheme or any other equivalent scheme maintained by the Central Provident Fund Board` IS 0
    `the value of the property administered by the Public Trustee`   IS 0
    `the amount of the funds in court directed to be paid, transferred or delivered to him` IS 0
    `the value of land settled previously to the death of the deceased` IS 0
    `the trustees of the settlement`                                 IS EMPTY
    `the value of the interest in property which at the time of his death is charged with the payment of money` IS 0
    `the amount of the charge on that interest`                      IS 0
    `the deceased has by will, deed or other document signified a contrary or other intention` IS FALSE
    `the debts and liabilities of the deceased`                      IS `the debts and liabilities`
    `the funeral, testamentary and administration expenses`          IS `the expenses of due administration`
    `the creditors of the deceased`                                  IS EMPTY
    `fully administered`                                             IS FALSE
    `the value of the property beneficially disposed of by the will` IS `the value disposed of by the will`

GIVEN `the taker` IS A Person
      `the kind` IS A `Kind of disposition`
      `the value` IS A Money
GIVETH A Disposition
`a beneficial gift` MEANS Disposition WITH
    `the donee`                    IS `an individual` `the taker`
    `the kind of disposition`      IS `the kind`
    `the value of the disposition` IS `the value`
    `beneficial`                   IS TRUE
    `not determinable at or before the death of the donee` IS TRUE

-- The same shape with `beneficial` FALSE, so that a will can carry a
-- disposition s 3 and s 10 do not count. Both sections turn on a BENEFICIAL
-- disposition, and the filter in `section 10 — the value of the part of his
-- property so disposed of` is what applies that word to the quantity.
GIVEN `the taker` IS A Person
      `the kind` IS A `Kind of disposition`
      `the value` IS A Money
GIVETH A Disposition
`a disposition that is not beneficial` MEANS Disposition WITH
    `the donee`                    IS `an individual` `the taker`
    `the kind of disposition`      IS `the kind`
    `the value of the disposition` IS `the value`
    `beneficial`                   IS FALSE
    `not determinable at or before the death of the donee` IS TRUE

GIVEN `the maker of the will` IS A Person
      `the dispositions` IS A LIST OF Disposition
      `the personal representative may take the undisposed part beneficially` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Will
`a duly executed will` MEANS Will WITH
    `the testator`                                     IS `the maker of the will`
    `the date of execution`                            IS NOTHING
    `in writing`                                       IS TRUE
    `signed at the foot or end thereof by the testator` IS TRUE
    `signed at the foot or end thereof by some other person in his presence and by his direction` IS FALSE
    `the signature made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time` IS TRUE
    `apparent on the face of the will that the testator intended to give effect by such his signature to the writing signed as his will` IS TRUE
    `the attesting witnesses`                          IS EMPTY
    `the executors appointed by the will`              IS EMPTY
    `the guardians of infant children appointed by the will` IS EMPTY
    `the dispositions made by the will`                IS `the dispositions`
    `expressed to be made in contemplation of a marriage` IS FALSE
    `the reference for the person with whom the marriage contemplated was to be solemnised` IS NOTHING
    `made in exercise of a power of appointment`       IS FALSE
    `the estate thereby appointed would not in default of such appointment pass to his or her heir, executor or administrator or the person entitled under the Intestate Succession Act 1967` IS FALSE
    `the life interests arising under the will`        IS EMPTY
    `it appears by the will that the personal representative is entitled to take that part beneficially` IS `the personal representative may take the undisposed part beneficially`
    `the codicils`                                     IS EMPTY

-- THE INFANTS ARE A PARAMETER, AND UNTIL NOW THEY WERE A CONSTANT. This builder
-- wrote `the infants concerned in the estate` IS EMPTY with no way for a case to
-- say otherwise. A cross-module audit found the same shape in the other three
-- files — `wills-act.l4`'s deceased-person builder hardcoding the same field,
-- `probate-administration-act.l4`'s infant builder hardcoding `the guardians of
-- the infant` IS EMPTY while its own fixtures asserted grants made in a
-- guardian's capacity — and named the consequence: every join in the corpus was
-- TYPED and none was EXERCISED, because each module zeroed out the other three's
-- facts, so no assertion anywhere could disagree with a neighbour. Those three
-- have since been parameterised in their own files (measured 2026-08-26); this
-- is the fourth. SEAM 3 is the join this Act can read, and case 40 fills the
-- list.
--
-- THE SIX-ARGUMENT FORM IS KEPT and defined in terms of the seven-argument one,
-- because forty-odd cases below neither have nor need an infant, and threading
-- a seventh EMPTY through all of them would have been forty lines of noise
-- around one line of substance.
GIVEN `the intestate` IS A Person
      `his family` IS A Family
      `his estate` IS AN Estate
      `his will` IS A MAYBE Will
      `domiciled in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN
      `he was a Muslim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the infants concerned in the estate` IS A LIST OF Infant
GIVETH A `Deceased person`
`an ISA deceased person, with infants concerned in the estate` MEANS `Deceased person` WITH
    `the deceased`  IS `the intestate`
    `the family`    IS `his family`
    `the estate`    IS `his estate`
    `the will`      IS `his will`
    `domiciled in Singapore at the time of his death` IS `domiciled in Singapore`
    `carried on business or resided in Singapore within 12 months of his death` IS TRUE
    `a Muslim`      IS `he was a Muslim`
    `the grants made in respect of the estate` IS EMPTY
    `the probate applications made in respect of the estate` IS EMPTY
    `a next of kin, creditor or other person has appeared and made out a claim to letters of administration` IS FALSE
    `the infants concerned in the estate` IS `the infants concerned in the estate`

GIVEN `the intestate` IS A Person
      `his family` IS A Family
      `his estate` IS AN Estate
      `his will` IS A MAYBE Will
      `domiciled in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN
      `he was a Muslim` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Deceased person`
`an ISA deceased person` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person, with infants concerned in the estate`
        `the intestate` `his family` `his estate` `his will`
        `domiciled in Singapore` `he was a Muslim` EMPTY

-- A guardian OF THE PROPERTY, because that is the only kind GIA s 17 reaches:
-- "A guardian of the property of an infant shall not ... be empowered to give a
-- good discharge for any legacy or other capital moneys payable to or
-- receivable by an infant." How he was appointed and whether he acts alone are
-- parameters rather than constants, because the two must agree with the rest of
-- the case file: an intestate leaves no will, so a guardian appointed BY WILL
-- cannot appear in a fixture of this module's own subject matter. Every other
-- field is the neutral value; this Act reads none of them, and the record exists
-- so that the same case file can be put to `guardianship-of-infants-act.l4`
-- without being rebuilt.
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the reference for the infant he is guardian of` IS A STRING
      `how he was appointed` IS A `How the guardian was appointed`
      `alone, or jointly with` IS AN `Alone or jointly`
GIVETH A Guardian
`an ISA guardian of the property of the infant` MEANS Guardian WITH
    `the guardian`                       IS `the person`
    `the reference for the infant`       IS `the reference for the infant he is guardian of`
    `how the guardian was appointed`     IS `how he was appointed`
    `of the person or of the property`   IS `guardian of the property of the infant`
    `alone or jointly`                   IS `alone, or jointly with`
    `refuses to act`                     IS FALSE
    `the mother or father objects to his so acting` IS FALSE
    `considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the infant` IS FALSE
    `removed from his guardianship by the court` IS FALSE
    `has given security`                 IS TRUE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to security` IS FALSE
    `has sold, mortgaged, exchanged, or otherwise parted with the possession of any of the movable or immovable property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has leased land belonging to the infant` IS FALSE
    `the term of the lease, in years`     IS NOTHING
    `has the permission of the court to deal with the property of the infant` IS FALSE
    -- The guardian has PURPORTED to discharge the share. Whether that discharge
    -- is good is GIA s 17's question and not this Act's; the fact is recorded so
    -- that the question can be asked of this record at all.
    `has given a good discharge for a legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by the infant` IS TRUE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to giving a good discharge` IS FALSE
    `the provision made out of the income of the property of the infant for his maintenance and education`
        IS (`A periodical sum` WITH
                `the sum`    IS 0
                `the period` IS `monthly`)
    `has the permission of the court to apply a sum exceeding $100 per month` IS FALSE

GIVEN `the child` IS A Person
      `his father` IS A MAYBE Person
      `his mother` IS A MAYBE Person
      `his guardians` IS A LIST OF Guardian
      `the capital of his property` IS A Money
GIVETH AN Infant
`an ISA infant concerned in the estate` MEANS Infant WITH
    `the infant`                         IS `the child`
    `the father of the infant`           IS `his father`
    `the mother of the infant`           IS `his mother`
    `the guardians of the infant`        IS `his guardians`
    `some other person has parental rights with respect to him` IS FALSE
    `the income of the property of the infant` IS 0
    `the capital of the property of the infant` IS `the capital of his property`
    `of an age to express an independent opinion` IS FALSE

-- A plain Singapore-domiciled estate: $900,000 movable and $300,000 immovable
-- in Singapore, $200,000 of expenses of due administration, so s 5's fund is
-- $1,000,000 and every fraction below is a fraction of that.
`a Singapore estate of one million after expenses` MEANS `an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 0 200_000 0 0 0


§§ `Worked cases — what the rules actually produce`

-- Forty-six case blocks, carrying sixty-five sets of worked facts between
-- them. Every rule of s 7 fires in at least one of them, both limbs of s 5 are
-- exercised, both gates (s 2 and the commencement date) are shown shutting the
-- Act off, and each of s 3, s 6(a), s 6(b), s 8, s 9 and s 10 has a case that
-- turns on it. Every entry in the ambiguity register that changes an answer has
-- a case that exhibits the reading taken: A4 (cases 20, 20a), A5 (21), A7
-- (23a, 15d), A12 (31), A13 (2, 27), A14 (26, and case 6's Wira), A15 (25),
-- A16 (15), A17 (24b, 24c), A18 (17a, 17d, 17f), A19 (17c), A20 (15c),
-- A21 (17e, 17g).
--
-- THE EXCLUSION LADDER HAS ITS OWN SECTION, cases 34 to 39, and it is the
-- coverage this file most conspicuously lacked. s 7's architecture is that a
-- nearer class shuts a remoter one out, and no fixture here put two classes
-- side by side: every case that reached rule 6 had no spouse and no parent,
-- every case that reached rule 7 had no sibling, every case that reached rule
-- 8 had no grandparent. Four separate one-line defects each caused the estate
-- to be distributed TWICE and every assertion in the file stayed green. A
-- ladder asserted only at its ends is not asserted.
--
-- EVERY EXPECTED ANSWER IS AN `#ASSERT`, NOT AN `#EVAL`. An earlier version of
-- this file stated its expectations in prose above seventy-six `#EVAL`s, so
-- `l4 run` printed numbers and exited 0 whether they were right or wrong and
-- nothing at all was machine-checked — including the module's own sum-check,
-- which was evaluated twelve times and asserted never. Each expectation below
-- was derived from the Act FIRST and then run; where the two disagreed, the
-- encoding was repaired and the case that caught it says so.
--
-- COVERAGE IS BY BRANCH, NOT BY HAPPY PATH. For each rule there is a case that
-- satisfies it and at least one that does not, and every threshold in the Act
-- is asserted from both sides: s 5's "after 2 June 1967" (cases 13, 13a, 13b),
-- s 8's "more than one wife" (cases 1, 11 and 29), rule 5's "in equal portions
-- if there be 2 parents" (cases 4 and 5), s 5's "possessed beneficially"
-- (cases 24, 24a, 24b, 24c, 24d), s 6(b)'s "shall rank immediately after" at
-- both degrees (cases 7, 7a, 9, 31, 31a, 32), s 6(a)'s two classes (17, 17a,
-- 17b, 17d, 17e, 17f, 17g), and the same-day boundary of AMBIGUITY A19 (17c).
--
-- AND COVERAGE IS BY LIMB, NOT BY RULE. A negative assertion on an
-- OVER-DETERMINED fixture — `NOT rule n` on facts that fail rule n for two
-- reasons at once — tests nothing: the conjunct it was written for can be
-- deleted and the assertion stays green, so the rule it appears to guard has
-- no test at all. For each conjunct of each rule there is therefore a fixture
-- that satisfies every OTHER conjunct of that rule, and the comment beside the
-- assertion names the limb it isolates. Case 10 does that work for four rules
-- at once, every class of taker being empty there; cases 34 to 39 do it for
-- the chapeaux of rules 6, 7 and 8; cases 1, 4a, 5 and 10 do it for the limbs
-- of rules 1, 4 and 5. Where an over-determined assertion is kept anyway —
-- because it is the pairing a reader will look for — the comment says that it
-- is over-determined and names the fixture that is not.
--
-- THE LAST TWO BLOCKS ARE NOT ABOUT THIS ACT'S RULES. Cases 40 and 40a, and
-- case 41, test the two decisions in `Joins with the other three Acts` — the
-- shares an infant takes, which GIA s 17 governs the payment of, and this Act's
-- half of the fact PAA s 29(5) swears to. They are labelled there and here as
-- belonging to no provision of this Act, and they are asserted because a join
-- that is stated and not asserted is the failure the cross-module audit found
-- in all four modules at once.

§§§ `Case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue: rule 2 with rule 3`

-- s 5's fund is $1,000,000 — $900,000 movable and $300,000 immovable in
-- Singapore, less $200,000 of expenses. Rule 2 gives the spouse one half;
-- rule 3 distributes the other half per stirpes among two surviving children,
-- so a quarter each.
`Beatrice` MEANS `living, childless` "Beatrice"
`Clara` MEANS `living, childless` "Clara"
`Daniel` MEANS `living, childless` "Daniel"
`Aaron` MEANS
    `who died on` "Aaron" (YMD 2024 3 1)
        (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Clara`), (`an ISA legitimate child` `Daniel`))

`case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue` EQUALS 1_000_000
-- Rule 2 is the rung that applies.
--
-- SAY WHICH LIMB EACH NEGATIVE ASSERTION IS ACTUALLY TESTING, because an
-- earlier comment here said "rules 1 and 4 both fail on 'issue'" and that was
-- true of rule 1 and false of rule 4. On these facts rule 1 fails on ONE limb
-- — "no issue" — the spouse being present and no parent surviving, so the
-- assertion below is a minimal witness for that limb. Rule 4 fails on TWO
-- limbs at once, "and no issue" and "but a parent or parents", so the
-- assertion on it is OVER-DETERMINED: it would still pass if rule 4's "and no
-- issue" conjunct were deleted. It is kept because rule 2 and rule 4 are the
-- two rungs a reader will confuse on these facts, and the minimal witness for
-- rule 4's issue limb is asserted on case 4a, where the parents survive.
#ASSERT     `rule 2 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and issue` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 1 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 4 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and no issue but a parent or parents` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue`
#ASSERT `the share of the estate to which the surviving spouse is entitled` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue` EQUALS (1 DIVIDED BY 2)
-- s 5(b)'s first limb from the side that fails it, and it is the SOLE failure:
-- Aaron is domiciled in Singapore, so limb (b)'s "domiciled outside Singapore"
-- is false while its second limb — $300,000 of Singapore land held
-- beneficially — is satisfied. Case 14a is the same limb from the other side.
#ASSERT NOT `section 5(b) — domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue`
#ASSERT `the immovable property situated in Singapore, possessed beneficially` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue` EQUALS 300_000
-- One wife: the other side of s 8's "more than one" threshold, which case 11
-- asserts from above.
#ASSERT NOT `section 8 — the intestate leaves surviving him more than one wife` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue` EQUALS LIST "Beatrice takes 0.5", "Clara takes 0.25", "Daniel takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 2 — rule 3 Proviso No. (2): descendants to the remotest degree`

-- Elias leaves three children. Fatimah survives. Gopal predeceased, leaving
-- Hana (who survives) and Ismail (who also predeceased, leaving Jia, who
-- survives). Kumar predeceased leaving nobody at all.
--
-- Two stocks take, not three (AMBIGUITY A13): Fatimah, and Gopal's. Each takes
-- one half. Gopal's half is divided again between Hana and Ismail's stock, so
-- Hana takes a quarter and Jia — the intestate's great-grandchild — takes the
-- quarter that would have been Ismail's. That second recursion is Proviso
-- No. (2)'s "remotest degree" doing its work.
`Jia` MEANS `living, childless` "Jia"
`the ISA Ismail` MEANS `who died on` "Ismail" (YMD 2022 1 1) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Jia`))
`Hana` MEANS `living, childless` "Hana"
`Gopal` MEANS
    `who died on` "Gopal" (YMD 2020 5 5)
        (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Hana`), (`an ISA legitimate child` `the ISA Ismail`))
`Kumar` MEANS `who died on` "Kumar" (YMD 2019 3 3) EMPTY
`Fatimah` MEANS `living, childless` "Fatimah"
`Elias` MEANS
    `who died on` "Elias" (YMD 2024 1 1)
        (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Fatimah`), (`an ISA legitimate child` `Gopal`), (`an ISA legitimate child` `Kumar`))

`case 2 — per stirpes to the remotest degree` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Elias` `no family in evidence`
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

-- AMBIGUITY A13 asserted, not merely argued: Kumar died childless and is NOT a
-- stock, so the halves are halves and not thirds.
#ASSERT NOT `rule 3 — this child is a stock which takes, in his own right or through those who legally represent him` (`an ISA legitimate child` `Kumar`) (JUST (YMD 2024 1 1))
#ASSERT     `rule 3 — this child is a stock which takes, in his own right or through those who legally represent him` (`an ISA legitimate child` `Gopal`) (JUST (YMD 2024 1 1))
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 2 — per stirpes to the remotest degree` EQUALS LIST "Fatimah takes 0.5", "Hana takes 0.25", "Jia takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 2 — per stirpes to the remotest degree` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 3 — rule 1: a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent`

-- "the spouse shall be entitled to the whole of the estate."
`Marcus` MEANS `living, childless` "Marcus"
`Lina` MEANS `who died on` "Lina" (YMD 2023 7 4) EMPTY
`case 3 — a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Lina`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Marcus`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `rule 1 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` `case 3 — a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 2 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and issue` `case 3 — a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 4 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and no issue but a parent or parents` `case 3 — a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent`
#ASSERT `the share of the estate to which the surviving spouse is entitled` `case 3 — a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 3 — a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` EQUALS LIST "Marcus takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 3 — a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 4 — rule 4 with rule 5: a surviving spouse, no issue, two parents`

-- The spouse takes one half under rule 4; the parents take "the other half" —
-- rule 4's words — "in equal portions if there be 2 parents", which is rule
-- 5's. The two rules agree on the parental quantity, and the assertions below
-- put rule 4's half beside rule 5's so that the agreement is checked and not
-- merely displayed.
`Omar` MEANS `living, childless` "Omar"
`Nadia's father` MEANS `living, childless` "Nadia's father"
`Nadia's mother` MEANS `living, childless` "Nadia's mother"
`Nadia` MEANS `who died on` "Nadia" (YMD 2024 2 2) EMPTY
`case 4 — a surviving spouse, no issue, two parents` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Nadia`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Omar`))
                         (JUST `Nadia's father`) (JUST `Nadia's mother`) EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `rule 4 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and no issue but a parent or parents` `case 4 — a surviving spouse, no issue, two parents`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 1 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` `case 4 — a surviving spouse, no issue, two parents`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 2 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and issue` `case 4 — a surviving spouse, no issue, two parents`
#ASSERT `rule 4 — the share of the estate to which the parent or parents are entitled` `case 4 — a surviving spouse, no issue, two parents` EQUALS (1 DIVIDED BY 2)
#ASSERT `rule 5 — the share of the estate the parent or parents take` `case 4 — a surviving spouse, no issue, two parents` EQUALS (1 DIVIDED BY 2)
-- "in equal portions if there be 2 parents" — this is the two-parent side of
-- that threshold; case 5 is the one-parent side.
#ASSERT count (`the parents of the intestate who survive him` `case 4 — a surviving spouse, no issue, two parents`) EQUALS 2
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 4 — a surviving spouse, no issue, two parents` EQUALS LIST "Omar takes 0.5", "Nadia's father takes 0.25", "Nadia's mother takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 4 — a surviving spouse, no issue, two parents` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 4a — rule 5's antecedent: surviving parents WITH descendants take nothing`

-- Rule 5 opens "If there are no descendants". Where there ARE descendants the
-- rule confers nothing on a parent, and a parent must not appear in "the
-- persons entitled to succeed beneficially" — s 5's own phrase — at all, not
-- even carrying a share of nought.
--
-- THIS CASE EXISTS BECAUSE ITS ABSENCE HID A BUG. No fixture in this file had
-- both a child and a surviving parent, and the rule-5 entitlement list was
-- guarded only on the parents being present, never on the rule's own
-- antecedent. Two named parents were emitted with a share of nought and the
-- module's sum-check could not see them, nought adding nothing to a total.
`Aaron's father` MEANS `living, childless` "Aaron's father"
`Aaron's mother` MEANS `living, childless` "Aaron's mother"
`case 4a — a surviving spouse, issue, and two surviving parents` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`))
                         (JUST `Aaron's father`) (JUST `Aaron's mother`) EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `rule 2 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and issue` `case 4a — a surviving spouse, issue, and two surviving parents`
-- RULE 4'S "and no issue" LIMB, ISOLATED. This is the one fixture in the file
-- on which rule 4's other two limbs are BOTH satisfied — a spouse survives and
-- parents survive — so the issue limb is the sole reason rule 4 fails, and
-- deleting that conjunct would make this assertion fail. Case 1's `NOT rule 4`
-- cannot do that work: there the parents are absent too.
#ASSERT NOT `rule 4 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and no issue but a parent or parents` `case 4a — a surviving spouse, issue, and two surviving parents`
-- Likewise rule 5's "If there are no descendants" limb in isolation: the
-- parents are present, so the descendants are the sole reason it fails.
#ASSERT NOT `rule 5 — there are no descendants, and there are a parent or parents of the intestate` `case 4a — a surviving spouse, issue, and two surviving parents`
#ASSERT `rule 5 — the share of the estate the parent or parents take` `case 4a — a surviving spouse, issue, and two surviving parents` EQUALS 0
-- The parents are alive and in evidence — the rule, not the facts, is what
-- keeps them out.
#ASSERT count (`the parents of the intestate who survive him` `case 4a — a surviving spouse, issue, and two surviving parents`) EQUALS 2
#ASSERT null (`rule 5 — the entitlements of the parent or parents` `case 4a — a surviving spouse, issue, and two surviving parents`)
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 4a — a surviving spouse, issue, and two surviving parents` EQUALS LIST "Beatrice takes 0.5", "Clara takes 0.25", "Daniel takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 4a — a surviving spouse, issue, and two surviving parents` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 5 — rule 5 alone: no spouse, no descendants, one surviving parent`

-- The father predeceased; the mother survives and takes the whole. Rule 5's
-- "in equal portions if there be 2 parents" does not bite, because there is
-- one — and the predeceased father is not counted among "the parent or
-- parents", which is AMBIGUITY A10.
`Priya's mother` MEANS `living, childless` "Priya's mother"
`Priya's father` MEANS `who died on` "Priya's father" (YMD 2010 1 1) EMPTY
`Priya` MEANS `who died on` "Priya" (YMD 2024 4 4) EMPTY
`case 5 — no spouse, no descendants, one surviving parent` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Priya`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY (JUST `Priya's father`) (JUST `Priya's mother`) EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `rule 5 — there are no descendants, and there are a parent or parents of the intestate` `case 5 — no spouse, no descendants, one surviving parent`
-- RULE 4'S "leaving a surviving spouse" LIMB, ISOLATED: rule 4's other two
-- limbs are both satisfied here — there is no issue and there IS a surviving
-- parent — so the absent spouse is the sole reason rule 4 fails.
#ASSERT NOT `rule 4 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and no issue but a parent or parents` `case 5 — no spouse, no descendants, one surviving parent`
-- Two parents are in evidence and only one survives, so the division is by one.
#ASSERT count (`the parents of the intestate who survive him` `case 5 — no spouse, no descendants, one surviving parent`) EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `rule 5 — the share of the estate the parent or parents take` `case 5 — no spouse, no descendants, one surviving parent` EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 5 — no spouse, no descendants, one surviving parent` EQUALS LIST "Priya's mother takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 5 — no spouse, no descendants, one surviving parent` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 6 — rule 6: brothers and sisters, and the children of a deceased sister`

-- No spouse, no descendants, no parents. Salim survives; Tan predeceased
-- leaving three surviving children, of whom two are "children" within s 3.
-- Two stocks, one half each; Tan's half is divided between Uma and Vik. Rule
-- 6's representation is ONE generation deep — see AMBIGUITY A15.
--
-- WIRA IS HERE BECAUSE THE FILTER WAS UNTESTED. Rule 6's representation helper
-- conjoins `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` and cites
-- s 3 in its @ref, but every child of every deceased sibling in this file was
-- legitimate, so the conjunct was never exercised: deleting it changed no
-- answer anywhere. It changes this one. Wira is neither legitimate nor
-- adopted, so s 3 excludes him from "the children of any deceased brother or
-- sister", Tan's half stays halved between Uma and Vik, and the read-out below
-- fails the moment the conjunct goes. This is AMBIGUITY A14 — s 3's definition
-- governing "this Act" — applied where rule 6 cites it, and it is the same
-- defect the s 9 walk had.
`Uma` MEANS `living, childless` "Uma"
`Vik` MEANS `living, childless` "Vik"
`Wira` MEANS `living, childless` "Wira"
`Salim` MEANS `living, childless` "Salim"
`Tan` MEANS
    `who died on` "Tan" (YMD 2021 6 6)
        (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Uma`),
              (`an ISA legitimate child` `Vik`),
              (`a child neither legitimate nor adopted` `Wira`))
`the ISA Rahim` MEANS `who died on` "Rahim" (YMD 2024 5 5) EMPTY
`case 6 — brothers and sisters and the children of a deceased sister` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Salim`), (`of the whole blood,` `Tan`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `rule 6 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants or parents` `case 6 — brothers and sisters and the children of a deceased sister`
-- s 3 filters the representatives: three of Tan's children survive and two of
-- them are "children" within the meaning of this Act.
#ASSERT count (`Tan`'s `children`) EQUALS 3
#ASSERT count (`rule 6 — the children of a deceased brother or sister who take according to their stocks` (`of the whole blood,` `Tan`) (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))) EQUALS 2
#ASSERT NOT `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` (`a child neither legitimate nor adopted` `Wira`)
-- Rules 7 and 8 are shut out, but note WHY, because these two assertions are
-- OVER-DETERMINED on these facts and cannot stand as the test of the ladder.
-- Rule 7 fails here both because rule 6's class of stocks is occupied and
-- because no grandparent survives; rule 8 fails on three limbs at once. Each
-- of those limbs is isolated on a fixture of its own — the grandparent limb on
-- case 10, the sibling limb on cases 36 and 39, the "or grandparents" limb on
-- case 37, the chapeau on case 38 — and it was measured that forcing any one
-- of them true left this pair green.
#ASSERT NOT `rule 7 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate` `case 6 — brothers and sisters and the children of a deceased sister`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 8 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate` `case 6 — brothers and sisters and the children of a deceased sister`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 9 — in default of distribution under rules 1 to 8` `case 6 — brothers and sisters and the children of a deceased sister`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 6 — brothers and sisters and the children of a deceased sister` EQUALS LIST "Salim takes 0.5", "Uma takes 0.25", "Vik takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 6 — brothers and sisters and the children of a deceased sister` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 7 — s 6(b): the half blood rank immediately after the whole blood`

-- The same estate, but the surviving siblings are one of the whole blood and
-- one of the half blood. Wati is excluded while Salim is entitled, and takes
-- the whole in default of him.
`Wati` MEANS `living, childless` "Wati"
`case 7 — one brother of the whole blood and one of the half blood` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Salim`), (`of the half blood,` `Wati`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

`case 7a — only brothers and sisters of the half blood` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the half blood,` `Wati`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `related to the deceased by the whole blood` (`of the whole blood,` `Salim`)
#ASSERT     `related to the deceased by the half blood`  (`of the half blood,` `Wati`)
#ASSERT NOT `related to the deceased by the whole blood` (`of the half blood,` `Wati`)
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 7 — one brother of the whole blood and one of the half blood` EQUALS LIST "Salim takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 7 — one brother of the whole blood and one of the half blood` EQUALS 1
-- With no relative of the whole blood entitled, those of the half blood take
-- in full — AMBIGUITY A11's reading of "shall rank immediately after".
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 7a — only brothers and sisters of the half blood` EQUALS LIST "Wati takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 7a — only brothers and sisters of the half blood` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 8 — rule 7: the grandparents take in equal portions`

`Xin` MEANS `living, childless` "Xin"
`Yusof` MEANS `living, childless` "Yusof"
`case 8 — two surviving grandparents` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY (LIST `Xin`, `Yusof`) EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `rule 7 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate` `case 8 — two surviving grandparents`
-- Rule 8's chapeau adds "or grandparents", so grandparents shut rule 8 out —
-- BUT NOT ON THESE FACTS ALONE. Case 8 has no surviving uncle or aunt either,
-- so rule 8's own class limb fails as well and the assertion below is
-- OVER-DETERMINED: it was measured that forcing rule 8's "or grandparents"
-- conjunct true left it green. Case 37 is the fixture that isolates the limb,
-- putting a surviving uncle beside the grandparents so that the grandparents
-- are the only thing keeping rule 8 out. This one is kept because it is the
-- pairing a reader coming from rule 7 will look for.
#ASSERT NOT `rule 8 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate` `case 8 — two surviving grandparents`
#ASSERT null (`the uncles and aunts of the intestate who survive him` `case 8 — two surviving grandparents`)
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 8 — two surviving grandparents` EQUALS LIST "Xin takes 0.5", "Yusof takes 0.5"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 8 — two surviving grandparents` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 9 — rule 8: the uncles and aunts, with s 6(b) applied again`

-- Three surviving uncles and aunts, two of the whole blood and one of the
-- half. The half blood rank immediately after, so the two of the whole blood
-- take one half each and the third takes nothing. Rule 8 gives an uncle no
-- representatives, so a deceased uncle's children never appear.
`the ISA Zainab` MEANS `living, childless` "Zainab"
`Anand` MEANS `living, childless` "Anand"
`the ISA Bala` MEANS `living, childless` "Bala"
`case 9 — uncles and aunts of the whole and half blood` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `the ISA Zainab`),
                               (`of the whole blood,` `Anand`),
                               (`of the half blood,` `the ISA Bala`)))
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `rule 8 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate` `case 9 — uncles and aunts of the whole and half blood`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 7 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate` `case 9 — uncles and aunts of the whole and half blood`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 9 — in default of distribution under rules 1 to 8` `case 9 — uncles and aunts of the whole and half blood`
-- Three uncles and aunts survive; s 6(b) leaves two of them entitled.
#ASSERT count (`the uncles and aunts of the intestate who survive him` `case 9 — uncles and aunts of the whole and half blood`) EQUALS 3
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 9 — uncles and aunts of the whole and half blood` EQUALS LIST "Zainab takes 0.5", "Anand takes 0.5"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 9 — uncles and aunts of the whole and half blood` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 10 — rule 9: in default of rules 1 to 8, the Government`

-- THE ONE FIXTURE WHERE EVERY CLASS IS EMPTY, WHICH MAKES IT THE MINIMAL
-- WITNESS FOR FOUR SEPARATE CLASS LIMBS. Rahim leaves no spouse, no issue, no
-- parent, no brother or sister, no grandparent and no uncle or aunt, so for
-- each of rules 1, 5, 7 and 8 every conjunct EXCEPT the one naming that rule's
-- own class of taker is satisfied. Each of the four negative assertions below
-- therefore turns on exactly one conjunct and fails if that conjunct is
-- deleted.
`case 10 — nobody within rules 1 to 8` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim` `no family in evidence`
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `case 10 — nobody within rules 1 to 8`
-- Rule 1's "leaving a surviving spouse", isolated: there is no issue and no
-- parent, so the absent spouse is the sole failure.
#ASSERT NOT `rule 1 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` `case 10 — nobody within rules 1 to 8`
-- Rule 5's "the parent or parents of the intestate", isolated: there are no
-- descendants, so the absent parents are the sole failure.
#ASSERT NOT `rule 5 — there are no descendants, and there are a parent or parents of the intestate` `case 10 — nobody within rules 1 to 8`
-- Rule 7's "but grandparents of the intestate", isolated: the chapeau holds
-- and rule 6's class of stocks is empty, so the absent grandparents are the
-- sole failure.
#ASSERT NOT `rule 7 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate` `case 10 — nobody within rules 1 to 8`
-- Rule 8's "but uncles and aunts of the intestate", isolated: its first three
-- conjuncts all hold, so the absent uncles and aunts are the sole failure.
#ASSERT NOT `rule 8 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate` `case 10 — nobody within rules 1 to 8`
#ASSERT `rule 9 — in default of distribution under rules 1 to 8` `case 10 — nobody within rules 1 to 8`
#ASSERT null (`the distribution under rules 1 to 8` `case 10 — nobody within rules 1 to 8`)
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 10 — nobody within rules 1 to 8` EQUALS LIST "the Government takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 10 — nobody within rules 1 to 8` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 11 — s 8: more than one wife share the one spousal share`

-- Rule 2 gives "the spouse" one half because there is issue; s 8 divides that
-- one half between the two surviving wives, so each takes a quarter, and the
-- single child takes the other half. The estate is not enlarged by the second
-- marriage — which is s 8's whole point.
`Halimah` MEANS `living, childless` "Halimah"
`Sofia` MEANS `living, childless` "Sofia"
`Idris` MEANS `living, childless` "Idris"
`the ISA Tariq` MEANS `who died on` "Tariq" (YMD 2024 8 8) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Idris`))
`case 11 — two surviving wives and one child` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Tariq`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Halimah`), (`a subsisting marriage to` `Sofia`))
                         NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

-- "more than one wife": two is above the threshold, one (case 1) is not.
#ASSERT `section 8 — the intestate leaves surviving him more than one wife` `case 11 — two surviving wives and one child`
#ASSERT count (`the spouses surviving the intestate` `case 11 — two surviving wives and one child`) EQUALS 2
-- "the share that the wife ... would have been entitled to, had the intestate
-- left only one wife surviving him" is rule 2's one half, undivided.
#ASSERT `the share of the estate to which the surviving spouse is entitled` `case 11 — two surviving wives and one child` EQUALS (1 DIVIDED BY 2)
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 11 — two surviving wives and one child` EQUALS LIST "Halimah takes 0.25", "Sofia takes 0.25", "Idris takes 0.5"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 11 — two surviving wives and one child` EQUALS 1

