§§§ `Case 13 — s 5: the commencement threshold, from both sides`

-- "If a person dies intestate after 2 June 1967". A death in 1965 is outside
-- the section and this Act distributes nothing. The word is "after", so the
-- comparison is STRICT: a death ON 2 June 1967 is outside it too, and a death
-- on 3 June 1967 is within it. All three are asserted.
`Cheng` MEANS `who died on` "Cheng" (YMD 1965 1 1) EMPTY
`case 13 — a death before the Act commenced` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Cheng`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

`Cheng, dying on the day of commencement` MEANS `who died on` "Cheng" (YMD 1967 6 2) EMPTY
`case 13a — a death on 2 June 1967` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Cheng, dying on the day of commencement`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

`Cheng, dying the day after commencement` MEANS `who died on` "Cheng" (YMD 1967 6 3) EMPTY
`case 13b — a death on 3 June 1967` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Cheng, dying the day after commencement`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `section 5 — the person dies intestate after 2 June 1967` `case 13 — a death before the Act commenced`
#ASSERT null (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `case 13 — a death before the Act commenced`)
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 13 — a death before the Act commenced` EQUALS 0
-- ON the day: outside, because the section says "after".
#ASSERT NOT `section 5 — the person dies intestate after 2 June 1967` `case 13a — a death on 2 June 1967`
#ASSERT null (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `case 13a — a death on 2 June 1967`)
-- One day later: within.
#ASSERT `section 5 — the person dies intestate after 2 June 1967` `case 13b — a death on 3 June 1967`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 13b — a death on 3 June 1967` EQUALS LIST "Beatrice takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 13b — a death on 3 June 1967` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 14 — s 5(a) against s 5(b): domicile outside Singapore`

-- (i) Domiciled outside Singapore with only MOVABLE property in Singapore:
--     neither limb of s 5 is satisfied — (a) needs Singapore domicile and (b)
--     reaches only immovable property — so this Act distributes nothing, and
--     s 4(1) sends the movables to the law of the country of domicile.
-- (ii) Domiciled outside Singapore with immovable property in Singapore: limb
--     (b) is satisfied, and the fund is that immovable property less the
--     expenses — $500,000 less $200,000, and NOT the movables beside it.
`Dinesh` MEANS `who died on` "Dinesh" (YMD 2024 9 9) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Clara`))
`case 14 — domiciled abroad, only movables in Singapore` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Dinesh` `no family in evidence`
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 0 0 0 200_000 0 0 0)
        NOTHING FALSE FALSE
`case 14a — domiciled abroad, immovables in Singapore` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Dinesh` `no family in evidence`
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 500_000 0 0 200_000 0 0 0)
        NOTHING FALSE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `case 14 — domiciled abroad, only movables in Singapore`
#ASSERT NOT `section 5(b) — domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore` `case 14 — domiciled abroad, only movables in Singapore`
#ASSERT NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `case 14 — domiciled abroad, only movables in Singapore`
#ASSERT null (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `case 14 — domiciled abroad, only movables in Singapore`)
-- s 4(1) refers those movables out; the referral is a boundary, not a share.
#ASSERT NOT `section 4(1) — the law of the country of domicile is the law of Singapore` `case 14 — domiciled abroad, only movables in Singapore`
#ASSERT     `section 5(b) — domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore` `case 14a — domiciled abroad, immovables in Singapore`
#ASSERT NOT `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `case 14a — domiciled abroad, immovables in Singapore`
-- "that property" under limb (b) is the immovable alone: $500,000, not $1,400,000.
#ASSERT `section 5 — that property` `case 14a — domiciled abroad, immovables in Singapore` EQUALS 500_000
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 14a — domiciled abroad, immovables in Singapore` EQUALS 300_000
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 14a — domiciled abroad, immovables in Singapore` EQUALS LIST "Clara takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 14a — domiciled abroad, immovables in Singapore` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 15 — s 10: a will beneficially disposing of part`

-- Aaron leaves a will giving Beatrice a pecuniary legacy of $400,000 and
-- nothing else. He is still an "intestate" within s 3, because he dies
-- intestate as to some beneficial interest. s 5's fund is $1,000,000; s 10
-- confines this Act to "the part of his property not so disposed of", which is
-- $600,000 — the expenses having been borne by the undisposed part, which is
-- AMBIGUITY A16. The fractions are unchanged: the spouse takes one half of
-- $600,000 and the two children a quarter each.
`a will giving Beatrice a pecuniary legacy` MEANS
    `a duly executed will` `Aaron` (LIST (`a beneficial gift` `Beatrice` `a pecuniary legacy` 400_000)) FALSE

`case 15 — a will beneficially disposing of part` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 0 200_000 400_000 0 0)
        (JUST `a will giving Beatrice a pecuniary legacy`)
        TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `case 15 — a will beneficially disposing of part`
#ASSERT `section 10 — the person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property` `case 15 — a will beneficially disposing of part`
-- The quantity read off the will's own dispositions, and the estate's record of
-- the same quantity, agree. Case 22 is the case where they do not.
#ASSERT `section 10 — the value of the part of his property so disposed of` `case 15 — a will beneficially disposing of part` EQUALS 400_000
#ASSERT `the value beneficially disposed of by the will recorded in the estate agrees with the dispositions made by the will` `case 15 — a will beneficially disposing of part`
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 15 — a will beneficially disposing of part` EQUALS 600_000
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 15 — a will beneficially disposing of part` EQUALS LIST "Beatrice takes 0.5", "Clara takes 0.25", "Daniel takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 15 — a will beneficially disposing of part` EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `section 10 proviso — the personal representative is a trustee for the persons entitled under this Act in respect of the part of the estate not expressly disposed of` `case 15 — a will beneficially disposing of part`

§§§ `Case 15a — the s 10 proviso's "unless": the will says otherwise`

-- The same will, save that "it appears by the will that the personal
-- representative is entitled to take that part beneficially". The proviso is a
-- "shall ... unless", so the trusteeship is the default and this is the
-- exception: everything else about the case is unchanged and only the
-- trusteeship goes.
`a will giving Beatrice a legacy, the representative to take the rest` MEANS
    `a duly executed will` `Aaron` (LIST (`a beneficial gift` `Beatrice` `a pecuniary legacy` 400_000)) TRUE

`case 15a — the will lets the personal representative take the undisposed part` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 0 200_000 400_000 0 0)
        (JUST `a will giving Beatrice a legacy, the representative to take the rest`)
        TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `it appears by the will that the personal representative is entitled to take that part beneficially` `case 15a — the will lets the personal representative take the undisposed part`
#ASSERT NOT `section 10 proviso — the personal representative is a trustee for the persons entitled under this Act in respect of the part of the estate not expressly disposed of` `case 15a — the will lets the personal representative take the undisposed part`
-- The exception goes to the trusteeship only, not to the distribution.
#ASSERT `section 10 — the person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property` `case 15a — the will lets the personal representative take the undisposed part`
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 15a — the will lets the personal representative take the undisposed part` EQUALS 600_000

§§§ `Case 15b — s 10: "so disposed of" is a SUM over the beneficial dispositions`

-- s 10's quantity is computed by summing the will's beneficial dispositions
-- and filtering out the rest, and neither operation had a fixture: every will
-- in this file carried at most ONE disposition, so a summation that returned
-- only its first element, and a filter that let a non-beneficial disposition
-- through, would both have been invisible.
--
-- Aaron's will gives Beatrice a pecuniary legacy of $300,000, gives Clara a
-- specific devise of $100,000, and charges $250,000 with the payment of a
-- debt. The charge is not a beneficial disposition — the Wills Act 1838's own
-- contrast, "charges and directions for the payment of any debt" — so
-- "the part of his property so disposed of" is $400,000 and not $650,000, and
-- the fund is $600,000 exactly as in case 15.
`a will giving Beatrice a legacy, Clara a devise, and charging a debt` MEANS
    `a duly executed will` `Aaron`
        (LIST (`a beneficial gift` `Beatrice` `a pecuniary legacy` 300_000),
              (`a beneficial gift` `Clara` `a specific devise or bequest` 100_000),
              (`a disposition that is not beneficial` `Marcus` `a charge or direction for the payment of a debt` 250_000))
        FALSE

`case 15b — a will with two beneficial dispositions and one that is not` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 0 200_000 400_000 0 0)
        (JUST `a will giving Beatrice a legacy, Clara a devise, and charging a debt`)
        TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `section 10 — the person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property` `case 15b — a will with two beneficial dispositions and one that is not`
-- The sum is over the two beneficial dispositions only: $300,000 + $100,000.
#ASSERT `section 10 — the value of the part of his property so disposed of` `case 15b — a will with two beneficial dispositions and one that is not` EQUALS 400_000
#ASSERT `the value beneficially disposed of by the will recorded in the estate agrees with the dispositions made by the will` `case 15b — a will with two beneficial dispositions and one that is not`
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 15b — a will with two beneficial dispositions and one that is not` EQUALS 600_000
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 15b — a will with two beneficial dispositions and one that is not` EQUALS LIST "Beatrice takes 0.5", "Clara takes 0.25", "Daniel takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 15b — a will with two beneficial dispositions and one that is not` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 15c — AMBIGUITY A20: a will beside property outside Singapore`

-- CASE 15 WITH ONE FIELD CHANGED: $700,000 of immovable property outside
-- Singapore, and nothing else about the case is different. s 4 reaches
-- $1,900,000 of property; s 5's fund is the $1,200,000 situated in Singapore;
-- and the whole $400,000 the will disposes of comes off THAT, so the fund is
-- $600,000 — the identical figure case 15 produces with no foreign land at
-- all. The $700,000 stands available to satisfy the legacy and this Act takes
-- no account of it.
--
-- That is the reading AMBIGUITY A20 takes, and the case exists so that the
-- reading is exhibited rather than merely argued: no fixture in this file
-- combined a will with foreign property before, so the whole path was
-- unreachable by any assertion.
`case 15c — a will beside immovable property outside Singapore` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 700_000 200_000 400_000 0 0)
        (JUST `a will giving Beatrice a pecuniary legacy`)
        TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `section 4 — the value of the property whose distribution is regulated by this Act` `case 15c — a will beside immovable property outside Singapore` EQUALS 1_900_000
#ASSERT `section 5 — that property` `case 15c — a will beside immovable property outside Singapore` EQUALS 1_200_000
#ASSERT `section 10 — the value of the part of his property so disposed of` `case 15c — a will beside immovable property outside Singapore` EQUALS 400_000
-- The whole disposed value comes off the Singapore fund: $1,200,000 less
-- $200,000 of expenses less $400,000 disposed of.
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 15c — a will beside immovable property outside Singapore` EQUALS 600_000
-- Identical to case 15's fund, on an estate $700,000 larger. That equality IS
-- AMBIGUITY A20.
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 15c — a will beside immovable property outside Singapore` EQUALS `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 15 — a will beneficially disposing of part`
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 15c — a will beside immovable property outside Singapore` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 15d — s 10: a will disposing of more than the fund`

-- AMBIGUITY A7's floor, at the s 10 subtraction rather than the s 5 one. A
-- will may beneficially dispose of more than s 5's fund — because the fund is
-- Singapore property net of the expenses and the will is not so confined (see
-- AMBIGUITY A20), or simply because the legacy exceeds what the estate turned
-- out to be worth. "The part of his property not so disposed of" is then
-- nothing, not a negative quantity, for the same reason a share of a negative
-- estate is not a thing this Act creates.
--
-- The gate still opens — s 5(a) asks whether he was possessed beneficially of
-- Singapore property and he was — so the persons entitled are identified and
-- their fractions still sum to 1. What is nil is the thing the fractions are
-- fractions of. That is the same combination case 23a asserts at the s 5
-- floor, and it had no fixture at the s 10 floor: the subtraction could be
-- unfloored with every assertion in this file still green.
`a will giving Beatrice a legacy larger than the estate` MEANS
    `a duly executed will` `Aaron` (LIST (`a beneficial gift` `Beatrice` `a pecuniary legacy` 5_000_000)) FALSE

`case 15d — a will disposing of more than the fund` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 0 200_000 5_000_000 0 0)
        (JUST `a will giving Beatrice a legacy larger than the estate`)
        TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `section 10 — the person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property` `case 15d — a will disposing of more than the fund`
#ASSERT `section 10 — the value of the part of his property so disposed of` `case 15d — a will disposing of more than the fund` EQUALS 5_000_000
#ASSERT `section 5 — the property to be distributed, after payment thereout of the expenses of due administration` `case 15d — a will disposing of more than the fund` EQUALS 1_000_000
-- Floored at nil, not carried negative.
#ASSERT `section 10 — the part of his property not so disposed of` `case 15d — a will disposing of more than the fund` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 15d — a will disposing of more than the fund` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `case 15d — a will disposing of more than the fund`
-- The fractions are still the fractions; they are fractions of nothing.
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 15d — a will disposing of more than the fund` EQUALS LIST "Beatrice takes 0.5", "Clara takes 0.25", "Daniel takes 0.25"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 15d — a will disposing of more than the fund` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 16 — s 3: a will beneficially disposing of the whole`

-- A residuary beneficial gift disposes of the whole, so the deceased is not an
-- "intestate" within s 3 and this Act has no effect at all. The s 10 proviso
-- is likewise not engaged: there is no undisposed part to hold on trust.
`a will giving Beatrice the residue` MEANS
    `a duly executed will` `Aaron` (LIST (`a beneficial gift` `Beatrice` `a residuary gift` 1_000_000)) FALSE

`case 16 — a will beneficially disposing of the whole` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 0 200_000 1_000_000 0 0)
        (JUST `a will giving Beatrice the residue`)
        TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `the will beneficially disposes of the whole of his property` `a will giving Beatrice the residue`
#ASSERT NOT `the will beneficially disposes of the whole of his property` `a will giving Beatrice a pecuniary legacy`
#ASSERT NOT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `case 16 — a will beneficially disposing of the whole`
#ASSERT NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `case 16 — a will beneficially disposing of the whole`
#ASSERT NOT `section 10 — the person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property` `case 16 — a will beneficially disposing of the whole`
#ASSERT null (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `case 16 — a will beneficially disposing of the whole`)
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 16 — a will beneficially disposing of the whole` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT NOT `section 10 proviso — the personal representative is a trustee for the persons entitled under this Act in respect of the part of the estate not expressly disposed of` `case 16 — a will beneficially disposing of the whole`
-- Each of the six kinds of disposition the ontology carries, tested against
-- `a universal or residuary beneficial gift` — the decision through which this
-- module answers s 3 and s 10 — from both sides.
#ASSERT     `a universal or residuary beneficial gift` (`a beneficial gift` `Beatrice` `a universal gift` 1_000_000)
#ASSERT     `a universal or residuary beneficial gift` (`a beneficial gift` `Beatrice` `a residuary gift` 1_000_000)
#ASSERT NOT `a universal or residuary beneficial gift` (`a beneficial gift` `Beatrice` `a specific devise or bequest` 1_000_000)
#ASSERT NOT `a universal or residuary beneficial gift` (`a beneficial gift` `Beatrice` `a pecuniary legacy` 1_000_000)
#ASSERT NOT `a universal or residuary beneficial gift` (`a beneficial gift` `Beatrice` `an appointment by will under a power` 1_000_000)
#ASSERT NOT `a universal or residuary beneficial gift` (`a beneficial gift` `Beatrice` `a charge or direction for the payment of a debt` 1_000_000)

§§§ `Case 17 — s 6(a): the child en ventre sa mère`

-- Two children born AFTER the death. Esther was conceived in the womb at the
-- date of the death and has since been born alive, so s 6(a) puts her on the
-- same footing as a child born in the intestate's lifetime. Farid was
-- conceived after the death and is outside both classes s 6(a) names, so he
-- does not take. The estate is therefore divided between Esther and one child
-- born in the lifetime — one half each — and not three ways.
`Esther` MEANS `an ISA person` "Esther" `living` (JUST (YMD 2024 10 1)) TRUE 0 EMPTY
`the ISA Farid` MEANS `an ISA person` "Farid" `living` (JUST (YMD 2025 8 1)) FALSE 0 EMPTY
`the ISA Gita` MEANS `an ISA person` "Gita" `living` (JUST (YMD 2001 4 4)) FALSE 0 EMPTY
`Hakim` MEANS
    `who died on` "Hakim" (YMD 2024 3 1)
        (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `the ISA Gita`), (`an ISA legitimate child` `Esther`), (`an ISA legitimate child` `the ISA Farid`))
`case 17 — a child conceived in the womb and a child conceived after the death` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Hakim` `no family in evidence`
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

-- s 6(a)'s FIRST limb, pinned. Like `section 4(2) — the distribution of the
-- immovable property is regulated by this Act`, this is a rule with no
-- antecedent, so its node is the constant TRUE and no input can falsify it.
-- The assertion is a PIN on the isomorphic encoding rather than a test of a
-- fact: it fails if a later editor gives the limb a condition the Act does not
-- have. It was previously the only provision of the Act named in no assertion
-- at all, while its twin at s 4(2) was pinned, and the asymmetry was
-- accidental. The limb's substantive effect is in the SHAPE of the ontology —
-- `the grandparents of the deceased` and `the uncles and aunts of the
-- deceased` are flat lists with no paternal/maternal split — which is why
-- there is nothing else here to assert.
#ASSERT `section 6(a) — there shall be no distinction between those related through his father and those related through his mother`
#ASSERT     `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` `Esther` (JUST (YMD 2024 3 1))
#ASSERT NOT `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` `the ISA Farid` (JUST (YMD 2024 3 1))
#ASSERT     `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` `the ISA Gita` (JUST (YMD 2024 3 1))
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 17 — a child conceived in the womb and a child conceived after the death` EQUALS LIST "Gita takes 0.5", "Esther takes 0.5"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 17 — a child conceived in the womb and a child conceived after the death` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 17a — s 6(a) reaches EVERY class of taker, not only the issue`

-- s 6 opens "For the purposes of distribution —", which is all nine rules of
-- s 7 and not the two it is easiest to apply to.
--
-- THIS CASE EXISTS BECAUSE ITS ABSENCE HID A BUG. The s 6(a) test used to be
-- conjoined into rule 3's stock test and rule 6's children-of-a-deceased-
-- sibling helper, and nowhere else. So a living brother born eight months
-- after the intestate's death, not conceived in the womb at the death, passed
-- rule 6 and took the WHOLE estate — while case 17's Farid, on identical
-- facts, took nothing because he arrived as a child. Whichever reading of
-- s 6(a) is right, one of those two answers had to be wrong. See AMBIGUITY A18.
--
-- Rahim died 2024-05-05. Under the reading taken, the posthumously-conceived
-- brother is outside s 6(a)'s classes, rule 6's class of stocks is therefore
-- empty, and with no grandparents and no uncles the estate falls to rule 9.
`Jamal, born after Rahim died` MEANS `an ISA person` "Jamal" `living` (JUST (YMD 2025 1 1)) FALSE 0 EMPTY
`case 17a — a brother conceived after the intestate's death` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Jamal, born after Rahim died`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` `Jamal, born after Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
-- He is alive, so it is s 6(a) and not survivorship that keeps him out.
#ASSERT `the person survives the intestate` `Jamal, born after Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
#ASSERT NOT `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a) names` `Jamal, born after Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
#ASSERT null (`rule 6 — the stocks of brothers and sisters between which the estate is shared in equal portions` `case 17a — a brother conceived after the intestate's death`)
#ASSERT `rule 9 — in default of distribution under rules 1 to 8` `case 17a — a brother conceived after the intestate's death`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 17a — a brother conceived after the intestate's death` EQUALS LIST "the Government takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 17a — a brother conceived after the intestate's death` EQUALS 1

-- The other side of the same limb: a brother born just as late, but conceived
-- in the womb at the date of the death and subsequently born alive, IS within
-- s 6(a) and takes the whole. The fact that distinguishes him is the only one
-- that differs.
`Jamal, conceived before Rahim died` MEANS `an ISA person` "Jamal" `living` (JUST (YMD 2025 1 1)) TRUE 0 EMPTY
`case 17b — a brother conceived in the womb at the intestate's death` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Jamal, conceived before Rahim died`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` `Jamal, conceived before Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 17b — a brother conceived in the womb at the intestate's death` EQUALS LIST "Jamal takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 17b — a brother conceived in the womb at the intestate's death` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 17c — AMBIGUITY A19: the same-day boundary, from both sides`

-- The Act's boundaries are moments and the ontology records dates, so a birth
-- or a death sharing the intestate's date of death has to be resolved by
-- choice. The two are resolved in OPPOSITE directions, and this is where that
-- is checked rather than merely stated: a same-day BIRTH is within "actually
-- born in his lifetime"; a same-day DEATH is not "surviving".
`born on the day Hakim died` MEANS `an ISA person` "born that day" `living` (JUST (YMD 2024 3 1)) FALSE 0 EMPTY
`born the day after Hakim died` MEANS `an ISA person` "born the next day" `living` (JUST (YMD 2024 3 2)) FALSE 0 EMPTY
`died on the day Hakim died` MEANS `who died on` "died that day" (YMD 2024 3 1) EMPTY
`died the day after Hakim died` MEANS `who died on` "died the next day" (YMD 2024 3 2) EMPTY

-- Birth: inclusive (AT MOST), resolved in the claimant's favour, matching A9.
#ASSERT     `actually born in the lifetime of the intestate` `born on the day Hakim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 3 1))
#ASSERT NOT `actually born in the lifetime of the intestate` `born the day after Hakim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 3 1))
-- Death: strict (GREATER THAN), resolved against the claimant, matching A8.
#ASSERT NOT `the person survives the intestate` `died on the day Hakim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 3 1))
#ASSERT     `the person survives the intestate` `died the day after Hakim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 3 1))

§§§ `Case 17d — s 6(a) reaches the UNCLES AND AUNTS, the class rule 8 pays`

-- THE THIRD CLASS THAT CAN PRESENT THE FACTS, AND IT HAD NO CASE. The note at
-- `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a)
-- names` says two of the six classes cannot present them — a parent and a
-- grandparent of the intestate are necessarily born before him — which leaves
-- the issue, the siblings and the UNCLES AND AUNTS as classes that can. The
-- issue had case 17 and the siblings had case 17a; the uncles and aunts had
-- nothing, and the s 6(a) conjunct at `the uncles and aunts of the intestate
-- who survive him` could be deleted with every assertion in this file still
-- green. It is a live site: an uncle conceived after the intestate's death is
-- entirely possible, the intestate's grandparent bearing another child.
--
-- Rahim died 2024-05-05. Kadir, his only uncle, was born 2025-06-01 and was
-- not conceived in the womb at the death. Under AMBIGUITY A18's reading he is
-- outside both classes s 6(a) names, so rule 8's class is empty and the estate
-- falls to rule 9 — the same answer case 17a gives the identically-placed
-- brother, which is the consistency A18 exists to secure.
`Kadir, born after Rahim died` MEANS `an ISA person` "Kadir" `living` (JUST (YMD 2025 6 1)) FALSE 0 EMPTY
`case 17d — an uncle conceived after the intestate's death` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Kadir, born after Rahim died`)))
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` `Kadir, born after Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
-- He is alive, so it is s 6(a) and not survivorship that keeps him out.
#ASSERT     `the person survives the intestate` `Kadir, born after Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
#ASSERT NOT `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a) names` `Kadir, born after Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
#ASSERT null (`the uncles and aunts of the intestate who survive him` `case 17d — an uncle conceived after the intestate's death`)
#ASSERT NOT `rule 8 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate` `case 17d — an uncle conceived after the intestate's death`
#ASSERT `rule 9 — in default of distribution under rules 1 to 8` `case 17d — an uncle conceived after the intestate's death`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 17d — an uncle conceived after the intestate's death` EQUALS LIST "the Government takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 17d — an uncle conceived after the intestate's death` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 17e — AMBIGUITY A21: an excluded brother is not a "deceased brother or sister"`

-- THE FIXTURE THAT FOUND A21, AND IT WAS BUILT LOOKING FOR SOMETHING ELSE.
-- The question it was written to ask was whether the s 6(a) membership test at
-- `rule 6 — the takers of the share which the brother or sister would have
-- taken` does any work; the answer it gave was that rule 6's stock test was
-- letting an ALIVE sibling be represented by his children, on a rule whose own
-- word is "deceased", said three times.
--
-- Nabil was conceived after Rahim's death and so is outside s 6(a); his
-- daughter Zara was born in Rahim's lifetime (no date of birth in evidence —
-- AMBIGUITY A9) and survives. Under AMBIGUITY A21's reading (i), Nabil does
-- not take, because s 6(a) excludes him — and Zara does not take either,
-- because rule 6 gives a stock to "the children of any DECEASED brother or
-- sister" and Nabil is alive. Rule 6's class is therefore empty, and with no
-- grandparent and no uncle the estate falls to rule 9: exactly the answer case
-- 17a gives an excluded sibling who has no children at all, which is the
-- parity the reading is chosen for.
--
-- On the rejected reading (ii) — s 6(a) exclusion as a deemed predecease —
-- Zara would take the whole $1,000,000. That is the size of this fork, and it
-- was being decided in the code and recorded nowhere.
`Nabil, born after Rahim died` MEANS
    `an ISA person` "Nabil" `living` (JUST (YMD 2025 1 1)) FALSE 0
        (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Zara`))
`case 17e — a brother conceived after the death, leaving a surviving child` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Nabil, born after Rahim died`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `the person survives the intestate and is within the classes section 6(a) names` `Nabil, born after Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
-- He is ALIVE, which is the whole of A21: rule 6's representation clause
-- reaches the children of a "deceased" brother, and he is not one.
#ASSERT `the person survives the intestate` `Nabil, born after Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
-- Zara herself is unimpeachable — a legitimate child, within s 6(a), alive —
-- so it is her father's status and nothing about her that keeps her out.
#ASSERT `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` (`an ISA legitimate child` `Zara`)
#ASSERT NOT null (`rule 6 — the children of a deceased brother or sister who take according to their stocks` (`of the whole blood,` `Nabil, born after Rahim died`) (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5)))
#ASSERT NOT `rule 6 — this brother or sister shares, or has children who take according to their stocks` (`of the whole blood,` `Nabil, born after Rahim died`) (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
#ASSERT null (`rule 6 — the stocks of brothers and sisters between which the estate is shared in equal portions` `case 17e — a brother conceived after the death, leaving a surviving child`)
#ASSERT `rule 9 — in default of distribution under rules 1 to 8` `case 17e — a brother conceived after the death, leaving a surviving child`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 17e — a brother conceived after the death, leaving a surviving child` EQUALS LIST "the Government takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 17e — a brother conceived after the death, leaving a surviving child` EQUALS 1

-- THE OTHER SIDE OF A21 — the same daughter, and a father rule 6 does reach.
-- Nabil predeceased Rahim on 2024-01-01, and Zara takes the share he would
-- have taken, in terms: she is "the child of any deceased brother or sister".
--
-- TWO FACTS DIFFER BETWEEN THE PAIR AND THEY CANNOT BE SEPARATED, which is
-- itself the sharpest thing that can be said about A21. A sibling outside
-- s 6(a) is necessarily born after the intestate's death, and a person born
-- after the intestate's death necessarily SURVIVES him — `the person survives
-- the intestate` compares the two dates of death, and he has none. So under
-- reading (i) the children of an s 6(a)-excluded sibling can NEVER represent
-- him, on any facts whatever; there is no minimal one-fact pair to build,
-- because the exclusion and the survivorship are the same fact seen twice.
-- Reading (ii) would be the only route by which such a child ever took.
`Nabil, who predeceased Rahim` MEANS
    `who died on` "Nabil" (YMD 2024 1 1) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Zara`))
`case 17g — a brother who predeceased, leaving the same surviving child` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Nabil, who predeceased Rahim`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `the person survives the intestate` `Nabil, who predeceased Rahim` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
#ASSERT `rule 6 — this brother or sister shares, or has children who take according to their stocks` (`of the whole blood,` `Nabil, who predeceased Rahim`) (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 17g — a brother who predeceased, leaving the same surviving child` EQUALS LIST "Zara takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 17g — a brother who predeceased, leaving the same surviving child` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 17f — s 6(a) among the CHILDREN of a deceased brother, under rule 6`

-- THE THIRD SITE OF THE SAME TEST, AND IT WAS UNTESTED TOO. `rule 6 — the
-- children of a deceased brother or sister who take according to their stocks`
-- conjoins s 6(a) on each child, and every niece and nephew in this file was
-- born long before the intestate, so the conjunct could be deleted with every
-- assertion in the file still green. Cases 17d and 17e close the OTHER two
-- sites of the test; this closes the one that decides how a stock is divided
-- among the representatives rather than whether a class takes at all.
--
-- Rahim died 2024-05-05, his brother Bakri having died on 2024-04-01. Nia was
-- conceived in Bakri's lifetime, was in the womb at Rahim's death, and was
-- born alive on 2024-12-01, so s 6(a)'s second class names her. Osman was
-- conceived after Rahim's death — posthumously, which is why this limb of
-- s 6(a) is not a museum piece — and is outside both classes. Bakri's stock is
-- the only stock and takes the whole; Nia takes all of it, and the stock is
-- NOT halved with Osman.
`Nia, in the womb when Rahim died` MEANS `an ISA person` "Nia" `living` (JUST (YMD 2024 12 1)) TRUE 0 EMPTY
`Osman, conceived after Rahim died` MEANS `an ISA person` "Osman" `living` (JUST (YMD 2026 3 1)) FALSE 0 EMPTY
`Bakri, who died a month before Rahim` MEANS
    `who died on` "Bakri" (YMD 2024 4 1)
        (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Nia, in the womb when Rahim died`),
              (`an ISA legitimate child` `Osman, conceived after Rahim died`))
`case 17f — a niece in the womb at the death, and a nephew conceived after it` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Bakri, who died a month before Rahim`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` `Nia, in the womb when Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
#ASSERT NOT `section 6(a) — actually born in his lifetime, or at the date of his death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` `Osman, conceived after Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
-- Both are alive and both are "children" within s 3, so s 6(a) is the only
-- thing that separates them.
#ASSERT `the person survives the intestate` `Osman, conceived after Rahim died` (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
#ASSERT `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` (`an ISA legitimate child` `Osman, conceived after Rahim died`)
#ASSERT count (`rule 6 — the children of a deceased brother or sister who take according to their stocks` (`of the whole blood,` `Bakri, who died a month before Rahim`) (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))) EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 17f — a niece in the womb at the death, and a nephew conceived after it` EQUALS LIST "Nia takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 17f — a niece in the womb at the death, and a nephew conceived after it` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 18 — s 3: "child" means a legitimate child and includes an adopted child`

-- Three people are called the deceased's children in the case file. Ivan is
-- legitimate; Jasmine was adopted by an order of court in Singapore; Kiran is
-- neither. s 3's definition admits the first two and not the third, so the
-- estate is halved and not divided three ways.
`Ivan` MEANS `living, childless` "Ivan"
`Jasmine` MEANS `living, childless` "Jasmine"
`Kiran` MEANS `living, childless` "Kiran"
`Leong` MEANS
    `who died on` "Leong" (YMD 2024 6 1)
        (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Ivan`),
          (`a child adopted by order of court in Singapore` `Jasmine`),
          (`a child neither legitimate nor adopted` `Kiran`))
`case 18 — a legitimate child, an adopted child and neither` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Leong` `no family in evidence`
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` (`an ISA legitimate child` `Ivan`)
#ASSERT     `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` (`a child adopted by order of court in Singapore` `Jasmine`)
#ASSERT NOT `section 3 — a “child” within the meaning of this Act` (`a child neither legitimate nor adopted` `Kiran`)
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 18 — a legitimate child, an adopted child and neither` EQUALS LIST "Ivan takes 0.5", "Jasmine takes 0.5"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 18 — a legitimate child, an adopted child and neither` EQUALS 1
-- s 9's "child" is s 3's "child" too, at every generation — AMBIGUITY A14. An
-- earlier draft cited s 3 on the s 9 walk and did not consult it, so Kiran was
-- not a "child" for the rule that would have paid him and was a "child" for
-- the rule cited to the definition.
#ASSERT     `section 9 — the distributive share is claimed by a child or any descendant of a child of that person` `Leong` `Ivan`
#ASSERT NOT `section 9 — the distributive share is claimed by a child or any descendant of a child of that person` `Leong` `Kiran`

§§§ `Case 19 — s 9: an advancement is not taken into account`

-- Mei's father settled $60,000 on her in his lifetime for her advancement. She
-- and her brother take one half each of a $1,000,000 fund. s 9 says no part of
-- that $60,000 "shall be taken into account in estimating such distributive
-- share", so her estimated share is $500,000 and not $440,000. The first
-- assertion pins the advancement standing at $60,000 in the record; the second
-- pins what s 9 takes into account, which is nil.
--
-- The last pair asks the same question about the widow. It answers NOTHING —
-- not nil — because s 9 speaks only of a claim "by a child or any descendant
-- of a child", and this Act states no rule of hotchpot for anybody else.
`Mei` MEANS `an ISA person` "Mei" `living` NOTHING FALSE 60_000 EMPTY
`Ng` MEANS `living, childless` "Ng"
`Poh` MEANS
    `who died on` "Poh" (YMD 2024 11 11)
        (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Mei`), (`an ISA legitimate child` `Ng`))
`case 19 — a child who received an advancement` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Poh` `no family in evidence`
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `Mei`'s `money or other property given, paid or settled by the intestate during his life to or for the advancement of the person` EQUALS 60_000
#ASSERT `section 9 — the distributive share is claimed by a child or any descendant of a child of that person` `Poh` `Mei`
#ASSERT `section 9 — the money or other property to be taken into account in estimating such distributive share` `Poh` `Mei` EQUALS JUST 0
#ASSERT `section 9 — the distributive share of a child or a descendant of a child, estimated` `Poh` `Mei` (1 DIVIDED BY 2) (`the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 19 — a child who received an advancement`) EQUALS JUST 500_000
#ASSERT NOT `section 9 — the distributive share is claimed by a child or any descendant of a child of that person` `Poh` `Beatrice`
#ASSERT `section 9 — the money or other property to be taken into account in estimating such distributive share` `Poh` `Beatrice` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 19 — a child who received an advancement` EQUALS LIST "Mei takes 0.5", "Ng takes 0.5"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 19 — a child who received an advancement` EQUALS 1
-- A descendant of a child is within s 9 as well: Jia claims through Ismail
-- through Gopal, three generations down from Elias.
#ASSERT `section 9 — the distributive share is claimed by a child or any descendant of a child of that person` `Elias` `Jia`
#ASSERT NOT `section 9 — the distributive share is claimed by a child or any descendant of a child of that person` `Elias` `Marcus`

§§§ `Case 20 — AMBIGUITY A4: s 4's reach against s 5's fund`

-- An estate with $400,000 of IMMOVABLE property and $250,000 of MOVABLE
-- property situated OUTSIDE Singapore. Aaron was domiciled in Singapore, so
-- s 4(1) refers the movables wherever situated to Singapore law, and s 4(2)
-- claims the immovables "wherever he may have been domiciled"; s 5, the
-- section that says what is actually distributed, reaches only property
-- "situated in Singapore" in either limb. BOTH LEGS OF THE FORK ARE NOW
-- EXERCISED — an earlier version of this case left the movable-outside cell at
-- nought, so the movables half of AMBIGUITY A4 was encoded and never run.
-- The figures are the fork, shown rather than reconciled: $1,850,000 under
-- s 4 (900,000 + 250,000 movable, 300,000 + 400,000 immovable) against
-- $1,200,000 of Singapore property under s 5, from which the $200,000 of
-- expenses then come, leaving a fund of $1,000,000.
`case 20 — an estate with movable and immovable property outside Singapore` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 250_000 400_000 200_000 0 0 0)
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `section 4 — the value of the property whose distribution is regulated by this Act` `case 20 — an estate with movable and immovable property outside Singapore` EQUALS 1_850_000
#ASSERT `section 5 — that property` `case 20 — an estate with movable and immovable property outside Singapore` EQUALS 1_200_000
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 20 — an estate with movable and immovable property outside Singapore` EQUALS 1_000_000

-- THE OTHER SIDE OF s 4(1): the same estate, but domiciled abroad. The
-- movables are then referred out to the law of that country and s 4's reach
-- under this Act is the immovables alone — $700,000, not $1,850,000.
`case 20a — the same estate, domiciled outside Singapore` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 250_000 400_000 200_000 0 0 0)
        NOTHING FALSE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `section 4(1) — the law of the country of domicile is the law of Singapore` `case 20a — the same estate, domiciled outside Singapore`
#ASSERT `section 4(2) — the distribution of the immovable property is regulated by this Act`
#ASSERT `section 4 — the value of the property whose distribution is regulated by this Act` `case 20a — the same estate, domiciled outside Singapore` EQUALS 700_000

§§§ `Case 21 — AMBIGUITY A5: a death with no date in evidence`

-- The ontology's `Vital status` distinguishes "dead, the date of death not
-- known" from "living", and this module rules that an undated death cannot
-- satisfy s 5's "dies intestate after 2 June 1967". Nothing is distributed,
-- and — again — rule 9 does not fire, so the answer reads as a failure of
-- proof rather than as an escheat to the Government.
`Qamar` MEANS
    `an ISA person` "Qamar" `dead, the date of death not known` NOTHING FALSE 0
        (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Clara`))
`case 21 — a death with no date in evidence` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Qamar`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `the date of the death of the intestate` `case 21 — a death with no date in evidence` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT NOT `section 5 — the person dies intestate after 2 June 1967` `case 21 — a death with no date in evidence`
-- The commencement gate is the SOLE cause: limb (a) is satisfied and s 2 does
-- not bite.
#ASSERT `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `case 21 — a death with no date in evidence`
#ASSERT `this Act applies to the estate` `case 21 — a death with no date in evidence`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 9 — in default of distribution under rules 1 to 8` `case 21 — a death with no date in evidence`
#ASSERT null (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `case 21 — a death with no date in evidence`)
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 21 — a death with no date in evidence` EQUALS 0

§§§ `Case 22 — s 10: the will's dispositions against the estate's recorded figure`

-- s 10 gives this Act "the part of his property not so disposed of", and the
-- word "so" points back at the dispositions that answered the antecedent. An
-- earlier draft read the KIND of disposition off the will and the QUANTITY off
-- the estate and never compared them, so a case file could hold at once that
-- the will disposed of part and that the fund was the whole — distributing the
-- same $400,000 legacy twice.
--
-- Case 22 is that file: case 15's will, with the estate's disposed-value cell
-- recorded as nought. The quantity now comes from the dispositions, so the
-- fund is $600,000 and not $1,000,000; and the divergence is reported rather
-- than silently resolved.
`case 22 — the estate's disposed value contradicts the will` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 0 200_000 0 0 0)
        (JUST `a will giving Beatrice a pecuniary legacy`)
        TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `section 10 — the person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property` `case 22 — the estate's disposed value contradicts the will`
#ASSERT NOT `the value beneficially disposed of by the will recorded in the estate agrees with the dispositions made by the will` `case 22 — the estate's disposed value contradicts the will`
#ASSERT `section 10 — the value of the part of his property so disposed of` `case 22 — the estate's disposed value contradicts the will` EQUALS 400_000
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 22 — the estate's disposed value contradicts the will` EQUALS 600_000

-- The mirror: a will that beneficially disposes of NOTHING, beside an estate
-- recording $400,000 as disposed of. s 10's antecedent fails — there is no
-- "part" beneficially disposed of — so this Act reaches the whole fund, and
-- the s 10 proviso is not engaged because s 10 is not.
`a will disposing of nothing` MEANS `a duly executed will` `Aaron` EMPTY FALSE
`case 22a — a will that disposes of nothing, beside a recorded disposed value` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 0 200_000 400_000 0 0)
        (JUST `a will disposing of nothing`)
        TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `the will makes some beneficial disposition` `a will disposing of nothing`
#ASSERT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `case 22a — a will that disposes of nothing, beside a recorded disposed value`
#ASSERT NOT `section 10 — the person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property` `case 22a — a will that disposes of nothing, beside a recorded disposed value`
#ASSERT NOT `the value beneficially disposed of by the will recorded in the estate agrees with the dispositions made by the will` `case 22a — a will that disposes of nothing, beside a recorded disposed value`
#ASSERT `section 10 — the value of the part of his property so disposed of` `case 22a — a will that disposes of nothing, beside a recorded disposed value` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 22a — a will that disposes of nothing, beside a recorded disposed value` EQUALS 1_000_000
#ASSERT NOT `section 10 proviso — the personal representative is a trustee for the persons entitled under this Act in respect of the part of the estate not expressly disposed of` `case 22a — a will that disposes of nothing, beside a recorded disposed value`

§§§ `Case 23 — s 5 deducts the expenses and not the debts`

-- s 5 takes the property "after payment thereout of the expenses of due
-- administration as prescribed by the Probate and Administration Act 1934",
-- and expenses are the only thing it names. So an estate with $5,000,000 of
-- debts and $1,200,000 of property still yields a fund of $1,000,000 under
-- this Act, and the shares are shares of that. Whether the debts are paid
-- first is the Probate and Administration Act's question, under s 57 and its
-- Schedules, and the closing section says so. The consequence is asserted here
-- so that a reader can see it rather than infer it from a silence.
`case 23 — an estate with debts far exceeding the property` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 0 200_000 0 0 5_000_000)
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 23 — an estate with debts far exceeding the property` EQUALS 1_000_000
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 23 — an estate with debts far exceeding the property` EQUALS LIST "Beatrice takes 0.5", "Clara takes 0.25", "Daniel takes 0.25"

-- AMBIGUITY A7 from the side the Act does contemplate: EXPENSES exceeding the
-- property. The fund is floored at nil rather than made negative — and note
-- exactly where that bites. The GATE still opens, because there IS property
-- possessed beneficially in Singapore; it is the FUND that is nil, and the
-- takers take their fractions of nothing. A share of a negative estate is not
-- a thing the Act creates, and the creditors' shortfall is the Probate and
-- Administration Act's business under its First Schedule.
`case 23a — expenses exceeding the property` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 0 3_000_000 0 0 0)
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `case 23a — expenses exceeding the property`
#ASSERT `section 5 — the property to be distributed, after payment thereout of the expenses of due administration` `case 23a — expenses exceeding the property` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 23a — expenses exceeding the property` EQUALS 0
-- The fractions are still the fractions; they are fractions of nothing.
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 23a — expenses exceeding the property` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 24 — s 5: "possessed beneficially", and the property held as trustee`

-- s 5 uses the word "beneficially" in both of its limbs and again in its
-- operative words. An earlier draft read a GROSS value cell and never
-- subtracted the property the deceased held for somebody else, so an estate
-- whose whole Singapore content was trust property passed the gate and was
-- distributed to the intestate's family: $900,000 of somebody else's money,
-- halved between a widow and two children. That is case 24.
--
-- The subtrahend is `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or
-- was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore`, and
-- it is the last argument to `an ISA estate` below.
`case 24 — the whole Singapore estate held as trustee for another` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 0 0 0 0 0 900_000 0)
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `the property held as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore` `case 24 — the whole Singapore estate held as trustee for another` EQUALS 900_000
#ASSERT `the property situated in Singapore, whether movable or immovable, or both, possessed beneficially` `case 24 — the whole Singapore estate held as trustee for another` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT NOT `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `case 24 — the whole Singapore estate held as trustee for another`
#ASSERT NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `case 24 — the whole Singapore estate held as trustee for another`
#ASSERT null (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `case 24 — the whole Singapore estate held as trustee for another`)
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 24 — the whole Singapore estate held as trustee for another` EQUALS 0

-- Part, not all: $1,200,000 in Singapore of which $400,000 is held as trustee,
-- $200,000 of expenses. The gate opens on the $800,000 he held beneficially
-- and the fund is $600,000 — the trustee property is out of the fund as well
-- as out of the gate, because "that property" in s 5 is the property the limb
-- found him possessed of beneficially.
`case 24a — part of the Singapore estate held as trustee` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 300_000 0 0 200_000 0 400_000 0)
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `the property situated in Singapore, whether movable or immovable, or both, possessed beneficially` `case 24a — part of the Singapore estate held as trustee` EQUALS 800_000
#ASSERT `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `case 24a — part of the Singapore estate held as trustee`
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 24a — part of the Singapore estate held as trustee` EQUALS 600_000
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 24a — part of the Singapore estate held as trustee` EQUALS LIST "Beatrice takes 0.5", "Clara takes 0.25", "Daniel takes 0.25"
-- s 4 against s 5 on the same estate. s 4 says "the movable property of a
-- person deceased" and never says "beneficially", so its figure is GROSS —
-- $1,200,000 — while s 5's is NET of the $400,000 held as trustee. The two
-- sections are quoted as they stand and the gap between them is asserted.
#ASSERT `section 4 — the value of the property whose distribution is regulated by this Act` `case 24a — part of the Singapore estate held as trustee` EQUALS 1_200_000
#ASSERT `section 5 — that property` `case 24a — part of the Singapore estate held as trustee` EQUALS 800_000

-- s 5(b) and AMBIGUITY A17: the whole Singapore trustee figure comes off the
-- immovable cell, because the ontology cannot say how much of it is land. That
-- coarseness can drive the subtraction NEGATIVE on a perfectly consistent case
-- file, and case 24b is such a file: $400,000 of Singapore movables, $500,000
-- of Singapore land and $700,000 held as trustee — the trustee property being
-- partly movable and partly land, which is exactly what the ontology cannot
-- record. Limb (b) subtracts the whole $700,000 from the $500,000 of land, and
-- the result is floored at nil rather than carried to minus $200,000. Nothing
-- beneficial, and limb (b) is not satisfied.
--
-- THE FLOOR IS WHAT CASE 24b TESTS, and an earlier version of it could not:
-- it set the trustee figure exactly equal to the land ($500,000 against
-- $500,000), so the subtraction landed ON nil and never went below it, and
-- `max 0` could be deleted with every assertion in this file still green. Case
-- 24c, unchanged, is the other side — reduce the trustee figure to $400,000
-- and limb (b) is satisfied on the $100,000 that remains — so the boundary of
-- "possessed beneficially" read as a positive net value (AMBIGUITY A6) is
-- still asserted from both sides.
`case 24b — domiciled abroad, the Singapore trustee figure exceeding the Singapore land` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Dinesh` `no family in evidence`
        (`an ISA estate` 400_000 500_000 0 0 200_000 0 700_000 0)
        NOTHING FALSE FALSE
`case 24c — domiciled abroad, part of the Singapore land held as trustee` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Dinesh` `no family in evidence`
        (`an ISA estate` 0 500_000 0 0 200_000 0 400_000 0)
        NOTHING FALSE FALSE

#ASSERT `the immovable property situated in Singapore, possessed beneficially` `case 24b — domiciled abroad, the Singapore trustee figure exceeding the Singapore land` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT NOT `section 5(b) — domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore` `case 24b — domiciled abroad, the Singapore trustee figure exceeding the Singapore land`
#ASSERT null (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `case 24b — domiciled abroad, the Singapore trustee figure exceeding the Singapore land`)
#ASSERT `the immovable property situated in Singapore, possessed beneficially` `case 24c — domiciled abroad, part of the Singapore land held as trustee` EQUALS 100_000
#ASSERT `section 5(b) — domiciled outside Singapore and possessed beneficially of immovable property situated in Singapore` `case 24c — domiciled abroad, part of the Singapore land held as trustee`
-- $100,000 of beneficial land less $200,000 of expenses is floored at nil
-- (AMBIGUITY A7), so the gate opens and the fund is nothing.
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 24c — domiciled abroad, part of the Singapore land held as trustee` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 24c — domiciled abroad, part of the Singapore land held as trustee` EQUALS LIST "Clara takes 1"

-- Case 24d — A MALFORMED CASE FILE, AND IT IS HERE SAYING SO. The trustee cell
-- is an exclusion drawn out of the same Singapore asset base as the movable
-- and immovable cells, so a well-formed record cannot make it larger than
-- their sum; this one does, recording $900,000 of Singapore movables and
-- $1,000,000 held as trustee in Singapore. The floor on limb (a) is what
-- decides the answer for such a file — nil, not minus $100,000 — and this is
-- the only assertion in the module that can distinguish `max 0` from `id`
-- there. It is not a scenario the Act contemplates and it is not offered as
-- one; every other fixture in this file is a possible state of affairs.
`case 24d — a case file whose trustee figure exceeds the property it is drawn from` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        (`an ISA estate` 900_000 0 0 0 0 0 1_000_000 0)
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `the property held as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore` `case 24d — a case file whose trustee figure exceeds the property it is drawn from` EQUALS 1_000_000
#ASSERT `the property situated in Singapore, whether movable or immovable, or both, possessed beneficially` `case 24d — a case file whose trustee figure exceeds the property it is drawn from` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT NOT `section 5(a) — domiciled in Singapore and possessed beneficially of property situated in Singapore` `case 24d — a case file whose trustee figure exceeds the property it is drawn from`
#ASSERT `section 5 — that property` `case 24d — a case file whose trustee figure exceeds the property it is drawn from` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT NOT `this Act has effect in respect of the estate` `case 24d — a case file whose trustee figure exceeds the property it is drawn from`

§§§ `Case 25 — AMBIGUITY A15: only the grandchildren of a deceased brother survive`

-- Rule 6 lets "the children of any deceased brother or sister" take that
-- sibling's stock and stops there; rule 3's Proviso No. (2) is the provision
-- that reaches "the remotest degree", and rule 6 does not import it. The
-- module's own note at AMBIGUITY A15 states the consequence rather than hiding
-- it: where every brother and sister is dead and only their grandchildren
-- survive, rule 6's class is empty and the estate falls through to rule 7.
-- This case checks that the stated consequence is what actually happens.
--
-- Tan (Rahim's sister) predeceased. Her only child Uma also predeceased,
-- leaving Zara, who survives. Zara is Rahim's grand-niece: rule 6 names
-- children, not descendants, so she takes nothing and the grandparent takes
-- the whole under rule 7.
`Zara` MEANS `living, childless` "Zara"
`Uma, who also predeceased` MEANS
    `who died on` "Uma" (YMD 2022 2 2) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Zara`))
`Tan, whose only child predeceased her` MEANS
    `who died on` "Tan" (YMD 2021 6 6) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Uma, who also predeceased`))
`case 25 — only a grand-niece survives, and a grandparent` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Tan, whose only child predeceased her`))
                         (LIST `Xin`) EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT null (`rule 6 — the children of a deceased brother or sister who take according to their stocks` (`of the whole blood,` `Tan, whose only child predeceased her`) (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5)))
#ASSERT NOT `rule 6 — this brother or sister shares, or has children who take according to their stocks` (`of the whole blood,` `Tan, whose only child predeceased her`) (JUST (YMD 2024 5 5))
#ASSERT null (`rule 6 — the stocks of brothers and sisters between which the estate is shared in equal portions` `case 25 — only a grand-niece survives, and a grandparent`)
#ASSERT `rule 7 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate` `case 25 — only a grand-niece survives, and a grandparent`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 25 — only a grand-niece survives, and a grandparent` EQUALS LIST "Xin takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 25 — only a grand-niece survives, and a grandparent` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 26 — AMBIGUITY A14: s 3's "child" at the SECOND generation of a descent`

-- Proviso No. (1) says the persons who legally represent the children of an
-- intestate "are their descendants and not their next-of-kin", and A14 rules
-- that s 3's definition of "child" is imported at every generation. So an
-- illegitimate, unadopted link BREAKS THE CHAIN: the predeceased child has no
-- representative, is not a stock at all (AMBIGUITY A13), and where he is the
-- only child the intestate leaves no issue.
--
-- Meng died leaving one child, Chandra, who predeceased him leaving one child,
-- Devi, who is neither legitimate nor adopted. Devi does not represent
-- Chandra; there is no issue; and with no spouse, no parents and no siblings
-- the estate falls to rule 7 and the surviving grandparent.
`the ISA Devi` MEANS `living, childless` "Devi"
`the ISA Chandra` MEANS
    `who died on` "Chandra" (YMD 2022 3 3)
        (LIST (`a child neither legitimate nor adopted` `the ISA Devi`))
`Meng` MEANS
    `who died on` "Meng" (YMD 2024 7 7) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `the ISA Chandra`))
`case 26 — the only grandchild is neither legitimate nor adopted` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Meng`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY (LIST `Xin`) EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `rule 3 — the ancestor leaves a child who takes, or who is himself represented` `the ISA Chandra` (JUST (YMD 2024 7 7))
#ASSERT NOT `rule 3 — this child is a stock which takes, in his own right or through those who legally represent him` (`an ISA legitimate child` `the ISA Chandra`) (JUST (YMD 2024 7 7))
#ASSERT NOT `the intestate leaves issue` `case 26 — the only grandchild is neither legitimate nor adopted`
#ASSERT `rule 7 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate` `case 26 — the only grandchild is neither legitimate nor adopted`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 26 — the only grandchild is neither legitimate nor adopted` EQUALS LIST "Xin takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 26 — the only grandchild is neither legitimate nor adopted` EQUALS 1

-- The other side of the same link: make Devi a legitimate child of Chandra and
-- the chain holds, so Chandra IS a stock, Devi stands in his place under
-- Proviso No. (2), and she takes the whole.
`Chandra, whose child is legitimate` MEANS
    `who died on` "Chandra" (YMD 2022 3 3) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `the ISA Devi`))
`Meng, whose grandchild is legitimate` MEANS
    `who died on` "Meng" (YMD 2024 7 7) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Chandra, whose child is legitimate`))
`case 26a — the only grandchild is legitimate` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Meng, whose grandchild is legitimate`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY (LIST `Xin`) EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `the intestate leaves issue` `case 26a — the only grandchild is legitimate`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 7 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate` `case 26a — the only grandchild is legitimate`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 26a — the only grandchild is legitimate` EQUALS LIST "Devi takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 26a — the only grandchild is legitimate` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 27 — AMBIGUITY A13 feeding rule 5: a childless predeceased child is not a descendant`

-- A child who predeceased leaving no surviving descendant is not a stock, so
-- an intestate whose only child died childless "leaves issue" for no rule and
-- there "are no descendants" for rule 5. The surviving parent therefore takes
-- the estate. This is the join between A13 and A3 asserted rather than argued:
-- if the two questions were answered by different machinery, an estate could
-- be kept from the parents by a child who takes nothing.
`Rani` MEANS `who died on` "Rani" (YMD 2020 1 1) EMPTY
`Selva` MEANS `who died on` "Selva" (YMD 2024 4 1) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Rani`))
`Selva's mother` MEANS `living, childless` "Selva's mother"
`case 27 — an only child predeceased childless, and a parent survives` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Selva`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING (JUST `Selva's mother`) EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `the intestate leaves issue` `case 27 — an only child predeceased childless, and a parent survives`
#ASSERT NOT `there are descendants` `case 27 — an only child predeceased childless, and a parent survives`
#ASSERT `rule 5 — there are no descendants, and there are a parent or parents of the intestate` `case 27 — an only child predeceased childless, and a parent survives`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 27 — an only child predeceased childless, and a parent survives` EQUALS LIST "Selva's mother takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 27 — an only child predeceased childless, and a parent survives` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 28 — s 3 feeding rules 1 and 2: an only child who is not a "child"`

-- The person the case file calls a child is neither legitimate nor adopted, so
-- he is not a "child" within s 3, the intestate leaves no issue, and rule 2
-- does not apply. With no parent either, rule 1 gives the surviving spouse the
-- WHOLE of the estate rather than one half. The whole-estate swing turns on
-- s 3 alone.
`Tessa` MEANS `living, childless` "Tessa"
`Umar` MEANS
    `who died on` "Umar" (YMD 2024 5 20)
        (LIST (`a child neither legitimate nor adopted` `Tessa`))
`case 28 — an only child who is not a "child" within section 3` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Umar`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Marcus`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `the intestate leaves issue` `case 28 — an only child who is not a "child" within section 3`
#ASSERT     `rule 1 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` `case 28 — an only child who is not a "child" within section 3`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 2 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and issue` `case 28 — an only child who is not a "child" within section 3`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 28 — an only child who is not a "child" within section 3` EQUALS LIST "Marcus takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 28 — an only child who is not a "child" within section 3` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 29 — "surviving spouse": a marriage dissolved before the death`

-- s 7 rules 1, 2 and 4 speak of "a surviving spouse", and s 8 of a wife
-- "surviving him". A marriage dissolved or annulled before the death is not
-- one of them, however much alive the former spouse is. So a deceased leaving
-- one subsisting marriage and one dissolved marriage leaves ONE surviving
-- spouse: s 8 does not bite, and the sole spouse takes the undivided share.
GIVEN `the husband or wife` IS A Person
GIVETH A Marriage
`a marriage dissolved before the death, to` MEANS Marriage WITH
    `the spouse` IS `the husband or wife`
    `the date of the solemnisation of the marriage` IS NOTHING
    `subsisting at the death of the deceased, in the sense of not dissolved or annulled before the death` IS FALSE

`case 29 — one subsisting marriage and one dissolved` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Tariq`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Halimah`),
                               (`a marriage dissolved before the death, to` `Sofia`))
                         NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT count (`the spouses surviving the intestate` `case 29 — one subsisting marriage and one dissolved`) EQUALS 1
#ASSERT NOT `section 8 — the intestate leaves surviving him more than one wife` `case 29 — one subsisting marriage and one dissolved`
#ASSERT `the intestate leaves a surviving spouse` `case 29 — one subsisting marriage and one dissolved`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 29 — one subsisting marriage and one dissolved` EQUALS LIST "Halimah takes 0.5", "Idris takes 0.5"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 29 — one subsisting marriage and one dissolved` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 30 — rule 3 with three stocks: "by equal portions per stirpes"`

-- Three surviving children, one third each. L4's arithmetic is exact rational,
-- so the three thirds sum to exactly 1 even though each of them PRINTS as a
-- rounded decimal — which is why the sum is asserted on the fraction and the
-- read-out is asserted on the string the display function produces.
`Wen` MEANS `living, childless` "Wen"
`Xiu` MEANS `living, childless` "Xiu"
`Yan` MEANS `living, childless` "Yan"
`Zhou` MEANS
    `who died on` "Zhou" (YMD 2024 12 1)
        (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Wen`), (`an ISA legitimate child` `Xiu`), (`an ISA legitimate child` `Yan`))
`case 30 — three surviving children and no spouse` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Zhou` `no family in evidence`
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT count (`the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their shares` `case 30 — three surviving children and no spouse`) EQUALS 3
#ASSERT `rule 3 — the share of the estate distributed among the issue, subject to the rights of the surviving spouse` `case 30 — three surviving children and no spouse` EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 30 — three surviving children and no spouse` EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 30 — three surviving children and no spouse` EQUALS LIST "Wen takes 0.3333333333333333", "Xiu takes 0.3333333333333333", "Yan takes 0.3333333333333333"

§§§ `Case 31 — AMBIGUITY A12: s 6(b) ranks the STOCKS, not the individual takers`

-- s 6(b) ranks persons "related to him in the same degree", and rule 6 puts
-- brothers and sisters (second degree) beside the children of deceased
-- brothers and sisters (third degree) in one distribution. A12 takes the
-- ranking to bite on the CLASS OF SIBLING STOCKS, and this is where the two
-- readings actually part company.
--
-- Ravi is the surviving child of Puan, a sister of the WHOLE blood who
-- predeceased. Wati is a surviving sister of the HALF blood. On the reading
-- taken, Puan's stock is entitled and Wati's is not, so Ravi — a third-degree
-- relative — takes the whole against a second-degree half-blood sister. On the
-- rejected reading Wati would outrank him.
`Ravi` MEANS `living, childless` "Ravi"
`Puan, a sister of the whole blood who predeceased` MEANS
    `who died on` "Puan" (YMD 2021 1 1) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Ravi`))
`case 31 — a deceased whole-blood sister's child against a living half-blood sister` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Puan, a sister of the whole blood who predeceased`),
                               (`of the half blood,` `Wati`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT count (`rule 6 — the stocks of brothers and sisters between which the estate is shared in equal portions` `case 31 — a deceased whole-blood sister's child against a living half-blood sister`) EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 31 — a deceased whole-blood sister's child against a living half-blood sister` EQUALS LIST "Ravi takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 31 — a deceased whole-blood sister's child against a living half-blood sister` EQUALS 1

-- The mirror, and the point of the ranking: reverse the blood ties and the
-- half-blood stock is excluded whole, its children with it. Siti's child takes
-- nothing while Salim, a surviving brother of the whole blood, is entitled.
`Siti, a sister of the half blood who predeceased` MEANS
    `who died on` "Siti" (YMD 2021 1 1) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `Ravi`))
`case 31a — a deceased half-blood sister's child against a living whole-blood brother` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the half blood,` `Siti, a sister of the half blood who predeceased`),
                               (`of the whole blood,` `Salim`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 31a — a deceased half-blood sister's child against a living whole-blood brother` EQUALS LIST "Salim takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 31a — a deceased half-blood sister's child against a living whole-blood brother` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 32 — s 6(b) under rule 8: uncles and aunts of the half blood alone`

-- s 6(b)'s ranking bites at the third degree exactly as it does at the second:
-- where no uncle or aunt of the whole blood is entitled, those of the half
-- blood take in full. Case 9 is the same rule from the other side.
`case 32 — only uncles and aunts of the half blood` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY
                         (LIST (`of the half blood,` `the ISA Bala`)))
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT `rule 8 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate` `case 32 — only uncles and aunts of the half blood`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 32 — only uncles and aunts of the half blood` EQUALS LIST "Bala takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 32 — only uncles and aunts of the half blood` EQUALS 1

§§§ `Case 33 — s 3 and s 10: a disposition that is not beneficial`

-- Both s 3's "beneficially disposing" and s 10's turn on a BENEFICIAL
-- disposition, so the `beneficial` flag has to be tested beside the kind. A
-- universal gift that is not beneficial answers neither: the will disposes of
-- no part, the deceased is an "intestate" as to the whole, and s 10 is not
-- engaged.
--
-- The Act's own example of a non-beneficial provision is the one the Wills Act
-- excepts: "charges and directions for the payment of any debt".
`a universal gift that is not beneficial` MEANS Disposition WITH
    `the donee`                    IS `an individual` `Beatrice`
    `the kind of disposition`      IS `a universal gift`
    `the value of the disposition` IS 1_000_000
    `beneficial`                   IS FALSE
    `not determinable at or before the death of the donee` IS TRUE

`a will containing only a non-beneficial universal gift` MEANS
    `a duly executed will` `Aaron` (LIST `a universal gift that is not beneficial`) FALSE

`case 33 — a will whose only disposition is not beneficial` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `Aaron`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Beatrice`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        (JUST `a will containing only a non-beneficial universal gift`)
        TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `a universal or residuary beneficial gift` `a universal gift that is not beneficial`
#ASSERT NOT `the will beneficially disposes of the whole of his property` `a will containing only a non-beneficial universal gift`
#ASSERT NOT `the will makes some beneficial disposition` `a will containing only a non-beneficial universal gift`
#ASSERT `section 3 — an “intestate” within the meaning of this Act` `case 33 — a will whose only disposition is not beneficial`
#ASSERT NOT `section 10 — the person dies leaving a will beneficially disposing of part of his property` `case 33 — a will whose only disposition is not beneficial`
#ASSERT `section 10 — the value of the part of his property so disposed of` `case 33 — a will whose only disposition is not beneficial` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 33 — a will whose only disposition is not beneficial` EQUALS 1_000_000
#ASSERT NOT `section 10 proviso — the personal representative is a trustee for the persons entitled under this Act in respect of the part of the estate not expressly disposed of` `case 33 — a will whose only disposition is not beneficial`


§§§ `Cases 34 to 39 — the s 7 exclusion ladder, one rung at a time`

-- THE ARCHITECTURE OF s 7 IS EXCLUSION, AND IT HAD NO TEST. Rules 6, 7 and 8
-- each open by naming every class ahead of them — rule 6 "If there are no
-- surviving spouse, descendants or parents", rule 7 "If there are no surviving
-- spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such
-- brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate", rule 8 "If there
-- are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their
-- children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate" — so a
-- nearer class shuts a remoter one out, and the whole of the Act's order of
-- succession is carried by those chapeaux.
--
-- NOT ONE FIXTURE IN THIS FILE PUT TWO CLASSES SIDE BY SIDE. Every case that
-- reached rule 6 had no spouse, no parent, no grandparent and no uncle; every
-- case that reached rule 7 had no sibling; every case that reached rule 8 had
-- no grandparent. The rules were all CORRECT — the answers below were right
-- before these cases were written — but four separate one-line defects each
-- caused the estate to be distributed TWICE, with `the total of the shares
-- distributed` reading 2, and all of the module's assertions stayed green
-- through every one of them. A sum-check that is described as "the check that
-- the nine rules together exhaust the estate" and never sees two rungs
-- competing is not that check.
--
-- Each case below differs from a case that already exists by ONE line of
-- family record, and on each the negative assertion turns on exactly one
-- conjunct: the fixture satisfies every other limb of the rule being excluded,
-- so deleting that conjunct makes the assertion fail rather than leaving it
-- green for a second reason.

-- CASE 34 — rule 1 (a spouse) shuts out rule 6 (the siblings). Rule 6's
-- "descendants" and "or parents" limbs are both satisfied here, so "no
-- surviving spouse" is the only thing keeping the two brothers out.
`case 34 — a surviving spouse beside two brothers` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Marcus`)) NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Salim`), (`of the whole blood,` `Wati`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `rule 1 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` `case 34 — a surviving spouse beside two brothers`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 6 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants or parents` `case 34 — a surviving spouse beside two brothers`
-- The brothers are there and would take but for the chapeau: two stocks stand
-- ready, and rule 6 pays nobody.
#ASSERT count (`rule 6 — the stocks of brothers and sisters between which the estate is shared in equal portions` `case 34 — a surviving spouse beside two brothers`) EQUALS 2
#ASSERT null (`rule 6 — the entitlements of the brothers and sisters and the children of deceased brothers or sisters` `case 34 — a surviving spouse beside two brothers`)
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 34 — a surviving spouse beside two brothers` EQUALS LIST "Marcus takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 34 — a surviving spouse beside two brothers` EQUALS 1

-- CASE 35 — rule 5 (a surviving parent) shuts out rule 6. Rule 6's "no
-- surviving spouse" and "descendants" limbs are both satisfied, so "or
-- parents" is the only thing keeping the two brothers out. Rule 5 takes the
-- whole, there being no spouse to be subject to under rule 4.
`Rahim's mother` MEANS `living, childless` "Rahim's mother"
`case 35 — a surviving parent beside two brothers` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING (JUST `Rahim's mother`)
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Salim`), (`of the whole blood,` `Wati`))
                         EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `rule 5 — there are no descendants, and there are a parent or parents of the intestate` `case 35 — a surviving parent beside two brothers`
#ASSERT NOT `rule 6 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants or parents` `case 35 — a surviving parent beside two brothers`
#ASSERT count (`rule 6 — the stocks of brothers and sisters between which the estate is shared in equal portions` `case 35 — a surviving parent beside two brothers`) EQUALS 2
#ASSERT null (`rule 6 — the entitlements of the brothers and sisters and the children of deceased brothers or sisters` `case 35 — a surviving parent beside two brothers`)
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 35 — a surviving parent beside two brothers` EQUALS LIST "Rahim's mother takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 35 — a surviving parent beside two brothers` EQUALS 1

-- CASE 36 — rule 6 (a sibling stock) shuts out rule 7 (the grandparents).
-- Rule 7's chapeau is satisfied and a grandparent survives, so "brothers and
-- sisters or children of such brothers and sisters" is the only thing keeping
-- rule 7 out. This is also the fixture case 6's `NOT rule 7` could not be:
-- there, no grandparent survived either.
`case 36 — a surviving brother beside a grandparent` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Salim`))
                         (LIST `Xin`) EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `rule 6 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants or parents` `case 36 — a surviving brother beside a grandparent`
#ASSERT NOT null (`the grandparents of the intestate who survive him` `case 36 — a surviving brother beside a grandparent`)
#ASSERT NOT `rule 7 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate` `case 36 — a surviving brother beside a grandparent`
#ASSERT null (`rule 7 — the entitlements of the grandparents` `case 36 — a surviving brother beside a grandparent`)
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 36 — a surviving brother beside a grandparent` EQUALS LIST "Salim takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 36 — a surviving brother beside a grandparent` EQUALS 1

-- CASE 37 — rule 7 (the grandparents) shuts out rule 8 (the uncles and aunts).
-- Rule 8's chapeau and its sibling limb are satisfied and an uncle survives,
-- so "or grandparents" is the only thing keeping rule 8 out. This is the
-- fixture case 8's `NOT rule 8` could not be: there, no uncle survived either.
`case 37 — a surviving grandparent beside an uncle` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY
                         (LIST `Xin`)
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `the ISA Zainab`)))
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `rule 7 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or children of such brothers and sisters but grandparents of the intestate` `case 37 — a surviving grandparent beside an uncle`
#ASSERT NOT null (`the uncles and aunts of the intestate who survive him` `case 37 — a surviving grandparent beside an uncle`)
#ASSERT NOT `rule 8 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate` `case 37 — a surviving grandparent beside an uncle`
#ASSERT null (`rule 8 — the entitlements of the uncles and aunts` `case 37 — a surviving grandparent beside an uncle`)
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 37 — a surviving grandparent beside an uncle` EQUALS LIST "Xin takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 37 — a surviving grandparent beside an uncle` EQUALS 1

-- CASE 38 — rule 1 (a spouse) shuts out rule 8, two rungs down. Rule 8's
-- sibling limb, its grandparent limb and its own class limb are all satisfied,
-- so the chapeau it borrows from rule 6 is the only thing keeping the uncle
-- out. Rule 8's chapeau is written as a conjunct in its own right and could be
-- deleted with every other assertion in this file still green.
`case 38 — a surviving spouse beside an uncle` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `Marcus`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `the ISA Zainab`)))
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `rule 1 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent` `case 38 — a surviving spouse beside an uncle`
#ASSERT NOT null (`the uncles and aunts of the intestate who survive him` `case 38 — a surviving spouse beside an uncle`)
#ASSERT NOT `rule 8 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate` `case 38 — a surviving spouse beside an uncle`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 38 — a surviving spouse beside an uncle` EQUALS LIST "Marcus takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 38 — a surviving spouse beside an uncle` EQUALS 1

-- CASE 39 — rule 6 (a sibling stock) shuts out rule 8, skipping rule 7. Rule
-- 8's chapeau, its grandparent limb and its own class limb are all satisfied,
-- so "brothers and sisters or their children" is the only thing keeping the
-- uncle out. Rule 8 states that limb separately from rule 7's, and it needs
-- its own fixture for the same reason.
`case 39 — a surviving brother beside an uncle` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Rahim`
        (`an ISA family` EMPTY NOTHING NOTHING
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `Salim`))
                         EMPTY
                         (LIST (`of the whole blood,` `the ISA Zainab`)))
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

#ASSERT     `rule 6 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants or parents` `case 39 — a surviving brother beside an uncle`
#ASSERT null (`the grandparents of the intestate who survive him` `case 39 — a surviving brother beside an uncle`)
#ASSERT NOT null (`the uncles and aunts of the intestate who survive him` `case 39 — a surviving brother beside an uncle`)
#ASSERT NOT `rule 8 — there are no surviving spouse, descendants, parents, brothers and sisters or their children or grandparents but uncles and aunts of the intestate` `case 39 — a surviving brother beside an uncle`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 39 — a surviving brother beside an uncle` EQUALS LIST "Salim takes 1"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 39 — a surviving brother beside an uncle` EQUALS 1


§§§ `Case 40 — SEAM 3: an infant among the persons entitled, and GIA s 17`

-- THE FIRST FIXTURE IN THIS FILE THAT CARRIES ANOTHER ACT'S FACTS. Every case
-- above leaves `the infants concerned in the estate` empty, and a cross-module
-- audit found the same shape in all four modules at once: each zeroed out the
-- neighbours' fields, so every join in the corpus was typed and none could be
-- exercised, and no assertion anywhere could disagree with a neighbour.
--
-- THE FACTS. Roslan died intestate on 1 September 2024 leaving a widow, Suria,
-- and one legitimate child, Amirah, who is a minor. Rule 2 gives the widow one
-- half; rule 3 gives Amirah the other half of a $1,000,000 fund, so $500,000 of
-- capital. Bakar was appointed by the court, under GIA s 6(1), to be guardian of
-- Amirah's property jointly with her mother, and has purported to give a
-- discharge for the share.
--
-- WHAT THIS ACT DECIDES ON THOSE FACTS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It decides that
-- Amirah is entitled to one half. It does not decide whether the personal
-- representative gets a good discharge by paying Bakar — that is GIA s 17. The
-- assertions below on the infant's share go no further than identifying WHICH
-- share s 17 governs the payment of.
--
-- MEASURED ACROSS THE MODULES on this very record, which is possible only since
-- the rename: with `intestate-succession-act` and `guardianship-of-infants-act`
-- imported into one file, the GIA's `section 17 denies the guardian power to
-- give a good discharge` of `the ISA Bakar, guardian of Amirah's property` is
-- TRUE, and its `the discharge the guardian purported to give is not a good
-- discharge` is TRUE. So the $500,000 this Act awards is, on the same file, not
-- yet validly paid over. That is the join working, and neither module had to
-- learn the other's rules for it to work — they share one `Guardian` record and
-- one `the reference for this person`.
`the ISA Amirah` MEANS `living, childless` "Amirah"
`the ISA Suria` MEANS `living, childless` "Suria"
`the ISA Bakar` MEANS `living, childless` "Bakar"
`the ISA Roslan` MEANS
    `who died on` "Roslan" (YMD 2024 9 1) (LIST (`an ISA legitimate child` `the ISA Amirah`))

-- GIA s 6(1) IS THE ROUTE, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE THESE FACTS ALLOW. Roslan
-- died intestate, so he appointed nobody by will; s 6(1) provides that on the
-- death of the father the mother is guardian, and that "When no guardian has
-- been appointed by the father ... the court may if it thinks fit appoint a
-- guardian to act jointly with the mother." Bakar is that appointee, and he acts
-- jointly with Suria, so `alone or jointly` carries her reference rather than
-- `alone`.
`the ISA Bakar, guardian of Amirah's property` MEANS
    `an ISA guardian of the property of the infant` `the ISA Bakar` "Amirah"
        `appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`
        (`jointly with` (LIST "Suria"))

`the ISA Amirah, an infant concerned in the estate` MEANS
    `an ISA infant concerned in the estate` `the ISA Amirah`
        (JUST `the ISA Roslan`) (JUST `the ISA Suria`)
        (LIST `the ISA Bakar, guardian of Amirah's property`)
        500_000

`case 40 — an infant child takes half` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person, with infants concerned in the estate` `the ISA Roslan`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `the ISA Suria`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE
        (LIST `the ISA Amirah, an infant concerned in the estate`)

-- Case 40a: case 40 with the infants list empty and nothing else changed. It is
-- not a different state of the world so much as a different STATE OF THE FILE —
-- the same girl takes the same half, and what has gone is the record that she is
-- an infant concerned in the estate. That is the right pair for this decision,
-- because the decision reads the record and not the age: see SEAM 5.
`case 40a — the same estate, no infant recorded` MEANS
    `an ISA deceased person` `the ISA Roslan`
        (`an ISA family` (LIST (`a subsisting marriage to` `the ISA Suria`)) NOTHING NOTHING EMPTY EMPTY EMPTY)
        `a Singapore estate of one million after expenses`
        NOTHING TRUE FALSE

-- This Act's own answer first: the same two rungs as case 1 — rule 2 for the
-- spouse and rule 3 for the issue — the infancy changing nothing at all about
-- who takes or how much.
#ASSERT     `rule 2 — an intestate dies leaving a surviving spouse and issue` `case 40 — an infant child takes half`
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 40 — an infant child takes half` EQUALS LIST "Suria takes 0.5", "Amirah takes 0.5"
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 40 — an infant child takes half` EQUALS 1
-- SEAM 3, both sides. The filter is doing work: the widow takes an equal share
-- and is not in the list, and the list is not simply the distribution again.
#ASSERT `the shares taken by an infant concerned in the estate, read out` `case 40 — an infant child takes half` EQUALS LIST "Amirah takes 0.5"
#ASSERT count (`the entitlements under this Act taken by an infant concerned in the estate` `case 40 — an infant child takes half`) EQUALS 1
-- AND THE MINIMAL PAIR, which is the whole point of case 40a above: the same
-- intestate, the same widow, the same child, the same fund and the same
-- distribution, differing from case 40 in EXACTLY ONE FIELD — the case file
-- records no infant — and the list is empty. Without a pair that differs in one
-- field, the assertion above would also pass on an implementation that returned
-- every entitlement, or one that returned every entitlement of a child.
#ASSERT `the distribution, read out` `case 40a — the same estate, no infant recorded` EQUALS LIST "Suria takes 0.5", "Amirah takes 0.5"
#ASSERT null (`the entitlements under this Act taken by an infant concerned in the estate` `case 40a — the same estate, no infant recorded`)
-- And on an unrelated fixture, for good measure.
#ASSERT null (`the entitlements under this Act taken by an infant concerned in the estate` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue`)
-- The facts the Guardianship of Infants Act needs are on this record and are
-- pinned here, so that a later `family-cases.l4` putting the same file to that
-- module is putting the same file.
#ASSERT count (`the ISA Amirah, an infant concerned in the estate`'s `the guardians of the infant`) EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `the ISA Amirah, an infant concerned in the estate`'s `the capital of the property of the infant` EQUALS 500_000
#ASSERT `the ISA Bakar, guardian of Amirah's property`'s `has given a good discharge for a legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by the infant`
#ASSERT NOT `the ISA Bakar, guardian of Amirah's property`'s `the court has otherwise ordered as to giving a good discharge`
-- The share is $500,000 in money, which is what "capital moneys payable to or
-- receivable by an infant" measures. s 9 is the conversion this Act supplies.
#ASSERT `section 9 — the distributive share of a child or a descendant of a child, estimated` `the ISA Roslan` `the ISA Amirah` (1 DIVIDED BY 2) (`the property of which every share under section 7 is a fraction` `case 40 — an infant child takes half`) EQUALS JUST 500_000

§§§ `Case 41 — SEAM 4: the half of PAA s 29(5) that this Act can answer`

-- `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act` is
-- not a rule of this Act; it is the fact `probate-administration-act.l4` swears
-- to at its s 29(5) and cannot compute, offered here so that the two can be
-- compared. It is asserted from both sides on fixtures that already exist.
--
-- TRUE, ON A SOLE TAKER. Case 3 is rule 1: the spouse takes the whole.
#ASSERT `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act` `case 3 — a surviving spouse, no issue and no parent`
-- TRUE, AND THE READER SHOULD LOOK AT THIS ONE TWICE. Case 10 is rule 9: the
-- Government takes the whole. This decision is satisfied, and PAA s 29(5) is
-- NOT — the Government is not an administrator who could be dispensed from
-- giving sureties, and `the reference for the person entitled` is NOTHING for it
-- precisely because rule 9's taker is not a human being. The two questions
-- overlap; they are not the same question, and this is where they come apart.
#ASSERT `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act` `case 10 — nobody within rules 1 to 8`
#ASSERT `the reference for the person entitled` `the Government` EQUALS NOTHING
-- FALSE, ON MORE THAN ONE TAKER — the first conjunct isolated, the shares
-- summing to 1 throughout.
#ASSERT NOT `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue`
#ASSERT `the total of the shares distributed` `case 1 — a surviving spouse and issue` EQUALS 1
-- FALSE, WHERE THE ACT HAS NO EFFECT AT ALL: no taker, and nothing to dispense
-- anybody from.
#ASSERT NOT `one person takes the whole of the property distributable under this Act` `case 12 — the estate of a Muslim`
-- THE SECOND CONJUNCT CANNOT BE ISOLATED BY ANY FIXTURE IN THIS FILE, and
-- saying so is better than leaving a reader to look for one. Where this Act has
-- effect the shares sum to 1 by construction — that is what `the total of the
-- shares distributed` is asserted to be in every case above — and where it does
-- not the list is empty, so the count conjunct fails first. The sum test is
-- kept as a guard against the one shape that would otherwise slip through: a
-- single taker carrying a share of nought, which is exactly the defect case 4a
-- was built to catch one rung down.

§§ `What is deliberately NOT encoded, and why`

-- A reader who cannot tell a provision that is not in the Act from one that is
-- merely not encoded has been misled. Every section of the Intestate
-- Succession Act 1967 is named below, and every part of a section that this
-- module leaves out is named with it. Nothing was dropped silently.
--
-- s 1 (Short title) — "This Act is the Intestate Succession Act 1967." NOT
--   ENCODED because there is nothing to encode: no antecedent, no consequent.
--
-- s 2, SECOND LIMB — "or shall affect any rules of the Muslim law in respect
--   of the distribution of the estate of any such person". NOT ENCODED. It
--   saves a body of law that is not in this module and that s 2 does not
--   identify by any statute, forum or rule; there is nothing determinate to
--   compute. The first limb — the exclusion — IS encoded, and it is the limb
--   that decides whether this Act applies at all.
--
-- s 3 ("issue") — the SET of issue is not built as a list. The Act never asks
--   for the set; it asks whether an intestate "leaves issue" and, under rule 3,
--   which of them take in which stocks, and rule 3's own machinery answers
--   both. A second list would be a second answer to the same question. This is
--   an encoding choice, not an omission: nothing in the Act goes unanswered.
--
-- s 4(1)'s REFERRAL OUT — "shall be regulated by the law of the country in
--   which he was domiciled at the time of his death", where that country is
--   not Singapore. NOT ENCODED, and not encodable here: the operative rule is
--   then a foreign law, and this cleanroom models no named territory or state.
--   `section 4(1) — the law of the country of domicile is the law of
--   Singapore` decides whether the referral lands back on this Act and stops
--   there. It is a boundary, not a gap.
--
-- s 5's "as prescribed by the Probate and Administration Act 1934" — the
--   COMPUTATION of the expenses of due administration. NOT ENCODED; it is the
--   Probate and Administration Act's, and this module reads the resulting
--   figure off `Estate`'s `the funeral, testamentary and administration
--   expenses`. The DEDUCTION itself is encoded, because it is what makes the
--   whole definite.
--
-- s 5's DEDUCTION OF DEBTS — there is none, and the silence is the Act's. The
--   fund is the property "after payment thereout of the expenses of due
--   administration as prescribed by the Probate and Administration Act 1934",
--   and expenses are the only thing s 5 names. So this module deducts the
--   expenses and does NOT deduct `the debts and liabilities of the deceased`,
--   which the ontology carries @ref'd to the Probate and Administration Act
--   1934 s 57(1) and s 57(4) — the provisions that branch on solvency and
--   choose between the First and Second Schedules. Whether a solvent estate's
--   debts come out before the residue is distributed is that Act's question
--   and its Schedules answer it; this Act's arithmetic stops where its own
--   words stop. The consequence is real and is asserted rather than hidden: an
--   estate with $5,000,000 of debts and $1,200,000 of property still yields a
--   $1,000,000 fund under s 5 — see case 23.
--
-- s 5's "shall be distributed" and s 7's "the following rules shall be
--   observed" AS DUTIES — NOT ENCODED as regulative rules. Neither names a
--   duty-holder or a time. Who distributes, when, and on pain of what is the
--   Probate and Administration Act 1934's subject, so encoding either as
--   `PARTY ... MUST ... WITHIN n` would require inventing a deadline the Act
--   does not contain. What both sentences determine — who is entitled to what
--   fraction — is encoded in full.
--
-- s 6(b)'s "in the same degree" AS A STORED DEGREE — no field records a
--   degree, and none should: a brother is in the second degree by construction
--   and an uncle in the third, so the comparison s 6(b) needs is available from
--   position. A stored degree could contradict the position the same record
--   occupies. See `family-domain.l4`'s note on `Collateral relative`.
--
-- s 7 rule 3's PARENTHETICAL — "(both as to the undistributed portion and the
--   reversionary interest)". NOT SEPARATELY ENCODED. It does not create an
--   asset class beside the four value fields; it forecloses a rule that would
--   hold a reversionary interest back from the per stirpes division, and there
--   is no such rule here to foreclose.
--
-- s 10's "subject to the provisions contained in the will" — NOT ENCODED. It
--   defers to the construction of a particular document, which is not this
--   Act's business and has no determinate output. What IS encoded is s 10's
--   own operative direction: this Act has effect as respects the part not
--   beneficially disposed of.
--
-- s 10 proviso's "subject to his rights and powers for the purposes of
--   administration" — NOT ENCODED. Those rights and powers are the Probate and
--   Administration Act 1934's. The trusteeship the proviso creates, and the
--   exception it creates it subject to, ARE encoded.
--
-- THE MATERIAL AT THE FOOT OF THE SOURCE TEXT — the ARRANGEMENT OF SECTIONS,
--   the long title, the commencement note "[2 June 1967]", and the LEGISLATIVE
--   HISTORY. The last of these says so itself: "This Legislative History is a
--   service provided by the Law Revision Commission on a best-efforts basis.
--   It is not part of the Act." The commencement date is not merely quoted —
--   it is bound as `2 June 1967` and read by s 5's gate.
--
-- s 5's "possessed beneficially", AS A COMPLETE ACCOUNT OF WHAT IS THE
--   DECEASED'S OWN. What IS encoded is the subtraction of the ontology's
--   `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to
--   as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore` — the Probate and
--   Administration Act 1934's own contrasting words, s 62(2)(a) and s 64(1).
--   What is NOT encoded is any further inquiry into beneficial entitlement: a
--   resulting or constructive trust that no case file records as trustee
--   property will be treated as the deceased's own, because the ontology
--   records values and nothing else. AMBIGUITY A17 states the one place where
--   the recorded figure is known to be too coarse for s 5's words — limb (b)
--   reaches only immovable property and the trustee cell is not split by kind.
--
-- s 4's REACH IS DELIBERATELY GROSS OF THE TRUSTEE PROPERTY. s 4 says "the
--   movable property of a person deceased" and "the immovable property of a
--   person deceased" and never says "beneficially"; s 5 says it twice. So
--   `section 4 — the value of the property whose distribution is regulated by
--   this Act` follows s 4's unqualified words and s 5's fund does not. This is
--   a divergence between two sections of the same Act, not between the Act and
--   this module, and case 24a asserts both figures on one estate so that a
--   reader sees it rather than discovering it.
--
-- THE PAYABILITY OF A SHARE — to whom the money may be handed, when, and on
--   whose receipt. NOT ENCODED, and not this Act's. s 5 ends at "shall be
--   distributed among the persons entitled to succeed beneficially" and ss 1-10
--   say nothing further; the Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 17 says what
--   stands between an infant's entitlement and its payment, and the Probate and
--   Administration Act 1934 says who does the paying. This is SEAM 3 in `Joins
--   with the other three Acts`, and what IS done there is to identify WHICH of
--   the shares this Act computes are an infant's — a fact this module can read
--   off `the infants concerned in the estate` — so that the other module's rule
--   has something to run over. It decides nothing s 17 decides.
--
-- THREE DECISIONS IN THIS FILE ANSWER NO PROVISION OF THIS ACT, and are named
--   here so that the section-by-section walk above is not read as exhaustive of
--   the file. `the value beneficially disposed of by the will recorded in the
--   estate agrees with the dispositions made by the will` is a consistency check
--   on a case file. `the entitlements under this Act taken by an infant
--   concerned in the estate` and `one person takes the whole of the property
--   distributable under this Act` are joins, defined and argued in `Joins with
--   the other three Acts`. All three are labelled at their site as not being
--   rules; a reader auditing coverage against the statute should skip them, and
--   a reader auditing the corpus should not.
--
-- NOTHING IS @export'ED. This module is inert, per the cleanroom's house
-- style; a downstream module that wants an API surface can import it and
-- decorate what it needs.


§§ `Ambiguity register`

-- Twenty-one places where two readings of the text both survive, tabulated so
-- that the choices are auditable in one place. Each is argued at the site
-- where it bites; this table and those comments must agree.
--
-- COUNT THE ROWS BEFORE YOU TRUST THE SENTENCE ABOVE. This header said
-- "Sixteen" over nineteen rows for one whole revision, because A17, A18 and
-- A19 were appended to the table without its own prose being re-read — and in
-- the same revision A4's and A6's rows were left saying what the site comments
-- had stopped saying. A register that is stale is worse than none, for exactly
-- the reason the closing paragraph gives about a register that is short: it
-- reads as authoritative. `grep -c '^--  A[0-9]'` over this file is the check,
-- and re-reading each row against its site comment is the rest of it.
--
--  A1  s 2      "any Muslim" — a fact about the deceased, arriving already
--               construed, against a question this Act itself decides.
--               TAKEN: already construed; s 2 supplies no test.
--  A2  s 3      the adoption limb's three named jurisdictions — exhaustive
--               against illustrative. TAKEN: exhaustive.
--  A3  s 3,     "issue" (rules 1, 2, 3) against "descendants" (rules 5-8) —
--      s 7      coextensive against the second being wider. TAKEN:
--               coextensive, stated as surviving descendants who take or are
--               represented under rule 3.
--  A4  s 4(1),  the situs of the property s 4 claims — ONE FORK APPEARING
--      s 4(2)   TWICE. s 4(2) is silent as to where the immovables are;
--               s 4(1) refers the movables of a Singapore domiciliary to
--               Singapore law wherever they are situated. Unlimited reach
--               narrowed by s 5, against an implied confinement to Singapore.
--               TAKEN: both encoded and kept apart, for the movables and the
--               immovables alike; case 20 carries a non-zero figure in each
--               of the two outside-Singapore cells and shows both legs of the
--               gap ($1,850,000 under s 4 against $1,200,000 under s 5), and
--               case 20a shows s 4(1) referring the movables out.
--  A5  s 5      an undated death — the commencement gate fails, against a
--               presumption that it is satisfied. TAKEN: the gate fails.
--  A6  s 5      "possessed beneficially of property" — a positive NET value
--               in the relevant cell, gross less the trustee figure, against
--               a further already-construed fact of possession. TAKEN: a
--               positive NET value. The word "net" is load-bearing: the raw
--               cell is gross of the property held as trustee by
--               `family-domain.l4`'s own construction, so a positive raw cell
--               is not evidence of BENEFICIAL possession at all (case 24).
--  A7  s 5      expenses exceeding the property — a nil fund, against a
--               negative one. TAKEN: nil. The gate still opens and the
--               fractions still sum to 1; it is the fund they are fractions
--               of that is nothing (case 23a).
--  A8  s 7      a claimant dead on an unknown date — not shown to survive,
--               against the distribution being incomputable. TAKEN: not
--               shown to survive; his stock is still represented.
--  A9  s 6(a)   a claimant whose date of birth is not in evidence — taken as
--               born in the lifetime, against excluded for want of proof.
--               TAKEN: born in the lifetime.
--  A10 s 7      "a parent or parents" — a parent who survives, against the
--      rules    bare existence of a parent. TAKEN: survives. (And a parent
--      1, 4, 5  not in evidence cannot be shown to survive.)
--  A11 s 6(b)   "rank immediately after" — the half blood take nothing while
--               the whole blood are entitled, against a concurrent reduced
--               share. TAKEN: take nothing, and take in full where none of
--               the whole blood is entitled.
--  A12 s 6(b),  the degree at which the ranking bites under rule 6 — the
--      rule 6   class of sibling stocks, against the individual takers.
--               TAKEN: the class of stocks.
--  A13 rule 3   a child who predeceased leaving no surviving descendant — not
--               a stock at all, against a stock whose portion fails. TAKEN:
--               not a stock; otherwise part of the estate belongs to nobody.
--  A14 rule 3,  whether s 3's "child" is imported at every generation of the
--      Provisos descent — yes, against descent as a question of fact. TAKEN:
--               yes; s 3 governs "this Act", and s 3's "issue" is built out
--               of the defined word.
--  A15 rule 6   the grandchild of a predeceased brother — takes nothing,
--               rule 6 naming only "children", against rule 3's per stirpes
--               machinery being imported wholesale. TAKEN: takes nothing;
--               otherwise Proviso No. (2) would be redundant.
--  A16 s 5,     where the expenses of due administration fall in a partial
--      s 10     intestacy — wholly on the undisposed part, against rateably.
--               TAKEN: wholly on the undisposed part, following the Probate
--               and Administration Act's own order of application.
--  A17 s 5(b)   the trustee figure is recorded by situs and not by kind, so
--               limb (b) — which reaches only immovable property — cannot net
--               it exactly. Subtract the whole Singapore trustee figure from
--               the Singapore immovable value, against subtracting nothing
--               under (b). TAKEN: subtract the whole figure. Under-inclusion
--               only fails to bring an estate within the section; the other
--               reading distributes somebody else's property.
--  A18 s 6(a)   whether the second limb settles MEMBERSHIP at all, being
--               drafted as a rule against distinctions — it says nothing about
--               a person in neither class, against the two classes being
--               exhaustive of those from whom takers are drawn. TAKEN: the
--               latter, applied to EVERY class of taker. On the former this Act
--               has no answer for a child conceived after the death.
--  A19 s 5,     the same-day boundary: the Act's tests are moments and the
--      s 6(a),  ontology records dates. Resolve the unknown moment against the
--      s 7      person asserting it, against resolving it in favour of
--               inclusion. TAKEN: against for survivorship (matching A8), in
--               favour for birth (matching A9), so the two comparisons are
--               strict and inclusive respectively and on purpose.
--  A20 s 10,    the SITUS of "the part of his property not so disposed of".
--      s 5      s 10 is silent as to where the disposed part is; s 5's fund is
--               Singapore property only. Subtract the whole value beneficially
--               disposed of from the Singapore fund, against subtracting only
--               so much of it as falls on Singapore property. TAKEN: the whole
--               value — for want of an alternative, `Disposition` carrying no
--               situs, which is the same kind of limit as A17. It can only
--               under-state the fund, never over-state it (matching A5, A7 and
--               A17). Case 15c exhibits it: $700,000 of foreign land makes no
--               difference to the fund at all.
--  A21 rule 6,  what triggers representation under rule 6 — DEATH, or merely
--      s 6(a)   not taking. Rule 6 says "deceased" three times in one sentence
--               and rule 3 says "then dead", but since A18 a sibling can fail
--               to take for a second reason: s 6(a) excludes him. Is he then
--               "deceased" for this rule? Representation runs only from a dead
--               sibling, against s 6(a) exclusion being a deemed predecease.
--               TAKEN: only from a dead sibling, on the words — and it
--               restores the parity with rule 3's stock test, which already
--               reads it that way. Case 17e is the fork ($1,000,000 to the
--               Government against $1,000,000 to the excluded brother's
--               daughter); case 17g is a real deceased brother, whose child
--               does take.
--
-- WHERE A17 TO A21 CAME FROM. All five were being decided in the code and
-- recorded nowhere, and adversarial review found them there: A17 by measuring
-- an estate held wholly on trust being distributed to the intestate's family,
-- A18 by measuring a posthumously-conceived brother taking the whole estate on
-- facts that gave a posthumously-conceived child nothing, A19 by reading the
-- two comparison operators against each other, A20 by noticing that the s 10
-- subtrahend has no situs while the fund it comes off has one, and A21 by
-- building the fixture A18's repair still lacked — an excluded sibling WITH
-- children — and finding that rule 6 was letting them represent a living man.
-- A register with sixteen smaller entries and not these five was worse than no
-- register, because it read as complete.
--
-- A18 AND A21 ARE THE SAME MISTAKE ONE GENERATION APART, which is worth saying
-- because it is a pattern and not a coincidence. Both were found by asking
-- whether rules 3 and 6 answer the same question the same way; both times they
-- did not; both times the repair was to make rule 6 agree with rule 3, which
-- is the rule whose words are the more explicit. If a third such asymmetry is
-- looked for, that is where to look.
